ry: Shaw and McKay — poverty, residential mobility, ethnic heterogeneity → weak social control → crime; collective efficacy: Sampson, Raudenbush, Earls — willingness to intervene for common good; school effects and neighborhood school quality; residential segregation: dissimilarity index, exposure index, isolation index; causes of segregation: discrimination, steering, exclusionary zoning, wealth disparities); urban poverty and inequality (concentrated poverty: 40% poverty threshold, hypersegregation; deindustrialization and urban decline: Detroit, Baltimore — manufacturing job loss, white flight, fiscal crisis; informal economy in cities; food deserts and food access disparities; environmental justice: NIMBY, disproportionate siting of hazardous facilities in minority and low-income neighborhoods)",
