            task: "Study environmental economics for my exam — review foundational concepts and policy instruments: market failure and externalities (externality definition: cost or benefit not reflected in market prices; negative externality — private MC < social MC, socially inefficient overproduction; positive externality — private MB < social MB, underproduction; Coase theorem: under zero transaction costs and well-defined property rights, private bargaining achieves efficient outcome regardless of initial entitlement; Coase theorem limitations: transaction costs, asymmetric information, many parties, non-excludability; public goods: non-rival and non-excludable; free-rider problem; tragedy of the commons: Hardin's model, common pool resource overextraction, solutions: property rights, regulation, Ostrom's common-pool governance principles); pollution control instruments (Pigouvian tax: tax equal to marginal external damage at socially optimal output — internalize externality; emission fee vs effluent charge; Pigouvian subsidy for positive externalities; command-and-control: emission standards, technology standards, performance standards — cost-ineffective without equimarginal principle; cap-and-trade: total cap set by regulator, permits distributed or auctioned, firms trade to equimarginal abatement cost; Weitzman prices vs quantities under uncertainty: price instrument preferred when MB curve is steep, quantity preferred when MC curve is steep; emissions trading systems: EU ETS structure and sectors, RGGI, California AB 32 cap-and-trade, price floors and ceilings, allowance banking); valuation of environmental goods (non-market valuation methods: contingent valuation (CV) — hypothetical market survey, willingness to pay vs willingness to accept, embedding effect, scope sensitivity tests; hedonic pricing: property value method — implicit price of environmental attribute, wage differential for environmental risk; travel cost method: revealed preference, consumer surplus for recreation site; benefit transfer; ecosystem services: provisioning, regulating, cultural, supporting services; TEEB and Millennium Ecosystem Assessment frameworks; ecosystem services valuation challenges: non-linearity, threshold effects, irreversibility, existence value); benefit-cost analysis (BCA steps: identify project alternatives, catalog costs and benefits, quantify and monetize, apply discount rate, compute NPV and BCR; social discount rate: market rate vs Ramsey formula r = ρ + η·g; intergenerational discounting controversy; distributional weights in BCA; cost-effectiveness analysis when benefits not monetizable; environmental impact assessment vs BCA)",
