            icon: "megaphone.fill",
            task: "Study science communication for my exam — review models, strategies, and frameworks: the deficit model and its critiques (deficit model: public misunderstands or mistrusts science because they lack information; if you provide facts, attitudes and behavior will follow; critiques: Wynne (1992) — public resistance is not ignorance but justified skepticism of institutions; knowledge alone insufficient — values, trust, and context shape reception; Irwin and Wynne (1996) — lay expertise and local knowledge are undervalued; alternative models: contextual model (tailoring information to audience background and prior knowledge), lay expertise model (acknowledging public knowledge), dialogue model (two-way communication, co-production), participatory model (public involvement in research decisions)); risk communication frameworks (Sandman's risk = hazard + outrage: technical risk is hazard; public perception is driven by outrage factors — dread, unfamiliarity, lack of control, inequity, distrust; implication: high-hazard/low-outrage problems are under-attended; low-hazard/high-outrage problems are over-attended; mental noise model: when people are upset, they can process only 3–4 key messages; good bad ugly framework: lead with the bad news, then what you're doing about it, then the good news; Covello's trust determination model: caring and empathy, competence and expertise, honesty and openness, dedication and commitment — trust is built cumulatively and lost quickly; National Academies (2017) report on science communication: move from deficit to engagement; audience research first; iterative message testing); framing science (emphasis framing: same facts, different salience; equivalence framing: logically equivalent descriptions produce different choices; gain vs loss framing: loss frames more persuasive for disease prevention; cultural cognition theory: Kahan — worldviews (egalitarian-communitarian vs hierarchical-individualist) predict risk perception more than knowledge; identity-protective cognition: people process information to confirm existing beliefs; trusted messengers: messenger credibility interacts with audience identity; narrative persuasion: narrative transportation reduces counter-arguing; science storytelling: story arc — character, conflict, resolution; use concrete examples not abstractions); science journalism and media (journalistic norms: balance norm — false equivalence in climate coverage; novelty bias; hook and nut graf structure; inverted pyramid); public engagement and participatory communication (science cafés, consensus conferences, citizen science; deliberative mini-publics; Foresight model; upstream engagement — involving public before decisions are locked in)",
            successCriteria: "Science communication study session completed (deficit model critiques named with at least one alternative model described, Sandman's risk = hazard + outrage formula stated, one framing effect explained with evidence, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
