        // healthbehavior
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "heart.text.clipboard.fill",
            task: "Study health behavior theory for my exam — review the major frameworks, constructs, and applications: Health Belief Model (HBM) (Rosenstock 1966; constructs: perceived susceptibility (belief you are at risk), perceived severity (belief the disease is serious), perceived benefits (belief the action reduces risk), perceived barriers (costs of taking action), cues to action (triggers — symptoms, media, advice), self-efficacy (added by Bandura 1977 — belief in ability to perform the action); HBM predicts behavior when all constructs are favorable; critique: static model, ignores social context, self-efficacy was retrofitted; evidence: explains cancer screening uptake, flu vaccination, HIV testing); Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB) (Ajzen 1991; constructs: attitude toward the behavior (evaluative judgment), subjective norms (perceived social pressure), perceived behavioral control (belief in ability — similar to self-efficacy); these three → behavioral intention → behavior; TPB extension — Theory of Reasoned Action (TRA): no PBC construct, assumes full volitional control; critique: intention-behavior gap, ignores habit and emotion; evidence: predicts contraceptive use, exercise, dietary change); Social Cognitive Theory (SCT) (Bandura; reciprocal determinism: person, behavior, and environment interact bidirectionally; key constructs: self-efficacy (most powerful predictor), outcome expectations (anticipated consequences), observational learning (modeling), self-regulation (goal-setting, self-monitoring, self-reward); applications: skills training, peer modeling, SCRIP programs; evidence: smoking cessation, HIV prevention, exercise adoption); Transtheoretical Model / Stages of Change (TTM) (Prochaska and DiClemente; stages: Precontemplation (not intending to change in the next 6 months), Contemplation (considering change in next 6 months), Preparation (planning change in next month), Action (changed <6 months ago), Maintenance (changed >6 months ago); processes of change: consciousness raising, dramatic relief, self-reevaluation, social liberation, self-liberation, counter-conditioning, helping relationships, reinforcement management, stimulus control; decisional balance: pros vs cons of behavior change; critique: assumes linear progression, stages may not be discrete, few RCTs show stage-matched interventions outperform generic; evidence: smoking cessation, exercise, mammography); Motivational Interviewing (MI) (Miller and Rollnick; spirit: partnership, acceptance, compassion, evocation; core skills: OARS — Open questions, Affirming, Reflecting, Summarizing; change talk (DARN-CAT: Desire, Ability, Reason, Need, Commitment, Activation, Taking steps); sustain talk vs change talk; rolling with resistance; discrepancy development; evidence-based for alcohol reduction, diet change, medication adherence)",
            successCriteria: "Health behavior theory study session completed (HBM six constructs named, TPB three antecedents to intention listed, TTM five stages named in order, one SCT construct defined, and exam notes saved)",
