        // disasterrisk
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "exclamationmark.triangle.fill",
            task: "Study disaster risk reduction for my exam — review the Sendai Framework, risk governance, vulnerability analysis, and DRR practice: disaster risk concepts and frameworks (UNDRR (United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction): global authority for DRR; key definitions — hazard: a process/phenomenon that may cause loss of life or health impacts; exposure: people/assets in hazard zones; vulnerability: conditions that increase susceptibility to harm; capacity: resources to cope, adapt, and recover; disaster risk = hazard × exposure × vulnerability / capacity; Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030: successor to Hyogo Framework for Action (HFA) 2005–2015; four priorities: (1) understanding disaster risk, (2) strengthening risk governance, (3) investing in DRR for resilience, (4) enhancing disaster preparedness for effective response; seven global targets: reduce mortality, reduce affected people, reduce economic losses, reduce infrastructure damage, increase national DRR strategies, enhance international cooperation, increase early warning systems; target G: national and local DRR strategies — 153+ countries with national strategies by 2023; disaster risk reduction vs disaster risk management: DRR is the goal; DRM is the practice — includes prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response, recovery; DRR vs adaptation: DRR is hazard-centric (earthquakes, floods, cyclones); climate change adaptation (CCA) addresses climate-driven risks; integration is the frontier); vulnerability and capacity analysis (Pressure and Release (PAR) model: Wisner et al. — disaster = hazard × vulnerability; unsafe conditions ← dynamic pressures ← root causes; root causes: lack of access to power, resources, knowledge; dynamic pressures: rapid urbanization, lack of training, endemic disease; unsafe conditions: fragile livelihoods, fragile local institutions, dangerous locations; access to power, structures, and resources; Disaster Crunch Model: pressure accumulates until vulnerability breaks under hazard; Birkmann's vulnerability dimensions: social, economic, physical, environmental; capacity assessment: resources, skills, institutional strength, social capital — "resilience"; community-based DRR (CBDRR): local risk mapping, early warning at community level, participatory vulnerability assessment; gender and DRR: women often more vulnerable due to mobility, property rights, information access — also key agents for resilience); risk governance and policy (multi-hazard risk assessment: single vs multi-hazard approach; quantitative vs qualitative risk assessment; probabilistic hazard modeling; exposure and asset mapping; risk index: ProVention, INFORM Risk Index, UNDP vulnerability index; early warning systems (EWS): risk knowledge, monitoring, warning dissemination, response capacity; end-to-end EWS; Community-based EWS; DRR financing: national government budgets, World Bank GFDRR (Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery), Climate Risk Financing, parametric insurance, disaster risk bonds; build back better (BBB): post-disaster reconstruction that reduces future risk; greening recovery; Hyogo Framework legacy: national platforms for DRR, regional mechanisms)",
            successCriteria: "Disaster risk study session completed (four Sendai Framework priorities named, disaster risk formula (hazard × exposure × vulnerability / capacity) stated, PAR model root causes and unsafe conditions described, one early warning system component explained, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
