        // humanrights
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.badge.shield.checkmark.fill",
            task: "Study human rights for my exam — review the international human rights framework, treaty bodies, and humanitarian law: international human rights law foundations (Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) 1948: 30 articles; non-binding but foundational; civil and political rights (Art. 3–21): life, liberty, security; freedom from torture, slavery; fair trial; privacy; free speech; political participation; economic, social, and cultural rights (Art. 22–27): work, social security, education, health, culture; international bill of rights: UDHR + ICCPR + ICESCR + Optional Protocols; International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR) 1966: binding treaty; 173 states parties; Human Rights Committee (treaty body) monitors implementation; Article 6 (right to life), Article 7 (freedom from torture), Article 18 (freedom of thought, conscience, religion), Article 19 (freedom of expression); derogations: Article 4 — states may derogate in public emergency that threatens life of nation; non-derogable rights: right to life, freedom from torture, slavery ban; International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (ICESCR) 1966: binding; CESCR monitors; progressive realization: Article 2 — states must take steps to achieve rights to maximum available resources; right to health (Article 12): AAAQ framework (Availability, Accessibility, Acceptability, Quality); indivisibility of human rights: civil-political and ESC rights are interdependent; core obligations vs progressive realization; treaty bodies: nine core UN human rights treaties; treaty body review process: state reports → constructive dialogue → concluding observations; special procedures: independent experts/working groups appointed by HRC; country mandates and thematic mandates; universal periodic review (UPR): all 193 UN member states reviewed every 4.5 years; peer-to-peer review mechanism); humanitarian law and protection (International Humanitarian Law (IHL) / Law of Armed Conflict: regulates conduct of hostilities; Geneva Conventions 1949: four conventions — wounded/sick soldiers (GC I), sea warfare (GC II), prisoners of war (GC III), civilians (GC IV); Common Article 3: minimum standards in non-international armed conflict; Additional Protocols I and II (1977); distinction principle: distinguish combatants from civilians; proportionality: civilian harm must not be excessive relative to military advantage; precaution: take feasible steps to minimize civilian harm; prohibition on torture, outrages upon personal dignity; ICRC (International Committee of the Red Cross): guardian of IHL; access to detainees; neutral intermediary; refugee law: 1951 Refugee Convention and 1967 Protocol; definition of refugee: well-founded fear of persecution on grounds of race, religion, nationality, political opinion, or membership in a particular social group; non-refoulement: Article 33 — cannot return refugee to territory where life/freedom is threatened; UNHCR mandate: international protection and durable solutions; IDPs: internally displaced persons — not covered by Refugee Convention; Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement; responsibility to protect (R2P): 2005 World Summit — three pillars: (1) state responsibility, (2) international assistance, (3) timely and decisive response; R2P triggers: genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing, crimes against humanity; ICC (International Criminal Court): Rome Statute 1998; four core crimes; complementarity principle; universal jurisdiction; Nuremberg legacy)",
            successCriteria: "Human rights study session completed (UDHR, ICCPR, and ICESCR distinguished with one article from each named, non-refoulement principle defined, IHL distinction and proportionality principles stated, R2P three pillars described, and exam notes saved)",
