        ),
        // exercisephysiology
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "heart.circle.fill",
            task: "Study exercise physiology for my exam — review the core concepts of exercise metabolism and physiological responses: energy systems (ATP-PCr/phosphocreatine system: immediate energy 0–10 s, creatine kinase: ADP + PCr → ATP + Cr, limited by PCr stores; glycolytic system: fast glycolysis 10 s–2 min, glucose → pyruvate → lactate when O2 insufficient, limited by lactate/H+ accumulation; oxidative system: >2 min, aerobic oxidation of carbohydrates and fats, limited by O2 delivery and mitochondrial capacity); VO2max and maximal aerobic capacity (VO2max definition: maximum rate of O2 consumption during maximal exercise; Fick equation: VO2 = Q × (CaO2 − CvO2), where Q = cardiac output, CaO2 = arterial O2 content, CvO2 = venous O2 content; central vs peripheral factors: central = cardiac output (stroke volume × HR); peripheral = O2 extraction (a-v O2 difference); training adaptations: increased SV (eccentric hypertrophy, increased plasma volume), increased mitochondrial density, increased capillary density; VO2max testing: graded exercise test (GXT) using metabolic cart, Bruce protocol or ramp protocol, RER ≥ 1.10 confirms maximal effort); lactate threshold and EPOC (lactate threshold (LT): exercise intensity at which blood lactate rises above baseline (~2 mmol/L); LT2/OBLA: onset of blood lactate accumulation (~4 mmol/L); significance: LT2 is a strong predictor of endurance performance; training shifts LT to higher intensities; excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC): elevated O2 consumption after exercise ends; fast component: resynthesis of PCr, myoglobin O2, clearing lactate; slow component: elevated body temperature, catecholamines, substrate cycling); cardiorespiratory responses to exercise (acute: HR increases linearly with VO2; SV increases up to ~40–60% VO2max then plateaus; Q = SV × HR; ventilation increases to match metabolic demand; VT (ventilatory threshold) aligns with LT; thermoregulation: heat production during exercise; sweat evaporation primary cooling mechanism; core temperature, skin blood flow, sweating rate; heat illness continuum: cramps → exhaustion → stroke)",
            successCriteria: "Exercise physiology study session completed (Fick equation written from memory with all variables defined, three energy systems compared by timeframe and rate-limiting factor, VO2max testing criteria stated, LT vs OBLA distinguished, EPOC fast and slow components explained, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "heart.circle.fill",
            task: "Complete my exercise physiology lab report or assignment — this may include: a VO2max graded exercise test report (describe subject demographics and fitness level; describe protocol used (Bruce, modified Bruce, or ramp); record HR at each stage, RPE, RER, and VO2 if available; identify the test endpoint and criteria met (volitional exhaustion, plateau in VO2, RER ≥ 1.10, HRmax ≥ age-predicted); calculate estimated VO2max if direct measurement unavailable using Fox equation or protocol-specific nomogram; calculate Fick equation components if O2 pulse data available; compare to normative values (ACSM age/sex-specific VO2max norms); discuss central vs peripheral limiters for this subject based on their fitness profile; identify one training recommendation based on the VO2max result), a lactate threshold or metabolic analysis (describe the graded exercise test protocol and blood lactate sampling protocol (capillary earlobe or fingertip); construct a blood lactate vs workload curve; identify LT1 (visual deflection from baseline) and LT2/OBLA (4 mmol/L or Dmax method); determine training zones based on LT1 and LT2; compare to ventilatory threshold data if available; discuss the physiological basis for the LT — match to LT1 = increased fast-twitch recruitment, decreased lactate clearance; identify sport-specific relevance of LT vs LT2 for this subject's event distance and training phase), or an energy systems analysis for a specific sport (identify the primary sport and competitive event duration; determine the dominant energy system for that duration using energy system contribution charts; calculate approximate PCr, glycolytic, and oxidative ATP contribution percentages; identify rate-limiting factors for performance; recommend training modalities to stress each energy system (PCr: short sprints with full rest; glycolytic: 30–90 s intervals; oxidative: long aerobic work); discuss how nutrition strategies can optimize each energy system on game day)",
            successCriteria: "Exercise physiology lab report or assignment completed (VO2max value calculated or test results analyzed, LT or energy system data interpreted with specific numerical values cited, physiological basis of findings explained, one training or nutritional recommendation stated, and report saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 75 * 60
        ),
        // nutritionscience
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "fork.knife.circle.fill",
            task: "Study nutrition science for my exam — review the core concepts of macronutrient and micronutrient metabolism and dietary assessment: macronutrients (carbohydrates: 4 kcal/g; types: simple (mono/disaccharides), complex (starch, fiber); digestion: amylase → maltose → glucose; blood glucose regulation: insulin (uptake), glucagon (release); glycemic index (GI) and glycemic load (GL); fiber: soluble (lowers LDL, slows glucose absorption — pectin, beta-glucan) vs insoluble (bulk/laxative — cellulose); proteins: 4 kcal/g; 20 amino acids, 9 essential (histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, valine); protein quality: PDCAAS (protein digestibility-corrected amino acid score), DIAAS; nitrogen balance: NB = N in − N out; positive in growth, negative in deficiency; functions: structural, enzymatic, transport, immune; fats: 9 kcal/g; classification: saturated (no double bonds, solid at room temp), monounsaturated (one double bond), polyunsaturated (multiple), trans (industrial hydrogenation); essential fatty acids: linoleic acid (omega-6, LA) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3, ALA); eicosanoids from EFAs; fat digestion: bile emulsification → lipase → monoglycerides + fatty acids → chylomicrons → lymphatics); micronutrients (fat-soluble vitamins: A, D, E, K — stored in liver/adipose; toxicity possible from excess; water-soluble: B vitamins (B1 thiamine, B2 riboflavin, B3 niacin, B5 pantothenic acid, B6 pyridoxine, B7 biotin, B9 folate, B12 cobalamin) + Vitamin C — excreted in urine, daily intake needed; key minerals: calcium (bone health, muscle contraction, 1000 mg/d RDA adults), iron (hemoglobin, myoglobin, RDA women 18 mg/d, men 8 mg/d), zinc (immune, enzyme cofactor), iodine (thyroid hormones), magnesium (300+ enzyme reactions)); dietary reference intakes (DRI framework: EAR (Estimated Average Requirement): meets needs of 50% of population; RDA (Recommended Dietary Allowance) = EAR + 2SD, meets needs of 97.5%; AI (Adequate Intake): when EAR unknown, based on observed intake of healthy population; UL (Tolerable Upper Intake Level): highest daily intake unlikely to cause adverse effects; AMDR: acceptable macronutrient distribution range: carbs 45–65%, fat 20–35%, protein 10–35%); dietary assessment methods (24-hour dietary recall: interview-based, easy, but single day may not be representative; food frequency questionnaire (FFQ): estimates usual intake, good for ranking, not absolute; diet history: comprehensive past patterns; food record/diary: prospective, accurate, but underreporting common; biomarkers: serum ferritin for iron, 25-hydroxyvitamin D for Vit D — objective but invasive)",
            successCriteria: "Nutrition science study session completed (9 essential amino acids named, three DRI categories (EAR/RDA/UL) defined with distinction, AMDR ranges for all three macronutrients stated, one water-soluble and one fat-soluble vitamin deficiency disease identified, one dietary assessment method described with one limitation, and exam notes saved)",
