        // reproductivehealth
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.2.circle.fill",
            task: "Study reproductive health for my exam — review the core concepts of contraception, STIs, reproductive physiology, and family planning: contraception methods (hormonal: combined oral contraceptive pills (COC) — estrogen + progestin; mechanism: suppress ovulation (GnRH suppression → no LH surge), thicken cervical mucus, thin endometrium; effectiveness: 91% typical, 99.7% perfect; risks: VTE, contraindicated in migraine with aura, smokers >35 yo; progestin-only pills (POP/mini-pill): no estrogen, safer for breastfeeding; mechanism primarily cervical mucus; emergency contraception: levonorgestrel (Plan B) up to 72 h, ulipristal acetate up to 120 h, copper IUD up to 5 days; hormonal IUDs (Mirena, Kyleena): levonorgestrel, 3–8 years, >99% effective, reduces menstrual bleeding; copper IUD: non-hormonal, 10+ years, >99.9% effective, can be used as EC; implant (Nexplanon): subdermal etonogestrel, 3 years, >99.9% effective; depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA/Depo-Provera): IM/SC injection, 3-month intervals, 96% typical; barrier: male condom 85% typical, only method with dual STI/pregnancy protection; female condom 79%; diaphragm with spermicide 88%; permanent: tubal ligation, vasectomy; fertility awareness methods (FAM): calendar, basal body temperature, cervical mucus (Billings), symptothermal method); sexually transmitted infections (bacterial: chlamydia (Chlamydia trachomatis) — asymptomatic in 70% of women, PID risk, treat with azithromycin/doxycycline; gonorrhea (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) — cervicitis/urethritis, increasing resistance, treat with ceftriaxone; syphilis: primary (chancre), secondary (rash including palms/soles), latent, tertiary — treat with benzathine penicillin G; viral: HSV-1/HSV-2 — latent in dorsal root ganglia, recurrent outbreaks, suppressive acyclovir; HIV — CD4+ T cell depletion, ART combinations, U=U (undetectable = untransmittable); HPV — most common STI, >150 types, high-risk (16, 18) cause 70% cervical cancer, vaccine; hepatitis B — sexual transmission, vertical transmission; parasitic: Trichomonas vaginalis — most curable STI; screening: USPSTF chlamydia/gonorrhea annual screening for all sexually active women <25 yo; HIV screening 15–65 yo); reproductive physiology (female cycle: menstrual phase (d 1–5), follicular phase (d 1–13): FSH → follicle development, estrogen rises; ovulation (d 14): LH surge → follicle rupture → egg release; luteal phase (d 15–28): corpus luteum → progesterone; negative feedback; male reproductive physiology: spermatogenesis in seminiferous tubules, 74 days; testosterone from Leydig cells; FSH → Sertoli cells → spermatogenesis; fertility: female fertility declines sharply after 35; male fertility declines more gradually; infertility: defined as inability to conceive after 12 months of unprotected intercourse (<35 yo) or 6 months (>35 yo); causes: ovulatory disorders (PCOS) 40%, tubal factor 30%, male factor 30%–40%)",
            successCriteria: "Reproductive health study session completed (three hormonal contraceptive methods compared by mechanism and effectiveness, four STIs named with causative organism and first-line treatment, female cycle phases and key hormones listed, infertility definition stated with two common causes, and exam notes saved)",
