        // medicalanthropology
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.crop.circle.badge.questionmark.fill",
            task: "Study medical anthropology for my exam — review the core concepts of illness, culture, and healing: foundational frameworks (illness vs disease distinction: disease = biomedical abnormality (pathophysiology); illness = patient's lived experience and meaning of sickness; Kleinman's explanatory model (EM): eight questions — what do you call the problem? What caused it? Why did it start when it did? What does it do to you? How severe is it? What kind of treatment should you receive? What are the chief problems the illness has caused? What do you fear most?; the biopsychosocial model (Engel 1977): challenges biomedical reductionism; integrates biological, psychological, social factors; clinical interview as complement to biomedical diagnosis; the biomedical model: body as machine, disease as malfunction, treatment as repair; critique — ignores social context, physician authority, patient passivity; sick role (Parsons): exemption from normal duties, expected to want to get well, must seek competent help; critiques: ignores agency, power, chronic illness); medical systems and ethnomedical approaches (ethnomedical systems: humoral medicine (hot/cold balance — Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine, Latino curanderismo); traditional healers: curandero, shaman, herbalist, biomedical physician — comparing role and legitimacy; medical pluralism: people simultaneously use multiple medical systems; culture-bound syndromes: ataque de nervios, susto (fright disease), taijin kyofusho, hwa-byung; biomedicalization: Conrad's medicalization theory (normal behaviors defined as medical problems: ADHD, menopause, obesity); biomedicalization (Clarke et al.): technoscientific expansion, surveillance, risk); critical medical anthropology (Paul Farmer: structural violence — social structures (poverty, racism, sexism) cause differential morbidity/mortality; Partners in Health model; medical pluralism under structural constraint; embodiment (Csordas, Merleau-Ponty): body as subject of culture, not just object; chronic illness experience: biographical disruption (Bury), loss of self, narrative reconstruction; disability studies: medical model vs social model of disability; health disparities as social/structural outcomes); illness narratives and clinical ethnography (Arthur Kleinman's illness narratives: chaos narrative, restitution narrative, quest narrative; narrative medicine (Rita Charon): close reading of illness stories; clinical ethnography: participant observation in healthcare settings; patient-centered care and the explanatory model elicitation)",
