hoices about whether to medicalize?; propose one policy or community-based alternative to medicalization that addresses the structural root cause), or a comparative ethnomedical systems analysis (select two medical systems to compare: Ayurvedic medicine, traditional Chinese medicine (TCM), curanderismo, biomedicine, or Hmong healing practices; describe the cosmological framework of each system (hot/cold balance, qi and meridians, spiritual causation, germ theory); describe the role of the healer in each system (training, authority, patient relationship); describe diagnosis and treatment approaches; identify one area of medical pluralism: a population that uses both systems simultaneously and for what conditions; discuss the concept of health-seeking behavior under medical pluralism — what determines which system a patient uses first?; apply the explanatory model concept: how does each system construct what a disease 'is'?; discuss how biomedicine's dominance creates power asymmetries that may devalue traditional systems; identify one evidence-based practice from the non-biomedical system that has been adopted or studied by biomedicine)",
