        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Study comparative politics for my exam — review the core concepts of regime types, democratization, and political institutions: regime types and classification (Dahl's polyarchy: two dimensions — contestation (political competition) and inclusion (suffrage); full democracy = high on both; authoritarianism: rule by a minority using coercion and/or ideology; types: totalitarianism (full penetration of society — Nazi Germany, Stalinist USSR), military juntas, single-party systems, electoral authoritarianism; hybrid regimes: competitive authoritarianism (Levitsky & Way) — formal democratic institutions used to legitimize authoritarian rule; elections held but unfair; Russia, Hungary, Venezuela; illiberal democracy: majority rule without minority rights protection — Zakaria; Freedom House score criteria: civil liberties and political rights, 0–100 scale); democratization (Lipset's modernization thesis: economic development → social changes (urbanization, education, middle class growth) → democracy; Przeworski et al. critique: wealth sustains but doesn't cause democracy; democratic consolidation: Linz & Stepan — five arenas: civil society, political society, rule of law, state apparatus, economic society; third wave of democratization (Huntington): 1974 Portugal → ~1990 Soviet collapse; causes: economic development, Catholic Church, weakening of authoritarian regimes; democratic backsliding: gradual erosion of democratic norms — Hungary, Turkey, Brazil under Bolsonaro; Bermeo: three types — executive aggrandizement, strategic election manipulation, promissory coups); comparative institutions (electoral systems: proportional representation (PR): seats ∝ vote share; encourages multi-party systems, coalition governments; party list PR vs single transferable vote (STV); majoritarian/plurality: FPTP (First Past the Post) → two-party systems (Duverger's Law); tends toward stable single-party governments; mixed member proportional (MMP): Germany — combines PR and local representation; executive-legislative relations: presidentialism: president elected separately, fixed term, dual democratic legitimacy — Linz's critique: presidentialism is prone to deadlock and democratic breakdown; parliamentarism: prime minister dependent on legislative confidence; no fixed term; fusion of powers; semi-presidentialism: France — directly elected president + prime minister dependent on legislature; party systems: Duverger's Law: FPTP → two parties, PR → multi-party systems; party system institutionalization: predictability, stable roots in society, legitimacy; cleavage theory: Lipset-Rokkan freeze — party systems reflect class, religion, center-periphery, and rural-urban cleavages); varieties of capitalism (Hall & Soskice: VoC framework; liberal market economies (LME): U.S., UK — firms coordinate via markets; flexible labor markets, fluid capital, short-term finance; coordinated market economies (CME): Germany, Japan — firms coordinate via non-market mechanisms; long-term finance (banks), stable employment, vocational training; implications: LMEs produce radical innovation; CMEs produce incremental innovation; welfare states: Esping-Andersen's three worlds — liberal (U.S.), conservative/corporatist (Germany), social democratic (Sweden)",
            successCriteria: "Comparative politics study session completed (three regime types defined with one example each, Duverger's Law stated with application to FPTP and PR, Hall and Soskice VoC framework distinguished between LME and CME, one democratization theorist and argument identified, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Write my comparative politics paper or regime analysis — this may include: a democratization case study paper (select one country that democratized in the third wave (Spain, Brazil, South Korea, Poland) or one that has experienced democratic backsliding (Hungary, Turkey, Venezuela, Philippines); for democratization: describe the type of authoritarian regime preceding democratization (military junta, single-party, personalist); identify the proximate trigger for transition (elite pact, mass mobilization, military withdrawal, external pressure); apply the transitology framework: liberalization → transition → consolidation; evaluate Przeworski's concept of institutional choice under uncertainty: which actors agreed to democratize and what side-payments were required?; assess the role of economic development using Lipset's modernization thesis — was Lipset vindicated in this case?; describe the consolidation outcome using Linz and Stepan's five arenas; for backsliding: describe the pre-backsliding democratic status; identify the mechanism of backsliding: executive aggrandizement (court packing, media capture, electoral manipulation); apply Bermeo's typology; identify the actor driving backsliding (executive) and the institutional weakness exploited; evaluate whether external actors (EU conditionality, U.S. democracy promotion) slowed or failed to prevent backsliding; discuss one theorized causal factor specific to this case (polarization, economic crisis, weak rule of law, ethnic divisions)), a varieties of capitalism and welfare state analysis (select two countries — one LME (U.S., UK, Australia) and one CME (Germany, Sweden, Japan, Netherlands); describe each country's production regime using Hall & Soskice's VoC framework: labor relations (collective bargaining coverage, union density), corporate governance (patient vs fluid capital), inter-firm relations (supply chain coordination, industry associations), employee relations (vocational training system, skill formation); compare innovation profiles: does each country exhibit radical innovation (new products, disruptive technologies) or incremental innovation (quality improvements within existing sectors)? Provide industry examples; apply Esping-Andersen's welfare state typology: identify each country's welfare regime (liberal, conservative, social democratic); compare: decommodification (degree to which individuals can maintain livelihood independent of market), stratification (does welfare reinforce or break down class differences?), and familialism (is care work publicly supported or assumed by the family?); discuss one mechanism by which the production regime and welfare regime are complementary (institutional complementarity); identify one pressure on the CME model from globalization, financialization, or demographic change and evaluate whether the CME model is converging with LME), or a comparative electoral systems and party system analysis (select two countries with different electoral systems (e.g., U.S./UK using FPTP vs Germany/New Zealand using MMP vs Israel/Netherlands using list PR); describe the electoral formula, district magnitude, and threshold for each system; apply Duverger's Law: predict and verify the number of effective parties (ENP = 1/Σp²) in each system; compare fragmentation (ENP), polarization (distance between parties on left-right scale), and coalition frequency; apply the cleavage theory of Lipset-Rokkan: what historical cleavages (class, religion, urban-rural, center-periphery) structured each party system?; evaluate whether the cleavages have thawed (new parties: Green parties, far-right populist parties) or remain frozen; discuss one advantage and one disadvantage of each system for democratic representation and government stability)",
            successCriteria: "Comparative politics paper completed (specific country case analyzed with correct theoretical framework applied, one causal argument about democratization, backsliding, production regime, or electoral system supported with evidence, key concepts defined precisely, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.badge.shield.checkmark.fill",
            task: "Write my human rights paper, treaty analysis, or humanitarian law case study — this may include: a human rights treaty implementation analysis paper (select one country and one treaty: ICCPR, ICESCR, CAT (Convention Against Torture), CRC (Convention on the Rights of the Child), or CEDAW (Convention on Discrimination Against Women); review the most recent treaty body concluding observations for that country — what were the top three concerns and recommendations?; assess implementation: does domestic law incorporate treaty obligations? Does the constitution protect the right? Is there an independent national human rights institution (NHRI) with A-status accreditation?; identify one specific human rights violation documented by the treaty body, a UN special procedure, or an NGO (Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch) — describe the violation, its legal basis in the treaty, and the government's response; evaluate the enforcement gap — why is the violation persisting? Identify structural, political, or resource barriers; recommend one specific remedy: legislative change, institutional reform, domestic court litigation, or UPR engagement; discuss the limits of international human rights law: what can treaty bodies do vs what they cannot do?), a refugee and forced displacement paper (select one displacement context: Syrian refugees, Rohingya, Venezuelan migration, or South Sudan IDPs; describe the scale — number of refugees, IDPs, or asylum seekers, and the primary countries of asylum; apply the 1951 Refugee Convention: does the target population meet the legal definition of refugee? Is persecution on one of the five Convention grounds? What evidentiary standard applies?; describe the non-refoulement principle and identify one documented case of refoulement or pushback that violates it; analyze the protection gap for IDPs: why does the Refugee Convention not cover them? Do the Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement provide comparable protection?; evaluate the durable solutions framework: voluntary repatriation, local integration, resettlement — which is most feasible for this population and why?; discuss one structural cause of the displacement and one policy recommendation to address it), or an IHL application to a conflict situation (select one armed conflict since 2010; identify the parties and the applicable legal framework: international armed conflict (IAC) — Geneva Conventions + AP I; non-international armed conflict (NIAC) — Common Article 3 + AP II; describe one documented violation: targeting of civilians, indiscriminate attack, use of prohibited weapons (chemical weapons, cluster munitions in civilian areas), denial of humanitarian access, or torture of detainees; apply the IHL principles: was the distinction principle violated? Was the attack disproportionate? Were precautions taken?; identify the accountability mechanism — ICC, domestic prosecution, UN Commission of Inquiry, or no mechanism; evaluate the enforcement: why has accountability succeeded or failed in this case?; discuss one proposed reform to strengthen IHL compliance in NIAC contexts)",
            successCriteria: "Human rights paper or analysis completed (specific treaty, legal principle, or IHL rule applied to a concrete country or conflict situation, one documented violation or protection gap identified, remedy or accountability mechanism described, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // foodsystems
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "fork.knife.circle.fill",
            task: "Study food systems for my exam — review sustainable food systems, food sovereignty, food justice, food security, and food policy: food systems framework (a food system encompasses all activities and processes involved in feeding a population — from production, processing, packaging, distribution, retail, consumption, to waste management; food systems approach: examines interconnections between environmental, social, economic, and health outcomes of food; industrial food system: characterized by consolidation, specialization, global supply chains, monoculture, and commodity crops — high productivity but vulnerable to disruption and externalities; alternative food systems: local food, organic, fair trade, community-supported agriculture (CSA), food hubs, farmer's markets — emphasize sustainability, equity, and shortening supply chains; food miles: distance food travels from producer to consumer — critiqued as oversimplification; life cycle assessment (LCA): more holistic measure of food environmental impact including production, transport, packaging, and waste); food sovereignty and food justice (food sovereignty: La Via Campesina (1996) — the right of peoples to define their own food systems; emphasizes peasant farmers, indigenous knowledge, and democratic control over food policy vs. WTO/corporate control; food justice: justice-based approach to food systems — addresses racial, economic, and geographic inequities in food access; food deserts: geographic areas with limited access to affordable and nutritious food, often in low-income or communities of color; food apartheid: Leah Penniman's term — argues "desert" obscures intentional disinvestment; food environment: the physical, economic, political, and sociocultural context in which food decisions are made; community food security: all community members have access to a culturally acceptable, nutritionally adequate diet through sustainable, non-emergency food sources; structural determinants: food retail redlining, agricultural subsidies favoring commodity crops, concentrated corporate food power, land tenure inequities); food supply chains and policy (global commodity chains: geographic dispersal of food production — GCC (global commodity chain) and GVC (global value chain) frameworks; consolidation in food sector: oligopoly among input suppliers (seeds, fertilizers, pesticides) and food processors and retailers — Walmart, Tyson, Cargill, Bunge; farm bill (U.S.): primary federal food and agriculture legislation — commodity programs, crop insurance, SNAP (nutrition programs), conservation, rural development; SNAP (Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program): largest federal food assistance program — income-tested; food labeling: FDA vs USDA jurisdictions; organic certification: USDA NOP; fair trade certification: minimum price guarantee for commodity producers; WTO Agreement on Agriculture: constrains domestic support and export subsidies in global food trade; food loss vs food waste: loss occurs pre-retail; waste occurs at retail and consumer levels — FAO estimates one-third of food produced is lost or wasted)",
            successCriteria: "Food systems study session completed (sustainable vs industrial food system defined with one distinguishing feature, food sovereignty vs food justice distinguished with one key concept each, one structural barrier to food access identified, food supply chain and one policy lever described, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "fork.knife.circle.fill",
            task: "Write my food systems paper — this may include: a food justice and food access analysis (select one geographic community — a specific U.S. city neighborhood, rural county, or international region; map the food environment: identify supermarkets, fast food outlets, corner stores, farmers' markets, and food banks within a defined radius; apply the food desert definition (USDA: low-income census tract + low supermarket access + 1 mile urban/10 miles rural) and evaluate whether the \"food apartheid\" framing is more analytically useful; identify at least two structural causes of limited food access in this community: historical redlining, grocery store redlining, agricultural subsidy structure, zoning laws, or economic disinvestment; apply a food justice lens: who bears the burden of food insecurity, and along what axes of identity (race, class, immigration status, disability)?; evaluate one existing intervention: SNAP, WIC, community garden, CSA subsidies, food hub, mobile grocery — does it address root causes or just symptoms?; propose one structural policy intervention beyond charitable food provision), a food sovereignty vs food security paper (define and contrast food security (four pillars: availability, access, utilization, stability — FAO) and food sovereignty (La Via Campesina: right of peoples to control food systems); analyze who gets to define the food system: states and international organizations (FAO, WTO, World Bank) vs peasant movements and indigenous communities; apply case study: select one country or region (Sub-Saharan Africa, Central America, India, Southeast Asia) and analyze how food sovereignty principles conflict with or complement national food security policy; examine trade-offs: does food sovereignty prioritize cultural and political autonomy at the expense of productivity or food availability?; assess one international policy arena — WTO Agreement on Agriculture, CGIAR seed systems, or IMF structural adjustment — through a food sovereignty lens; argue whether food security and food sovereignty are complementary goals, competing frameworks, or whether sovereignty is a precondition for security), or a sustainable food systems transition analysis (define the current industrial food system using three sustainability criteria: environmental (GHG emissions, land use, biodiversity, water); social (labor conditions, community impacts, food access equity); economic (farm income, consolidation, price volatility); identify one leverage point for systems change: agroecological transition, alternative ownership models (cooperative, CSA), public procurement reform, or food policy councils; apply a food systems lens: trace the impacts of your chosen intervention across at least three system domains (environment, equity, supply chain, consumer behavior); evaluate one policy instrument that could enable the transition at scale — farm bill reform, urban agriculture zoning, food hub investment, carbon farming incentives, or fair trade expansion; discuss one trade-off or barrier: productivity concerns, cost, political opposition, or path dependence of the industrial system)",
            successCriteria: "Food systems paper completed (food justice, food sovereignty, or sustainable food systems framework applied to a specific case, structural causes identified and analyzed, policy intervention evaluated with at least one trade-off, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // musichistory
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "music.quarternote.3",
            task: "Study music history for my exam — review the major historical periods, composers, genres, and analytical frameworks: medieval and Renaissance music (Medieval: monophony → polyphony — Gregorian chant: monophonic, unaccompanied liturgical singing; modes (Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian, etc.) vs major/minor scales; Notre Dame school: Léonin (Magnus Liber Organi) and Pérotin — first polyphonic composers; motet: sacred polyphonic form — multiple texts in different languages simultaneously; troubadours and trouvères: secular song tradition in Occitan and Old French; ars nova (14th century): more complex rhythmic notation — Philippe de Vitry's Ars Nova treatise; isorhythm: rhythmic pattern (talea) repeating with melodic pattern (color) — used in motets; Guillaume de Machaut: leading composer of ars nova — Messe de Nostre Dame (earliest complete polyphonic mass); Renaissance: smooth polyphony, imitation, humanist values — word painting: musical illustration of text meaning (e.g., ascending line on "rise"); Josquin des Prez: master of imitative polyphony — "Mille regretz"; Palestrina: Catholic polyphony in Counter-Reformation style — smooth, consonant, few dissonances; madrigal: secular vocal form — Italian text, expressive word painting; Orlando di Lasso, William Byrd, Thomas Tallis; chanson, motet, mass as dominant forms); Baroque music (1600–1750) (key characteristics: basso continuo (thorough bass) — harpsichord + cello holding harmonic foundation; terraced dynamics: sudden (not gradual) shifts in volume; ornamentation; counterpoint and polyphony; affect (Affektenlehre): one emotional state per movement; major/minor tonal system replaces modes; opera: born in Florence (Camerata) — Claudio Monteverdi, L'Orfeo (1607), first major opera; recitative: speech-like vocal delivery; aria: set-piece song with melody; da capo aria (ABA form); fugue: highly developed polyphonic form — Bach, Well-Tempered Clavier; Johann Sebastian Bach: master of counterpoint — Mass in B Minor, Goldberg Variations, The Art of Fugue; George Frideric Handel: opera and oratorio — Messiah; Antonio Vivaldi: concerto grosso — The Four Seasons; Henry Purcell: English Baroque — Dido and Aeneas; suite, sonata, concerto, oratorio as primary instrumental/vocal forms); Classical period (1750–1820) (key characteristics: homophony replaces polyphony; clarity, balance, formal structure; Alberti bass in piano music; gradual dynamics (crescendo/decrescendo); sonata form: exposition (two themes, tonic-dominant) → development → recapitulation; Joseph Haydn: 104 symphonies, string quartets — "Father of the Symphony"; Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart: symphonies, concertos, operas (Don Giovanni, The Magic Flute, Le Nozze di Figaro) — supreme melodic gift; Ludwig van Beethoven: transitions Classical→Romantic — 9 symphonies; string quartets; piano sonatas (Moonlight, Pathétique); hero narrative (overcoming deafness); symphony, string quartet, piano sonata, concerto, opera as primary forms); Romantic music (1820–1900) (key characteristics: emotional expression, individualism, nationalism, program music, large orchestras, chromaticism; program music vs absolute music: program music tells a story (Berlioz's Symphonie Fantastique) vs absolute music (Brahms' symphonies); nationalism: Dvořák, Sibelius, Smetana, Chopin; Lied (art song): Schubert, Schumann — piano + voice; Richard Wagner: Gesamtkunstwerk (total artwork) — leitmotif: recurring musical theme associated with character/idea; opera cycle Ring of the Nibelung; Johannes Brahms: Classical forms with Romantic harmony; Piotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky: ballets (Swan Lake, Nutcracker), symphonies; symphonic poem: Franz Liszt — single-movement orchestral work depicting narrative; Frédéric Chopin: piano miniatures — nocturne, étude, polonaise, mazurka; verismo opera: Puccini, Verdi — realistic subjects); 20th-century and contemporary music (impressionism: Debussy — whole-tone scale, parallel chords, orchestral color — La Mer, Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune; expressionism: Schoenberg, Berg, Webern — atonality, 12-tone technique (serialism) — Pierrot Lunaire; neoclassicism: Stravinsky, Prokofiev — return to Classical forms with modern harmony — The Rite of Spring; minimalism: Steve Reich, Philip Glass, John Adams — repetition, gradual change, steady pulse; John Cage: chance music (aleatoric) — 4'33"; electronic music: musique concrète (Pierre Schaeffer); spectralism; popular music studies: blues, jazz, rock and roll, hip-hop as legitimate fields of musicological inquiry)",
            successCriteria: "Music history study session completed (each major period identified with one defining characteristic and one representative composer, sonata form defined with exposition/development/recapitulation, one 20th-century style distinguished from Romantic tradition, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "music.quarternote.3",
            task: "Write my music history paper or style analysis — this may include: a comparative period style analysis (select two works from different historical periods — e.g., a Baroque Bach fugue vs a Classical Haydn string quartet movement, or a Romantic Brahms symphony vs a 20th-century Stravinsky work; for each work describe: texture (monophony, polyphony, homophony), rhythm (meter, rhythmic complexity, ornamentation), melody (melodic contour, phrase structure, motivic development), harmony (diatonic, chromatic, atonal), form (binary, ternary, sonata, fugue, through-composed), timbre and instrumentation, and dynamics; apply the Affektenlehre to Baroque works; apply sonata form analysis to Classical and early Romantic works; identify one compositional technique unique to each period; discuss one historical or cultural factor that shaped each compositional style — patronage system, religious context, nationalism, industrialization, or avant-garde reaction), a composer biography and influence paper (select one composer: Bach, Mozart, Beethoven, Schubert, Brahms, Wagner, Debussy, Stravinsky, Schoenberg, or Shostakovich; describe the composer's historical context: who were their patrons? What institutions shaped their career?; identify their major works by genre; analyze one specific work in depth — describe its form, texture, harmonic language, and expressive intent; trace one compositional innovation introduced by this composer and its influence on later composers; evaluate the composer's reception: were they celebrated or rejected in their time? How has critical assessment changed?; discuss one social, political, or religious dimension of their music), or a music history listening journal entry (select one assigned listening work; identify: composer, title, genre, historical period, approximate date of composition; describe musical elements: texture, rhythm, meter, melody, harmony, form, dynamics, timbre; identify at least two period-specific features that locate this work in its historical style; connect to one historical or biographical fact about the composer or context; write a brief personal response: what emotional or intellectual response does the work evoke, and does it align with or challenge the era's aesthetic goals?)",
            successCriteria: "Music history paper or style analysis completed (at least two musical elements analyzed using correct period-specific terminology, one historical or cultural context described, one specific musical work identified by composer and title, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 75 * 60
        ),
        // genderstudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            task: "Study gender studies for my exam — review feminist theory, queer theory, intersectionality, and gender and sexuality frameworks: waves of feminism (first wave: late 19th–early 20th century — focused on legal equality, suffrage (19th Amendment, 1920); key figures: Elizabeth Cady Stanton, Sojourner Truth, Emmeline Pankhurst; Seneca Falls Declaration (1848): modeled on Declaration of Independence, demanded women's suffrage and legal rights; second wave: 1960s–1980s — expanded focus to reproductive rights, workplace equality, domestic violence, sexuality; The Feminine Mystique (Betty Friedan, 1963): critiqued domestic role of middle-class women; NOW (National Organization for Women, 1966); radical feminism: patriarchy is the primary oppressor — Andrea Dworkin, Catharine MacKinnon; socialist/Marxist feminism: links gender oppression to capitalism; second wave critiqued for centering white, middle-class, heterosexual women — Audre Lorde, bell hooks; third wave: 1990s — embraced diversity, intersectionality, individual expression, reclamation of "femininity," sex positivity; influenced by poststructuralism and queer theory; fourth wave: 2010s–present — digital feminism, #MeToo, online activism, focus on sexual violence and harassment); feminist theory (liberal feminism: equal rights within existing institutions — law, workplace; radical feminism: patriarchy as the root system; socialist feminism: capitalism + patriarchy; standpoint epistemology: Sandra Harding, Patricia Hill Collins — knowledge is situated; the standpoint of the oppressed reveals truths invisible from positions of privilege; care ethics: Carol Gilligan — relational, context-sensitive ethical reasoning (vs Kohlberg's abstract justice); Simone de Beauvoir: "One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman" — gender as socially constructed; Betty Friedan: "the problem that has no name" — suburban women's dissatisfaction; Audre Lorde: "the master's tools will never dismantle the master's house" — difference and identity; bell hooks: feminism as a movement to end sexism and sexist oppression, not just women's issues; Kate Millett: sexual politics — patriarchy as political system); gender performativity and queer theory (Judith Butler, Gender Trouble (1990): gender is not an inner identity but a performance — repeated acts that create the illusion of a stable gendered self; performativity: not a free choice but a constrained repetition of norms; parody and drag: expose the imitative structure of gender — no original to copy; heteronormativity: the assumption that heterosexuality is the default and natural; Michel Foucault: The History of Sexuality — sexuality is not liberated but produced by power/knowledge; queer theory: deconstructs fixed categories of sexual identity; Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick: homosexual panic and the homosocial; Adrienne Rich: "compulsory heterosexuality" — heterosexuality is a political institution, not natural; intersex and trans studies: challenge binary sex categories); intersectionality (Kimberlé Crenshaw, 1989: intersectionality — Black women's legal injuries were not addressed by race discrimination law OR sex discrimination law because courts analyzed each separately; social categories (race, gender, class, sexuality, disability, citizenship) are not additive but mutually constitutive; Patricia Hill Collins: matrix of domination — interlocking systems of oppression; privilege: unearned advantages conferred by social location; oppression: unjust and harmful treatment of individuals based on group membership; standpoint theory applied: Black feminist thought — outsider-within status provides unique epistemological standpoint; intersectionality in practice: policy analysis must account for multiple axes of identity, not treat groups as internally homogeneous)",
            successCriteria: "Gender studies study session completed (each feminist wave identified with one key demand or focus, Butler's gender performativity defined, Crenshaw's intersectionality explained with the original legal context, one feminist theorist and their central argument identified, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.2.fill",
            task: "Write my gender studies paper — this may include: a feminist theory application paper (select one social institution or cultural phenomenon: the wage gap, reproductive rights policy, domestic division of labor, representation in media or politics, sexual harassment law, or beauty standards; apply two feminist theoretical frameworks to your chosen topic — e.g., liberal feminism (policy reform) vs radical feminism (structural patriarchy) or intersectional feminism vs standpoint epistemology; describe how each framework diagnoses the problem and proposes solutions; compare the explanatory power of each framework: what does each illuminate and what does it miss?; incorporate at least one primary text: a key theorist's direct argument (Friedan, Butler, Crenshaw, Collins, hooks, or Lorde); evaluate a critique of your preferred framework: a common objection and how theorists in that tradition respond; discuss one concrete implication for policy, law, or cultural practice), a gender performativity and identity analysis (apply Judith Butler's theory of gender performativity to one cultural text, social practice, or institutional context — choose from: drag performance, beauty pageants, sports gender verification, workplace dress codes, parenting norms, or military service standards; describe Butler's core claim: gender is a performative repetition, not an expression of inner essence; identify the specific norms being performed or enforced in your chosen context; locate evidence of heteronormativity: how does this context assume or enforce heterosexual gender norms?; identify one moment of "gender trouble" or parody — where the performance exposes the constructed nature of gender; apply Foucault: how is power operating through this context? What bodies or practices are normalized and which are pathologized?; discuss one concrete consequence: for individuals who conform vs those who deviate from the norm), or an intersectionality analysis paper (select a legal case, policy area, or social issue where intersecting identities create unique disadvantages: healthcare access, immigration enforcement, housing discrimination, criminal sentencing, or reproductive policy; apply Crenshaw's framework: how does analyzing race and gender separately fail to capture the experience of people at the intersection?; apply Collins' matrix of domination: identify structural (law, policy), disciplinary (bureaucratic enforcement), hegemonic (cultural norms), and interpersonal dimensions of oppression; identify one group whose specific situation is made invisible by single-axis analysis; propose a policy or legal remedy that is explicitly intersectional — designed for people at the intersection rather than a generic protected class; evaluate the limits of your analysis: what intersections are you not accounting for?)",
            successCriteria: "Gender studies paper completed (feminist theory, gender performativity, or intersectionality framework applied to a specific case or phenomenon, at least one key theorist cited with their core argument, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // peacestudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "leaf.fill",
            task: "Study peace and conflict studies for my exam — review Galtung's peace theory, conflict resolution, peacebuilding, and transitional justice: Galtung's framework (Johan Galtung: father of peace studies — \"peace studies\" as an academic field; negative peace: the absence of direct violence (war, interpersonal violence) — a minimal definition; positive peace: the absence of structural and cultural violence + the presence of social justice, equity, and human rights — a more demanding standard; violence triangle: direct violence (physical/verbal harm), structural violence (systemic inequality built into social structures — poverty, discrimination), cultural violence (beliefs/ideologies that justify direct and structural violence — religion, ideology, art); conflict triangle: attitudes (hostile perceptions and emotions) + behavior (violent or coercive actions) + contradiction (incompatible goals) — ABC model; conflict transformation vs conflict resolution: resolution implies ending the conflict; transformation aims to address underlying contradictions and relationships; positive peace building framework: reconstruction of social systems, institutions, and relationships that support sustainable peace); conflict dynamics and theories (conflict escalation: Glasl's escalation model — nine stages from hardening positions to mutual destruction; spoilers: armed groups or elites who undermine peace agreements (Stephen Stedman); greed vs grievance debate: Collier and Hoeffler — civil war driven by opportunity (lootable resources) vs Gurr — relative deprivation and grievances; ethnic conflict theories: primordialist (ancient hatreds) vs constructivist (identities constructed and manipulated) vs instrumentalist (elites use ethnicity for political goals); resource conflict: climate change, water scarcity, and land tenure as structural conflict drivers; peace zones: local peace agreements, peace villages, and civilian protection strategies); conflict resolution and peacebuilding (conflict resolution tools: negotiation, mediation, arbitration, facilitation, restorative justice; mediation: third party facilitates negotiation — evaluative vs facilitative vs transformative mediation; track-one diplomacy: official state-to-state negotiations; track-two diplomacy: unofficial, citizen, NGO, and academic-level dialogue; multi-track diplomacy: combines all tracks simultaneously; peacebuilding continuum: prevention → crisis response → stabilization → normalization → reconciliation → development; DDR: disarmament, demobilization, and reintegration of ex-combatants; SSR: security sector reform — restructuring police, military, judiciary for accountability; liberal peacebuilding critique: Roland Paris — liberal peace assumes democracy + market economy = peace; criticized as externally imposed and culturally inappropriate; local turn: Séverine Autesserre — international peacebuilding misses local conflict drivers and local agency); transitional justice (transitional justice: set of judicial and non-judicial mechanisms to address past human rights violations in societies transitioning from conflict or authoritarianism; four pillars: truth (truth and reconciliation commissions — South Africa, Rwanda Gacaca), justice (prosecutions — ICTY, ICTR, ICC, hybrid courts), reparations (material and symbolic — Colombia, Peru), guarantees of non-recurrence (institutional reform, memory); truth commissions: investigate past violations without necessarily prosecuting — South Africa TRC; amnesty: controversial tool — facilitates settlements but may deny justice; ICC: International Criminal Court — jurisdiction over genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes, aggression; complementarity principle: ICC acts only when states are unable or unwilling to prosecute; vetting: removal of rights abusers from public office as institutional reform)",
            successCriteria: "Peace studies study session completed (negative vs positive peace defined with examples, Galtung's violence triangle explained, one conflict resolution mechanism described, transitional justice defined with at least two of four pillars, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "leaf.fill",
            task: "Write my peace studies paper or conflict analysis — this may include: a conflict analysis using the ABC model (select one contemporary or historical conflict: a civil war, inter-communal violence, territorial dispute, or post-conflict society; apply Galtung's ABC triangle: what are the attitudes (hostile perceptions, dehumanization, historical grievances)?; what behaviors (military operations, sanctions, political violence, ceasefire violations)?; what is the core contradiction (incompatible goals: territorial control, political power, resource access, identity recognition)?; assess whether the conflict is primarily characterized by greed (lootable resources, economic incentives for combatants) or grievance (relative deprivation, discrimination, historical injustice) — evaluate the evidence for each; identify structural violence underlying the conflict: what pre-existing inequalities, institutional exclusions, or resource distributions created the conditions?; evaluate one peace initiative that has been attempted: what track was it (track-one diplomacy, mediation, power-sharing agreement)?; apply one spoiler theory: which actors have incentives to undermine peace and why?; propose one conflict transformation strategy addressing attitudes, behavior, and contradiction simultaneously), a peacebuilding evaluation paper (select one post-conflict case: Sierra Leone, Liberia, Rwanda, Bosnia, East Timor, Colombia, or South Sudan; describe the conflict that preceded the peacebuilding operation: key parties, nature of violence, duration, and the peace agreement that ended it; evaluate the peacebuilding operation against the five pillars of peacebuilding: security (DDR, SSR), political (elections, governance), economic (reconstruction, development), social (reconciliation, return of displaced persons), and justice/rule of law (transitional justice, accountability); apply Roland Paris's liberal peacebuilding critique: did the operation impose externally-derived democracy and market economy frameworks without attention to local conditions?; apply Séverine Autesserre's local turn critique: did the international intervention miss local conflict drivers or bypass local peacebuilders?; evaluate the transitional justice mechanisms used: truth commission, prosecutions, reparations, vetting — were all four pillars addressed?; assess the current status: is the peace sustainable and what structural risks remain?), or a nonviolent resistance and civil resistance analysis (select one nonviolent movement: Gandhi's independence campaign, U.S. civil rights movement, Solidarity in Poland, Serbia's Otpor, the Arab Spring in Tunisia, or the Hong Kong protests; describe the movement's goals and the regime or structure it challenged; apply Gene Sharp's theory: identify at least three categories of nonviolent action used (protest/persuasion, noncooperation, nonviolent intervention); apply Erica Chenoweth's research findings: how does nonviolent resistance compare to armed insurgency in achieving campaign success?; identify the pillars of support that the movement sought to undermine: military, police, economic elites, civil servants, or media; evaluate loyalty shifts: did the regime's own supporters defect? What was the mechanism?; assess the outcome: what did the movement achieve? What was not achieved? What role did repression play in either strengthening or weakening the movement?; discuss one structural condition that contributed to success or failure)",
            successCriteria: "Peace studies paper or conflict analysis completed (Galtung's ABC model or peacebuilding framework or civil resistance theory applied to a specific conflict or movement, at least one structural factor identified, one peace mechanism evaluated, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // globalstudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "globe.americas.fill",
            task: "Study global studies for my exam — review globalization, global citizenship, transnational issues, and cross-cultural competency: globalization frameworks (globalization: the intensification of worldwide social relations linking distant localities — Anthony Giddens; Held and McGrew: transformationalist view — globalization is reshaping but not dissolving the state; hyperglobalist view: global markets supersede states; skeptical view: globalization is not historically unprecedented and states remain primary; economic globalization: trade liberalization (GATT/WTO), foreign direct investment (FDI), global supply chains, transnational corporations (TNCs); Bretton Woods institutions: IMF (balance of payments support), World Bank (development lending), WTO (trade rules) — created post-WWII liberal international economic order; Washington Consensus: market liberalization, privatization, deregulation — promoted by IMF/World Bank in 1980s–90s; critiques: increases inequality, undermines public services, fails to account for local context; cultural globalization: McDonaldization (Ritzer) — rationalization of global culture; hybridization (Pieterse) — global and local cultures blend; cultural imperialism critique: Western/American culture dominates and displaces local cultures; political globalization: UN system, international organizations (WTO, IMF, WHO, UNESCO), global governance; democratic deficit: international organizations lack democratic accountability; state sovereignty erosion: international law, R2P, human rights regimes constrain state autonomy; glocalization: Robertson — global processes are adapted and reinterpreted at local level); transnational issues (global migration: push factors (conflict, poverty, climate displacement) + pull factors (economic opportunity, family reunion); remittances: $800B+ annually — exceed ODA; brain drain; xenophobia and anti-immigrant politics; climate change as transnational challenge: no single state can address it — collective action problem; Paris Agreement (2015): nationally determined contributions (NDCs); common but differentiated responsibilities (CBDR); climate justice: historically responsible vs most vulnerable nations; global health governance: WHO; TRIPS Agreement and access to medicines; COVID-19 as test of global health cooperation; global inequality: Milanovic's elephant curve — global middle class in Asia grew, Western working class stagnated, global top 1% gained most; North-South divide; least developed countries (LDCs); Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs, 2015–2030): 17 goals, 169 targets — poverty, health, education, gender equality, climate, peace); cross-cultural competency (cross-cultural competence (CCC): ability to interact effectively across cultures; Bennett's DMIS (Developmental Model of Intercultural Sensitivity): ethnocentrism (denial, defense, minimization) → ethnorelativism (acceptance, adaptation, integration); cultural dimensions: Hofstede's six dimensions — power distance, individualism vs collectivism, masculinity vs femininity, uncertainty avoidance, long-term orientation, indulgence; Edward Hall: high-context vs low-context communication — implicit vs explicit; monochronic vs polychronic time; Hall's proxemics: personal space varies by culture; Geert Hofstede's individualism vs collectivism: U.S. highest individualism index; Japan high uncertainty avoidance; cultural humility: ongoing self-reflection and openness vs static cultural competence; global citizenship: cosmopolitan identity, responsibility to the global community, awareness of global interconnectedness)",
            successCriteria: "Global studies study session completed (globalization defined with one economic and one cultural dimension, one transnational challenge analyzed with a governance response, Hofstede's cultural dimensions described with one example, and global citizenship concept explained, exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "globe.americas.fill",
            task: "Write my global studies paper — this may include: a globalization analysis paper (select one dimension of globalization — economic (global supply chains, FDI, TNCs), cultural (media flows, hybridization, cultural imperialism), political (global governance, sovereignty erosion, multilateralism), or environmental (climate change, biodiversity loss as global commons problems); describe the dimension's core dynamics using at least one theoretical framework: world-systems theory (Wallerstein), world polity theory (Meyer), global civil society (Kaldor), or Held and McGrew's transformationalism vs hyperglobalism; select one specific case study: a corporation's global supply chain, a cultural product's global diffusion, an international organization's governance response, or an environmental agreement; apply your framework: who are the winners and losers in this globalization process? Across what axes (nation, class, gender, region)?; evaluate one critique of globalization from below: anti-globalization movement, fair trade activism, global justice movement, or degrowth advocacy; assess what the case reveals about the limits or possibilities of global governance; discuss one reform proposal: cosmopolitan democracy (Held), global taxation (Piketty), alternative trade frameworks, or commons governance), a global migration and transnationalism paper (select one migration corridor or migrant community — Mexico-U.S., Philippines-Gulf States, Sub-Saharan Africa-Europe, South Asia-U.K., or a specific diaspora; describe the migratory system: magnitude, duration, legal pathways (labor migration, family reunification, asylum), undocumented flows; apply push-pull framework AND structural critique: what structural conditions in sending and receiving countries shape migration?; analyze transnationalism: in what ways do migrants maintain social, economic, and political ties across borders? (dual citizenship, hometown associations, remittances, transnational political participation); quantify remittances: how significant are they to the sending country's GDP and household welfare? Do they reduce inequality or create dependency?; evaluate one migration governance regime: EU free movement, Gulf kafala system, U.S. DACA, or a bilateral labor agreement — what does it allow and restrict?; assess one tension: integration vs multiculturalism in the receiving country; right of movement vs national sovereignty), or a cross-cultural competency reflection and application paper (describe a cross-cultural encounter or challenge — from a study abroad experience, international fieldwork, diverse classroom, or organizational context; apply Bennett's DMIS: at what stage of intercultural sensitivity did you or others respond? What evidence supports this assessment?; apply Hofstede's dimensions: identify at least two cultural dimension differences that shaped the interaction (power distance, individualism/collectivism, uncertainty avoidance, long-term orientation); apply Hall's framework: were communication style differences (high vs low context) or time orientation (monochronic vs polychronic) a factor?; reflect on your own cultural positioning: what assumptions did you bring? How were they challenged?; describe one specific moment of cultural misunderstanding or successful intercultural communication; propose one concrete action to develop greater cultural humility in this context; discuss how this connects to your understanding of global citizenship responsibilities)",
            successCriteria: "Global studies paper completed (globalization framework, migration analysis, or cross-cultural competency model applied to a specific case, at least one theoretical framework named and applied, winners/losers or structural factors identified, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // socialinnovation
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "lightbulb.circle.fill",
            task: "Study social innovation for my exam — review systems thinking, design thinking, theory of change, and impact measurement frameworks: foundations of social innovation (social innovation: new solutions — products, services, processes, or business models — that simultaneously address social needs more effectively than alternatives and create new social relationships and collaborations; Joseph Schumpeter's creative destruction applied to social change; four stages of social innovation (Murray, Caulier-Grice, Mulgan): prompts (identifying the problem), proposals (generating solutions), prototyping (testing ideas), sustaining (scaling and embedding); social enterprise: businesses that trade to tackle social problems — Community Interest Companies (CIC), B Corps, worker cooperatives, social purpose organizations; systems thinking: understanding complex interdependencies — Donella Meadows, Thinking in Systems; feedback loops: reinforcing (amplifying change) and balancing (stabilizing); leverage points: places in a system where a small shift can produce large changes — Meadows' 12 leverage points — paradigm shifts are most powerful; iceberg model: visible events → patterns and trends → underlying structures → mental models; wicked problems: problems characterized by incomplete, contradictory, and changing requirements (Rittel and Webber) — complex social problems where the solution affects the problem definition); design thinking for social impact (design thinking process: empathize → define → ideate → prototype → test — IDEO/d.school model; human-centered design: starting with the needs, behaviors, and experiences of the people being served; empathy mapping: capturing what users say, think, do, and feel; journey mapping: visualizing the end-to-end experience of a user; co-design: involving beneficiaries and stakeholders in designing solutions; prototyping: low-fidelity and high-fidelity testing before full implementation; fail fast, learn fast: iterative improvement rather than fear of failure; asset-based community development (ABCD): building on existing community strengths rather than deficits; participatory action research (PAR): community members as co-researchers); theory of change and impact measurement (theory of change (ToC): a comprehensive description of how and why a desired change is expected to happen in a particular context; inputs → activities → outputs → outcomes → impact; logic model: visual representation of a program's theory of change; assumptions: underlying conditions that must be true for the theory to hold; causal pathways: the chain of events linking activities to long-term outcomes; SMART outcomes: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound; impact measurement frameworks: Social Return on Investment (SROI) — ratio of social value to investment; Balanced Scorecard; Theory-Driven Evaluation; Most Significant Change (MSC); IRIS metrics (Impact Reporting and Investment Standards); counterfactual: what would have happened without the intervention?; attribution vs contribution: organizations rarely cause change alone; deadweight: the amount of change that would have happened anyway; displacement: how much does the intervention displace other activity?; scaling social innovation: spreading and replicating successful innovations — scaling up (policy change), scaling out (replication and adaptation), scaling deep (changing norms and relationships))",
            successCriteria: "Social innovation study session completed (systems thinking feedback loops defined, design thinking process described with at least three stages, theory of change logic model explained with inputs/outputs/outcomes, one impact measurement approach identified, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "lightbulb.circle.fill",
            task: "Write my social innovation paper — this may include: a theory of change development (identify a persistent social problem: educational attainment gaps, youth unemployment, food insecurity, housing unaffordability, mental health access, or climate vulnerability in a specific community; conduct a root cause analysis: apply the iceberg model to identify the events, patterns, structures, and mental models that sustain the problem; develop a theory of change: map inputs (financial, human, physical resources), activities (what the organization does), outputs (direct products/services), short-term outcomes (changes in knowledge, skills, attitudes), long-term outcomes (changes in behavior and conditions), and ultimate impact (systemic change); identify at least three key assumptions your theory relies on; identify potential unintended consequences — what negative side effects might this intervention produce?; propose an evaluation plan: what would you measure at each level of the logic model, using which data collection methods and at what intervals?; apply a scaling lens: would this innovation scale up (policy), scale out (replication), or scale deep (norm change)?; identify one potential barrier to scaling and one enabling condition), a social innovation case study analysis (select one recognized social innovation: microfinance (Grameen Bank), Aravind Eye Care System, Khan Academy, BRAC, One Acre Fund, Crisis Text Line, or a local social enterprise; describe the problem it addresses and the population served; analyze the innovation: what makes it innovative? How does it differ from previous solutions or government/market approaches?; map the organization's theory of change: inputs, activities, outputs, outcomes, impact; apply systems thinking: what feedback loops does this innovation disrupt or create? What leverage point does it address?; evaluate evidence of impact: what metrics does the organization use? What does the evidence show? What methodological limitations exist in the evaluation?; assess the scaling journey: how has the organization grown? What adaptations were required? What was lost in scaling?; identify one tension or trade-off: mission vs growth, effectiveness vs efficiency, innovation vs accountability), or an impact measurement plan (select a social program or organization to evaluate — it can be real or hypothetical; define the problem and target population precisely; construct a logic model: list inputs, activities, outputs, short-term outcomes, long-term outcomes, and ultimate impact; select three indicators for the most important outcomes — justify why each indicator is appropriate, measurable, and actionable; address attribution: how would you estimate the counterfactual (what would have happened without the intervention)?; select an evaluation design: randomized control trial, quasi-experimental, pre-post comparison, or most significant change — justify your choice given constraints; identify data sources: administrative data, surveys, focus groups, ethnography, or secondary data; describe one ethical consideration in evaluation: consent, power dynamics, community participation in shaping evaluation, or use of findings; propose how you would communicate findings to stakeholders: policymakers, funders, beneficiaries, and the general public)",
            successCriteria: "Social innovation paper completed (theory of change, case analysis, or impact measurement framework applied to a specific social problem or organization, inputs/outputs/outcomes described, at least one evaluation approach proposed, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // philosophyofscience
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "atom",
            task: "Study philosophy of science for my exam — review Kuhn, Popper, Lakatos, Feyerabend, scientific realism, and the demarcation problem: demarcation and falsificationism (the demarcation problem: how do we distinguish science from non-science and pseudoscience?; logical positivism and the verification principle: a statement is meaningful only if empirically verifiable — Ayer, Carnap, Vienna Circle; Karl Popper's falsificationism: a theory is scientific only if it is falsifiable — capable of being tested and potentially proven wrong; corroboration: a theory that has survived rigorous attempts to falsify it is corroborated (not confirmed); Popper's critique of Freud and Marx: their theories are unfalsifiable because they explain everything and predict nothing definitive; verisimilitude: Popper's notion that science aims at theories that are closer to the truth; nave falsificationism's problem: a single failed prediction rarely causes scientists to reject a well-established theory — auxiliary hypotheses absorb the falsification; Duhem-Quine thesis (underdetermination): any observation can be accommodated by adjusting auxiliary hypotheses — theories are never tested in isolation; holism in science: Pierre Duhem and Willard Van Orman Quine — it is the body of knowledge, not a single hypothesis, that faces the tribunal of experience); Kuhn's structure of scientific revolutions (Thomas Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962): science develops not through accumulation but through periodic revolutionary restructuring; paradigm: a shared framework of concepts, theories, methods, and values within a scientific community (disciplinary matrix); normal science: puzzle-solving within a paradigm — anomalies are explained away or tolerated; crisis: when anomalies accumulate and can no longer be accommodated within the existing paradigm; scientific revolution: a paradigm shift — the community adopts a new paradigm (Copernican, Newtonian, Einsteinian, DNA); incommensurability: paradigms are not directly comparable — scientists working in different paradigms see the world differently; world-change: Kuhn's controversial claim that after a paradigm shift scientists work in a different world; sociology of science: Kuhn introduced sociological factors (community, consensus, persuasion) into the philosophy of science; influence: constructivist turn, relativism debates, sociology of scientific knowledge (SSK)); Lakatos, Feyerabend, and scientific realism (Imre Lakatos, methodology of scientific research programmes: series of theories connected by a hard core (untouchable assumptions) + protective belt of auxiliary hypotheses; progressive research programme: adds new predictions confirmed by evidence; degenerating research programme: only modifies auxiliary hypotheses ad hoc; Paul Feyerabend, Against Method (1975): epistemological anarchism — "anything goes" — no fixed method separates science from non-science; criticism of Popper and Lakatos: Galileo succeeded by violating methodological rules; scientific realism: the view that successful scientific theories are approximately true and refer to real entities — electrons, quarks, fields; anti-realism: instrumentalism (theories are just useful tools — van Fraassen's constructive empiricism), social constructivism (scientific facts are constructed by communities — Latour, SSK), structural realism (Worrall — structure is preserved across theory change even if entities change); miracle argument for realism: the success of science would be a miracle if theories were not approximately true (Putnam); pessimistic meta-induction: past successful theories were later proven false — so why believe current theories are approximately true? (Laudan))",
            successCriteria: "Philosophy of science study session completed (Popper's falsificationism defined, Kuhn's paradigm shift and normal science explained, Lakatos's research programme described, demarcation problem stated, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "atom",
            task: "Write my philosophy of science paper — this may include: a Kuhn vs Popper comparison paper (state each philosopher's answer to the demarcation problem: what makes something scientific?; explain Popper's falsificationism: give a clear example of a falsifiable vs unfalsifiable claim; apply Popper's criterion: is Newtonian mechanics falsifiable? What happened when Mercury's perihelion anomaly emerged? Did scientists immediately reject the theory?; introduce Kuhn's critique: normal science does not operate by bold conjectures and severe testing — scientists protect their paradigm and only abandon it in a crisis; analyze the role of anomalies: how does each thinker explain why scientists don't immediately falsify a theory when disconfirming evidence emerges?; introduce Lakatos as a synthesis: how does the methodology of scientific research programmes attempt to incorporate both Popperian falsification and Kuhnian sociology?; discuss incommensurability: is Kuhn's claim that paradigms cannot be rationally compared consistent with scientific progress?; take a position: whose account of science is more accurate? Justify using at least one historical case — the Copernican revolution, the germ theory of disease, or the shift to quantum mechanics; identify one limitation of your preferred account), a scientific realism debate paper (define scientific realism: the claim that successful scientific theories are approximately true and refer to real unobservable entities; present the miracle argument (Putnam/Boyd): explain why the predictive success of science seems to require that theories are approximately true; present the pessimistic meta-induction (Laudan): trace at least two examples of past theories that were successful but later abandoned — caloric theory of heat, phlogiston, the luminiferous ether, Newtonian absolute space; assess what entity continuity across theory change shows: do electrons survive the shift from classical to quantum mechanics?; introduce constructive empiricism (van Fraassen): what does it mean to accept a theory as empirically adequate rather than true?; introduce structural realism (Worrall): how does structural realism attempt to preserve realism about structure while remaining agnostic about unobservable entities?; evaluate: does the structural realist solution to the pessimistic meta-induction succeed?; take a position and defend it with at least two arguments), or a case study in scientific change (select one historical episode of scientific change: Copernican revolution, Lavoisier's oxygen vs phlogiston, the continental drift revolution, the germ theory of disease, the transition from classical to quantum mechanics, or the discovery of the double helix; apply Kuhn's framework: identify the prevailing paradigm, the accumulating anomalies, the crisis, and the paradigm shift; apply Popper's framework: what predictions did the old theory make that were falsified? What new bold conjectures did the new theory make?; apply Lakatos's framework: was the old research programme progressive or degenerating? What auxiliary hypotheses were modified to protect the hard core?; evaluate which framework best explains this episode of change; identify any social or political factors Kuhn would emphasize that Popper ignores; discuss what this episode reveals about the relationship between evidence and theory acceptance in science)",
            successCriteria: "Philosophy of science paper completed (Kuhn/Popper/Lakatos frameworks applied to a scientific realism debate or historical case study, demarcation criterion stated and evaluated, at least two philosophers' positions analyzed, and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // environmentalhumanities
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "leaf.circle.fill",
            task: "Study environmental humanities for my exam — review ecocriticism, posthumanism, more-than-human theory, and nature-culture frameworks: ecocriticism and environmental literary studies (ecocriticism: the study of the relationship between literature and the physical environment — Cheryll Glotfelty (ASLE, 1992); first-wave ecocriticism: focused on nature writing, pastoral literature, wilderness, and the representation of wild/pristine nature; second-wave ecocriticism: turned to urban environments, built environments, toxic landscapes, and environmental justice; third-wave and material ecocriticism: multispecies, affect theory, posthumanism; pastoral ideology: Raymond Williams, The Country and the City — the pastoral romanticizes rural nature and conceals the labor relations that produce it; nature writing: Thoreau (Walden), Muir, Leopold (A Sand County Almanac — land ethic); ecofeminism: connection between domination of nature and domination of women — Sherry Ortner, Carolyn Merchant; environmental justice ecocriticism: Rob Nixon's slow violence — violence that occurs gradually and out of sight (toxic contamination, climate displacement) vs spectacular violence; the language of environmentalism: how does framing shape environmental politics? (wilderness vs working landscape; conservation vs preservation); reading for the environment: what does literature reveal about human-nature relationships that scientific data cannot?); posthumanism and more-than-human theory (humanism: the Enlightenment tradition that places the human at the center of moral, political, and epistemological life; posthumanism: the critique of humanism — questioning the boundaries between human/animal/machine; N. Katherine Hayles, How We Became Posthuman (1999): the liberal humanist subject is historically constructed and now being challenged by information technology and cybernetics; critical posthumanism: Rosi Braidotti — the posthuman as an affirmative project of decentering the human; more-than-human turn: human life is always entangled with nonhuman actors — animals, plants, bacteria, soils, technologies, weather; Donna Haraway's Companion Species Manifesto (2003): dogs and humans co-constitute each other through shared evolution and practice; assemblage theory: Deleuze and Guattari — things are not stable entities but temporary assemblages of heterogeneous elements; Jane Bennett, Vibrant Matter: nonhuman things (electricity grids, food, metal) have their own form of agency — vitality or thing-power; agency distributed across human and nonhuman actors; multispecies ethnography: ethnographic work that takes seriously the roles of nonhuman species in shaping social life — Anna Tsing, The Mushroom at the End of the World (matsutake mushrooms and precarious capitalism)); nature-culture and environmental justice (Bruno Latour's nature-culture distinction: modernity was built on the purification of nature and culture into separate domains — We Have Never Been Modern; reality is always hybrid: science and politics are never fully separate; assemblages and socio-natural systems: watersheds, urban ecologies, disease ecologies as socio-natural co-productions; political ecology: unequal access to and control over natural resources — power, nature, and identity; environmental justice: the fair treatment and meaningful involvement of all people in environmental decision-making — Bullard's Dumping in Dixie; sacrifice zones: communities that bear disproportionate environmental costs for others' benefit; climate justice: who is responsible for and most affected by climate change? Historical emissions, current vulnerability, and adaptive capacity; Anna Tsing's frictions: global connections always require and produce unexpected forms of difference and friction; plantationocene (Tsing, Haraway, Mitman): the plantation as a model of monoculture, racial capitalism, and ecological devastation that persists into the present)",
            successCriteria: "Environmental humanities study session completed (ecocriticism defined with first/second/third waves, posthumanism distinguished from humanism, more-than-human theory explained, one key thinker and their core argument identified, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "leaf.circle.fill",
            task: "Write my environmental humanities paper — this may include: an ecocritical analysis of a text (select a literary or cultural text: a novel, film, documentary, nature essay, or public environmental campaign; identify and describe the landscape or nonhuman nature represented: what environments appear? How are they described — wild or domesticated, pristine or contaminated, resource or ecosystem?; apply pastoral critique (Raymond Williams or Leo Marx): does the text romanticize or idealize nature? Does it conceal human labor or social relations in the landscape?; apply a justice lens: whose relationship to the land is centered? Who is absent? Whose environmental knowledge is valued?; apply Rob Nixon's slow violence: does the text represent visible, spectacular environmental events or gradual, accumulating harm? What are the representational challenges of depicting slow violence?; apply material ecocriticism: how do nonhuman things — soil, water, weather, animals — act on and shape the human characters? Are nonhumans depicted as passive background or active agents?; evaluate the political stakes of the text: does it reinforce or challenge dominant environmental ideologies (market environmentalism, wilderness conservation, climate denial, technofix)?; propose one alternative reading that centers a nonhuman or marginalized human perspective the text suppresses), a posthumanist or more-than-human analysis (select one human practice or social domain: companion animal keeping, urban agriculture, industrial meat production, rewilding, biotechnology, precision agriculture, or synthetic biology; describe the human-nonhuman entanglement at the center of your case: which nonhuman actors (animals, microbes, plants, machines, chemicals) are involved?; apply Donna Haraway's companion species or sympoiesis framework: how are humans and nonhumans making each other in this case?; apply Jane Bennett's vibrant matter: what forms of nonhuman agency or thing-power are operative? How do the nonhuman materials push back, exceed, or redirect human intention?; apply assemblage theory: describe the case as a temporary, unstable assemblage of heterogeneous elements — human, nonhuman, institutional, discursive; decenter the human: what would change if you told this story from the nonhuman's perspective — the chicken's, the microbe's, the seed's?; discuss one ethical implication: what obligations or responsibilities toward nonhuman actors does your analysis generate?; evaluate one critique of posthumanism: does decentering the human risk losing the ability to hold specific powerful actors accountable for environmental harm?), or an environmental justice analysis (select a site of environmental injustice: a sacrifice zone (Cancer Alley, Louisiana; fenceline communities near petrochemical plants); a climate displacement case (Pacific island nations; Bangladesh delta communities; Louisiana's Isle de Jean Charles); a food justice issue (food deserts in U.S. cities; pesticide exposure among farmworkers; commodity agriculture and food sovereignty); or a water justice case (Flint, Michigan; Dakota Access Pipeline and Standing Rock; urban-rural water access in the Global South); describe the place and community: who lives there, what is the environmental hazard, and how long has the problem existed?; apply political ecology: trace the power relations — who controls land, resources, regulatory processes, and media narratives in this case?; apply slow violence (Rob Nixon): how does the gradual, cumulative nature of the harm make it harder to recognize, represent, and redress?; apply environmental justice principles: how does race, class, or indigeneity structure who bears the environmental burden?; analyze one representational challenge: how does the affected community make its suffering visible? What genres, media, or forms of testimony are available to them?; propose one structural and one representational intervention: what policy change would address the injustice, and how could storytelling, art, or documentation amplify the community's claim?)",
            successCriteria: "Environmental humanities paper completed (ecocriticism, posthumanism, or environmental justice framework applied to a specific text, practice, or place; at least one nonhuman actor or more-than-human relationship analyzed; and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // africanstudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "globe.africa.fill",
            task: "Study African studies for my exam — review Pan-Africanism, Negritude, African political economy, and postcolonial theory in Africa: Pan-Africanism and Negritude (Pan-Africanism: a political and cultural movement advocating solidarity and unity of Africans and people of African descent globally; origins: late 19th–early 20th century — Henry Sylvester Williams (First Pan-African Conference, London, 1900); W.E.B. Du Bois: Pan-African congresses (1919–1945), the concept of the color line, double consciousness; Marcus Garvey and UNIA: Back-to-Africa movement, Black Star Line, Black nationalism; Kwame Nkrumah: Ghana's independence (1957) — first sub-Saharan African country; African Union vision, continental unity, anti-imperialism; pan-African socialism: Nkrumah's consciencism, Julius Nyerere's Ujamaa (African socialism), Sékou Touré; Negritude movement: 1930s–1940s, Francophone African and Caribbean intellectuals — Aimé Césaire (Martinique), Léopold Sédar Senghor (Senegal), Léon-Gontran Damas (French Guiana); response to French colonial assimilation policy — assertion of African cultural identity, dignity, and aesthetic values; Senghor's Negritude: African civilization values — communality, emotion, rhythm, vitality; Frantz Fanon's critique of Negritude: The Wretched of the Earth (1961) — national consciousness must move beyond racial identity to a new humanism; "the pitfalls of national consciousness" — warning against native bourgeoisie replacing colonial elites without transforming structures; decolonization wave: 1957–1975 — independence across sub-Saharan Africa; neocolonialism (Nkrumah): political independence without economic independence — continued control through trade, debt, and multinational corporations); African political economy and development (African political economy: how power, production, distribution, and accumulation shape and are shaped by African political institutions; colonial political economy: exploitation of raw materials, cash crop agriculture, forced labor, migrant labor systems, racial classification; Walter Rodney, How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972): European colonialism systematically drained Africa's resources and arrested its economic development; dependency theory applied to Africa: Africa's integration into the world economy on unequal terms perpetuates underdevelopment; structural adjustment programs (SAPs): IMF/World Bank conditions in 1980s–90s — privatization, trade liberalization, austerity, removal of subsidies; critiques: worsened inequality, destroyed public services, failed to generate growth; African economic recovery: growth in 2000s–2010s driven by commodity prices, debt relief (HIPC Initiative), and some governance improvement; China-Africa relations: infrastructure investment, trade, no-strings-attached aid vs conditionality debate; natural resource curse: paradox of plenty — oil and mineral wealth associated with conflict, corruption, weak institutions; land tenure: customary vs statutory rights, land grabs by foreign investors, women's land rights; regional integration: African Union (AU), African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA, 2021)); decolonization thought and postcolonial Africa (Frantz Fanon: The Wretched of the Earth — violence as a cleansing force, the pitfalls of national consciousness, psychiatry and colonialism; Black Skin, White Masks — colonial psychology, the colonized as Other; Chinua Achebe: Things Fall Apart — colonialism's destruction of Igbo society, representation of Africa from within; African literary tradition: Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o — Decolonising the Mind: the importance of writing in African languages; Afrocentrism (Molefi Asante): centering African cultural values and history in scholarship; Ubuntu: "I am because we are" — African philosophy of communal humanity and ethics; governance and democracy in Africa: neopatrimonialism, hybrid regimes, electoral authoritarianism, democratic backsliding — Freedom House and Polity IV data; African civil society: social movements, women's organizations, religious institutions as political actors)",
            successCriteria: "African studies study session completed (Pan-Africanism defined with one key figure and period, Negritude explained with one central concept, Walter Rodney's underdevelopment thesis summarized, one postcolonial African thinker identified with their argument, and exam notes saved)",
