orse, and why?; apply one alternative framework: developmental state theory (Korea/Taiwan applied to Africa), institutionalism (Acemoglu and Robinson — inclusive vs extractive institutions), or African socialism; critique: what does your framework explain well, and what does it miss?; propose one policy intervention grounded in your analysis: who are the key stakeholders, what are the political constraints, and what would success look like?), or an African literary and cultural studies paper (select one African literary work or cultural text: Things Fall Apart (Achebe), Season of Migration to the North (Salih), Petals of Blood (Ngũgĩ), Purple Hibiscus (Adichie), Half of a Yellow Sun (Adichie), or a major film from African cinema; describe the historical and political context in which the text was produced: colonialism, independence, civil war, dictatorship, contemporary globalization?; apply postcolonial theory: how does the text engage with colonial legacies, identity, hybridity, or resistance? (Bhabha's ambivalence and mimicry; Spivak's subaltern; Fanon's decolonization); apply Ngũgĩ's language politics: what does it mean that African writers must often write in European languages? How does the text negotiate this?; analyze at least one central character's experience of colonial or postcolonial identity; identify one form of resistance — cultural, political, or psychological — represented in the text; evaluate what the text reveals about African experiences that Western representations typically obscure; discuss one contemporary resonance: how does the text speak to issues in Africa or the African diaspora today?)",
