        // sciencetechnologystudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "microscope",
            task: "Study science and technology studies (STS) for my exam — review actor-network theory, social construction of science and technology, Latour, Haraway, and sociology of scientific knowledge: sociology of scientific knowledge and social construction (sociology of scientific knowledge (SSK): the study of how social factors shape the content of scientific knowledge — Edinburgh school (Strong Programme); symmetry principle (Bloor): the same types of cause should explain both true and false scientific beliefs — sociology should not give science a free pass; laboratory studies: Latour and Woolgar, Laboratory Life (1979) — scientific facts are constructed through material and social practices in the lab; inscription devices: instruments that transform phenomena into literary inscriptions (graphs, tables, figures) — the fact is the inscription, not the thing itself; black box: when a fact or technology is stabilized, the process of its construction is hidden — opening the black box reveals the controversies; social construction of technology (SCOT — Pinch and Bijker): technologies are not determined by their technical properties but by social groups' interpretations; interpretive flexibility: different social groups interpret the same artifact differently; closure: through rhetorical closure or redefinition of the problem, one interpretation stabilizes; relevant social groups: the groups whose interests and interpretations shape technological design; technological determinism vs social shaping: determinism says technology drives social change; social shaping says social forces drive technological design); actor-network theory (ANT: Bruno Latour and Michel Callon — a framework for understanding how social order is produced through networks of human and nonhuman actors; actors (actants): any entity — human, nonhuman, material, conceptual — that acts within a network; translation: the process by which actors define each other, create intermediaries, and enroll allies; obligatory passage point (OPP): a bottleneck that all actors must pass through — created by the central actor through inscription; inscription: encoding strategies, interests, and identities into durable artifacts (documents, machines, standards, metrics); immutable mobiles: objects that can travel across networks while retaining their form — maps, scientific papers, standards; Latour's We Have Never Been Modern: modernity rests on the impossible separation of nature and society — ANT reveals the hybrids that modernity tries to deny; Michel Callon's scallops: translation involves enrolling nonhuman actors — scallops, fishermen, scientists — into a network; controversies: ANT prefers to study science in the making — before black boxes are closed); Haraway, feminist STS, and contemporary debates (Donna Haraway, Situated Knowledge (1988): the view from nowhere is an illusion — all knowledge is embodied, positioned, and partial; the god trick: the fantasy of a neutral, universal view that denies its own situatedness; feminist objectivity: strong objectivity requires taking seriously the perspectives of marginalized groups; Haraway's Cyborg Manifesto (1985): the boundary between human, animal, and machine is historically produced and politically contested; Haraway's Staying with the Trouble: sympoiesis — making-together across species; Chthulucene; STS and race: Ruha Benjamin, Race After Technology — algorithms and medical technologies encode racial bias; Safiya Umoja Noble, Algorithms of Oppression — Google search as racist infrastructure; co-production (Jasanoff): scientific knowledge and social order are co-produced — neither precedes the other; boundary work (Gieryn): scientists police the boundary between science and non-science strategically; expertise and lay knowledge: Brian Wynne — lay publics have legitimate knowledge that expert framings exclude)",
            successCriteria: "STS study session completed (SSK/Strong Programme defined, SCOT's interpretive flexibility and closure explained, ANT and actor-network described with translation and obligatory passage point, Haraway's situated knowledge explained, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "microscope",
            task: "Write my science and technology studies paper — this may include: a social construction of technology (SCOT) analysis (select one technology: the bicycle, the refrigerator, the internet, the algorithm, CRISPR gene editing, the nuclear power plant, or the smartphone; describe the artifact as it currently exists — stabilized, black-boxed; open the black box: apply SCOT to trace the artifact's development history; identify the relevant social groups: who were the different parties with an interest in the artifact's design (engineers, manufacturers, users, regulators, social movements)?; describe the interpretive flexibility: how did different social groups define the artifact and its problems differently?; trace the closure mechanism: how did interpretive flexibility close? Through rhetorical closure (one design won the argument), redefinition of the problem, or market selection?; apply the politics of artifacts (Langdon Winner): does the artifact embody values, power relations, or social arrangements? Does it have politics?; evaluate: what were the alternatives not built? What interests shaped the design that was stabilized? Who was excluded from the relevant social groups and how did this shape the outcome?), an actor-network theory (ANT) analysis (select one scientific controversy or technological controversy: Pasteur vs spontaneous generation, the HIV/AIDS debate, climate science and climate denial, GMO controversies, facial recognition technology, COVID-19 mRNA vaccines, or COVID-19 lab-leak vs natural origin debate; describe the network: who are the actants (human and nonhuman) enrolled in this controversy?; apply translation: how did the central actors define the problem, create an obligatory passage point, and enroll allies?; identify immutable mobiles: what inscriptions — data, standards, publications, artifacts — are mobilized across the network?; trace the moment of black-boxing: if the controversy resolved, how did one position become a settled fact? What was delegated to nonhuman actors (instruments, protocols, standards)?; apply Latour's critique of purification: how did both sides attempt to separate nature (facts) from society (politics)?; apply ANT symmetrically: treat both sides of the controversy with the same explanatory tools; evaluate: what does ANT reveal that a straightforward internalist account (scientific facts speak for themselves) would miss?; discuss one critique of ANT: does it adequately address power, capitalism, or the materiality of race and gender?), or a feminist STS and algorithmic bias analysis (select one algorithmic system or medical technology: facial recognition, predictive policing, credit scoring, hiring algorithms, diagnostic AI, or maternal mortality risk scores; describe the system: what does it do, who developed it, and who is subject to it?; apply Haraway's situated knowledge: what "view from where" is encoded in the system's training data and design choices? Whose knowledge is treated as universal?; apply Ruha Benjamin's race after technology: how does the system encode or amplify racial disparities? What is the "New Jim Code" — the use of technology to reinforce racial hierarchy while appearing neutral?; apply co-production: how did the social context in which the technology was built shape what counts as a good prediction, an acceptable error rate, and whose errors matter?; identify who bears the costs of algorithmic errors: false positives vs false negatives distributed across which populations?; apply feminist objectivity: what would strong objectivity require in the design or regulation of this system?; propose one structural intervention: what changes to the development process, governance regime, or accountability mechanisms would address the bias you identified?)",
            successCriteria: "STS paper completed (SCOT, ANT, or feminist STS framework applied to a specific technology or scientific controversy; at least one key concept (interpretive flexibility, translation, obligatory passage point, situated knowledge) applied to the case; and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // mediatheory
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "antenna.radiowaves.left.and.right",
            task: "Study media theory for my exam — review McLuhan, Baudrillard, Hall, Frankfurt School, Debord, and the political economy of media: McLuhan and media ecology (Marshall McLuhan: media ecology — the study of media environments and how communication technologies shape human perception and culture; The Gutenberg Galaxy (1962): the printing press produced individualism, nationalism, and linear, sequential thinking — the 'typographic man'; Understanding Media (1964): 'the medium is the message' — the form of a medium shapes how content is understood, independent of content itself; hot vs cool media: hot media (radio, film) have high definition, require little participation; cool media (telephone, TV) have low definition, require high participation; global village: electronic media collapse space and time, creating a retribalized world of simultaneous experience; Harold Innis: the bias of communication — time-biased media (stone tablets, parchment) build durable empires; space-biased media (papyrus, newspapers) build spatially extended empires; Neil Postman: Amusing Ourselves to Death — television transforms public discourse into entertainment; Technopoly — the surrender of culture to technology; media ecology as a field: studies media as environments that shape what people know, think, and value; Friedrich Kittler: Gramophone, Film, Typewriter — media determine thought; discourse networks 1800/1900; inscription machines that produce the subject); Baudrillard and simulation theory (Jean Baudrillard: Simulacra and Simulation (1981) — in postmodern consumer society, representations precede and determine reality; the real is replaced by the hyperreal; orders of simulacra: first order (faithful copy of reality — Renaissance icon); second order (perversion of reality — industrial mass production); third order (simulation — pure simulacrum with no original referent); fourth order (pure simulation); hyperreality: a condition where simulations substitute for the real — Disneyland exists to make America appear real; the Gulf War Did Not Take Place (1991): media spectacle replaced the real event; the map precedes the territory (Borges): in postmodernism, the map (representation) becomes more real than the territory (reality); Guy Debord and the situationists: The Society of the Spectacle (1967) — modern life is the accumulation of spectacles; the spectacle is capital accumulated to the point where it becomes image; détournement: appropriation and subversion of spectacular images; psychogeography: the effect of urban environments on emotional behavior); Stuart Hall and the Frankfurt School (Stuart Hall, Encoding/Decoding (1980): meaning is not simply transmitted from sender to receiver — it is encoded by the producer and decoded by the reader; dominant-hegemonic reading: reader decodes as intended; negotiated reading: reader accepts dominant framework but applies it to their context; oppositional reading: reader decodes against the grain; representation: how media construct the world, not reflect it; the Frankfurt School: Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944) — the culture industry; mass culture produces standardized products that promote conformity and passive consumption; pseudo-individuality: products appear individualized but are structurally identical; autonomous art resists commodification; Walter Benjamin, The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction (1935): the aura of the original is destroyed by reproduction; reproduction enables politicization of aesthetics; political economy of media: Noam Chomsky and Edward Herman, Manufacturing Consent (1988) — propaganda model; five filters that shape media output: ownership, advertising, sourcing, flak, anti-communism/fear ideology; Herman and Chomsky: the media are not a fourth estate but a system for manufacturing public consent for elite interests; platform studies and digital media: Tarleton Gillespie on the politics of platforms; Nick Couldry: media as practice; Jose van Dijck: the culture of connectivity; datafication and surveillance capitalism)",
            successCriteria: "Media theory study session completed (McLuhan's medium is the message and global village explained, Baudrillard's simulacra and hyperreality described with one example, Hall's encoding/decoding positions named, Frankfurt School culture industry summarized, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "antenna.radiowaves.left.and.right",
            task: "Write my media theory paper — this may include: a media ecology or McLuhan analysis (select one communication technology: the printing press, the telegraph, radio, television, the internet, the smartphone, or social media algorithms; apply McLuhan's medium is the message: how does the form of this medium shape thought, perception, and social organization, independent of its content?; apply hot vs cool media classification: what level of definition does the medium have, and what participation does it demand from users?; apply Innis's bias of communication: is this medium time-biased (building durable communities across time) or space-biased (enabling spatially extended control)?; apply Postman's critique: how has this medium reshaped public discourse, knowledge, and the relationship between entertainment and information?; apply Kittler's media materialism: how does this medium's technical infrastructure produce or transform the human subject?; trace one concrete social or cultural shift directly attributable to the medium's form, not its content; evaluate one critique of media ecology: does it overemphasize technological determinism and underestimate human agency or social structures?; identify a contemporary application: how does media ecology illuminate one aspect of social media, streaming, or AI-generated content?), a Baudrillard and simulation analysis (select one cultural phenomenon: Disneyland, reality television, social media influencer culture, 24-hour cable news, luxury brand advertising, video game environments, or AI-generated synthetic media; describe the phenomenon: what real event, experience, or identity does it purport to represent?; apply Baudrillard's orders of simulacra: which order does this phenomenon occupy? Has the copy become more real than the original?; identify the hyperreal: what specific aspect of the phenomenon has replaced the real with the simulation?; apply Debord's society of the spectacle: how is the phenomenon an expression of spectacular capitalism?; trace the commodification: what is being commodified, and how does the spectacle conceal the underlying economic and political relations?; evaluate the political implications: does the hyperreal foreclose political critique (Baudrillard) or produce possibilities for détournement and resistance (Debord)?; critique: what does Baudrillard's framework miss about agency, material inequality, or genuine cultural production?), or a political economy of media / encoding-decoding analysis (select one media text: a prime-time TV news broadcast, a Netflix documentary, a viral social media campaign, a music video, a video game, or a streaming platform's recommendation algorithm; apply Hall's encoding/decoding: how is meaning encoded by the producer? Identify the dominant ideology embedded in formal and technical codes (camera work, framing, language, sound) and broader cultural codes (assumptions about race, gender, class, nation); test three readings: what would the dominant, negotiated, and oppositional readings look like for this text?; apply Herman and Chomsky's propaganda model: which of the five filters (ownership, advertising, sourcing, flak, fear ideology) shaped the text's production?; apply platform studies to a digital text: whose interests does the platform's architecture serve? How do affordances, visibility, and algorithmic curation shape what is represented?; apply datafication and surveillance capitalism: how does the platform extract value from user data and behavior?; propose one intervention: what media literacy practice, regulatory reform, or alternative media form would address the power relations you identified?)",
            successCriteria: "Media theory paper completed (McLuhan/media ecology, Baudrillard/simulation, or Hall/political economy of media framework applied to a specific medium or text; at least one key concept applied concretely; and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // theaterstudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "theatermasks",
            task: "Study theater studies for my exam — review Brecht, Artaud, Grotowski, Schechner, dramatic theory, and theater history: dramatic theory and performance (Aristotle's Poetics (c. 335 BCE): the foundational text of Western dramatic theory; mimesis: drama as imitation of human action; six elements of tragedy: plot (mythos), character (ethos), thought (dianoia), diction (lexis), song (melos), spectacle (opsis); hamartia: the fatal flaw or error of judgment; anagnorisis: recognition or revelation; peripeteia: reversal of fortune; catharsis: the emotional purging experienced by the audience; the unities of time, place, and action (later codified by Renaissance critics); Bertolt Brecht (1898–1956): epic theater vs dramatic theater; Verfremdungseffekt (V-Effekt or alienation effect): theatrical techniques that prevent emotional identification and encourage critical thinking — direct address, signs and banners, episodic structure, visible scene changes, actors commenting on their characters; Gestus: the social attitude expressed through gesture, language, and staging; didactic theater: theater as a tool for political education and consciousness-raising; The Three-penny Opera, Mother Courage and Her Children, The Caucasian Chalk Circle; Antonin Artaud (1896–1948): Theater and Its Double (1938) — the theater of cruelty; rejection of text as primary: theater should speak through gesture, sound, light, and space rather than dialogue; the double: theater is a double of life — dangerous and transformative; plague metaphor: theater should act on audiences like the plague — releasing hidden forces; influence on environmental theater, physical theater, and postdramatic theater; Constantin Stanislavski (1863–1938): the system — psychological realism; emotional memory: accessing personal experience to fuel performance; given circumstances, the magic if, through-line of action, units and objectives; Lee Strasberg's Method acting: American adaptation emphasizing emotional recall; acting from the inside out); Grotowski, Schechner, and contemporary approaches (Jerzy Grotowski (1933–1999): Towards a Poor Theater (1968) — stripping theater to its essential relationship between actor and spectator; poor theater: elimination of scenery, costumes, makeup, and technical effects — pure acting; via negativa: the actor's process of eliminating blocks to authentic expression; plasticity of voice and body; paratheatrical work: rituals and laboratory investigations; Richard Schechner (b. 1934): performance studies and environmental theater; environmental theater: staging in nontraditional spaces — the spectator-performer relationship is environmental and improvisational; the performance studies field: drawing on anthropology, ritual, and performance as a broad category of human behavior; the performance continuum: from ritual to art to everyday life; liminality (Victor Turner): performances occupy liminal space — between social roles and structures; postdramatic theater (Hans-Thies Lehmann): theater after Brecht — postdramatic theater challenges narrative, character, and dramatic structure; site-specific performance, durational work, immersive theater; theater history: Greek theater (fifth century BCE) — City Dionysia, chorus, masks, orchestra, skene; medieval mystery and morality plays; Elizabethan theater — the Globe, the bare stage, soliloquy, the unities questioned; the well-made play (Scribe) vs realism (Ibsen, Chekhov); Expressionism (Strindberg, Kaiser), Dada, Surrealism; the Living Theater, the Open Theater, performance art; contemporary forms: verbatim theater, physical theater, devised theater)",
            successCriteria: "Theater studies study session completed (Aristotle's catharsis and hamartia defined, Brecht's Verfremdungseffekt explained with one technique, Artaud's theater of cruelty described, Grotowski's poor theater summarized, and exam notes saved)",
