        // developmentstudies
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "globe.americas.fill",
            task: "Study international development for my exam — review capabilities approach (Sen/Nussbaum), post-development (Escobar), modernization theory (Rostow), HDI, MDGs/SDGs, and the aid effectiveness debate: theories of development (modernization theory: W.W. Rostow, The Stages of Economic Growth (1960) — traditional society → preconditions for take-off → take-off → drive to maturity → mass consumption; development as economic growth through stages; ideology: anti-communist, pro-Western; critique: ethnocentric, linear, ignores internal structures; dependency theory: André Gunder Frank, Walter Rodney — development and underdevelopment are two sides of the same coin; core countries extract surplus from periphery; structural conditions, not culture, explain underdevelopment; ECLA/CEPAL: Raúl Prebisch — terms of trade deteriorate for commodity exporters; import substitution industrialization (ISI) as a response; world systems theory (Wallerstein): core, semi-periphery, periphery — a capitalist world-system that perpetuates unequal exchange; human development approach: Amartya Sen, Development as Freedom (1999) — development is freedom; capability approach: the real freedoms (capabilities) that people have to achieve functionings they value; functionings: things a person can do or be (being nourished, being educated, participating in political life); capabilities: real opportunities available (not just formal rights); five instrumental freedoms: political freedoms, economic facilities, social opportunities, transparency guarantees, protective security; critique of GDP as development measure: GDP ignores distribution, freedom, and human flourishing; Martha Nussbaum's central capabilities list: life, bodily health, bodily integrity, senses/imagination/thought, emotions, practical reason, affiliation, other species, play, control over one's political and material environment); post-development and the HDI (Arturo Escobar, Encountering Development (1995): development is a discourse produced in the West to govern the Third World; development creates poverty as its subject; the deconstruction of development: development is a historical construction, not a natural process; post-development alternatives: local knowledge, social movements, buen vivir, solidarity economy; Wolfgang Sachs, The Development Dictionary — key concepts of development (planning, poverty, environment, participation) critiqued as hegemonic; human development index (HDI): UNDP's annual measure of human development — composite of life expectancy, education (mean years of schooling + expected years), and GNI per capita; introduced in 1990 Human Development Report by Mahbub ul Haq and Amartya Sen; limitations: does not capture inequality, gender, sustainability, or freedom; inequality-adjusted HDI (IHDI), Gender Development Index (GDI), Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI); Millennium Development Goals (MDGs, 2000–2015): 8 goals — eradicate extreme poverty, achieve universal primary education, promote gender equality, reduce child mortality, improve maternal health, combat HIV/AIDS/malaria, ensure environmental sustainability, develop a global partnership; successes and shortfalls; Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs, 2015–2030): 17 goals, 169 targets; leave no one behind; SDGs vs MDGs: universality (apply to all countries), integration, and emphasis on structural transformation vs technical fixes); aid effectiveness and the Sachs-Easterly debate (Jeffrey Sachs, The End of Poverty (2005): the big push — massive coordinated aid can end extreme poverty; poverty trap: poor countries lack the capital to invest in the escape from poverty; clinical economics: diagnose and prescribe targeted interventions; Millennium Villages Project; William Easterly, The White Man's Burden (2006): planned aid is ineffective; top-down vs bottom-up: planners impose blueprints; searchers find local solutions; incentive problem: aid agencies are not accountable to beneficiaries; Dambisa Moyo, Dead Aid (2009): aid is ineffective and perpetuates dependency — calls for trade, foreign investment, and bond markets; Esther Duflo and Abhijit Banerjee (Poor Economics, 2011): randomized control trials (RCTs) to test aid interventions; evidence-based development; micro-level interventions; Paris Declaration (2005) on aid effectiveness: ownership, alignment, harmonization, managing for results, mutual accountability; Global Fund, GAVI, PEPFAR as models of vertical funding)",
            successCriteria: "Development studies study session completed (Rostow's stages of growth named, Sen's capabilities approach explained with one example, Escobar's post-development critique summarized, HDI components listed, and Sachs vs Easterly debate described with exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "globe.americas.fill",
            task: "Write my international development paper — this may include: a capabilities approach analysis (select one development challenge: female education in sub-Saharan Africa, food insecurity and child malnutrition, urban informal labor markets, refugee displacement, or access to healthcare in low-income countries; describe the challenge in material terms: who is affected, where, at what scale, and what are the underlying structural causes?; apply Amartya Sen's capability approach: what capabilities — real freedoms to achieve valued functionings — are being denied? Use Sen's five instrumental freedoms as a framework; apply Martha Nussbaum's central capabilities list: which of the 10 central capabilities are most directly affected? What would a minimally just situation require?; critique GDP-based framings: how does a traditional growth-based account of the challenge miss what the capabilities approach reveals?; apply the HDI: what do HDI scores (or IHDI/MPI) reveal about the challenge that GDP per capita does not?; evaluate one policy intervention through the capabilities lens: does it expand real freedoms and agency, or does it deliver services without addressing structural barriers to capability development?; identify one tension: Sen's approach is non-prescriptive about which capabilities to prioritize — how does Nussbaum's list address this, and what are the concerns about cultural imperialism or paternalism?), a post-development and alternatives analysis (select one development program or concept: a structural adjustment program, a Millennium Village, a conditional cash transfer program (CCT), microfinance (Grameen Bank), a large infrastructure project, or a USAID development initiative; describe the program: what are its goals, design, implementing agencies, and target population?; apply Escobar's post-development critique: what development discourse does the program instantiate? What assumptions about poverty, progress, modernity, and the poor are embedded in its design?; apply Sachs's big push critique: does the program address poverty traps and coordination failures, or is it too narrow?; apply Easterly's counter-critique: does the program rely on top-down planning rather than local knowledge and incentives? Who is accountable to whom?; evaluate the empirical evidence: what do outcome evaluations (including RCTs, if available) say about impact?; apply a post-development alternative: is there a buen vivir, solidarity economy, or local social movement approach that offers a different development logic?; evaluate tradeoffs: is a post-development critique practically useful, or does it leave affected people without solutions?), or a comparative development policy analysis (select two countries at similar income levels but with different development trajectories — e.g. South Korea vs Ghana, Botswana vs Zimbabwe, Brazil vs Colombia, Bangladesh vs Pakistan, or Vietnam vs Cambodia; describe both countries' development trajectories over the past 30 years using HDI, poverty headcount, infant mortality, and one additional indicator; apply modernization theory: does the developmental divergence support or undermine Rostow's stage framework?; apply dependency/world systems theory: how did each country's integration into the global economy shape its development trajectory?; apply the developmental state model (Amsden, Evans): did one country's state capacity, industrial policy, or strategic coupling explain its performance?; apply Sen's capabilities approach: which country more effectively expanded real freedoms, and why?; evaluate aid and external finance: how did bilateral aid, concessional loans, FDI, and remittances differ between the two countries?; draw one policy lesson: what institutional, political, or social factor seems most explanatory?)",
