ncies Act, NLRA)):  on-screen and on-set labor law (SAG-AFTRA (Screen Actors Guild–American Federation of Television and Radio Artists): union representing actors, singers, voice actors, and media performers; collective bargaining agreements (CBA) with major studios and streamers; minimum wages, residuals, working conditions, health and pension benefits; residuals: payments to actors when a film/TV show is re-distributed or replayed — streaming residuals are a major contemporary issue; SAG-AFTRA strike (2023): streaming residuals, AI protections, use of likeness; WGA (Writers Guild of America): represents TV/film writers; separation of rights: writer retains certain rights (theatrical, sequel, novelization); minimum basic agreement (MBA); spec scripts, assignment writing, WGA credits determination; DGA (Directors Guild of America): above-the-line talent; right of publicity: the right of individuals to control the commercial use of their name, image, likeness (NIL); state law: California Civil Code § 3344, New York Civil Rights Law §§ 50–51; post-mortem right of publicity (California: 70 years); NIL (name, image, likeness) rights for college athletes: NCAA v Alston (2021), state NIL laws; Midler v Ford Motor Co. (1988): sound-alike voice protected; White v Samsung (1992): Vanna White — celebrity persona protected beyond literal likeness); entertainment deal structures and IP (film development and finance: option agreement — option to purchase underlying IP rights (book, article, screenplay) for a set period; screenplay development: WGA-covered or non-covered; production agreement; studio vs independent film finance; co-production treaties; talent agreements: actor/director agreements — pay-or-play clause (studio pays talent whether or not production proceeds), first-class airfare, hotel, billing, credit contractual provisions; product placement agreements; location agreements; intellectual property in entertainment: copyright in screenplays, music, characters, and visual elements; trademark protection for franchise titles; character merchandising — licensing use of characters in consumer products; entertainment litigation: music copyright infringement (Blurred Lines — Williams v Gaye (9th Cir. 2018)), sampling without license (Grand Upright v Warner, 1991), likeness rights, contract disputes)",
