        // constitutionaltheory
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "book.closed.fill",
            task: "Study constitutional theory and interpretation for my exam — review originalism, living constitutionalism, textualism, purposivism, departmentalism, popular constitutionalism, and Dworkin's law as integrity: interpretive methodologies (originalism: seeks the original public meaning (OPM) or original intent (OI) of the Constitution at the time of framing/ratification; OPM (Justice Scalia, Justice Thomas) vs OI (traditional originalism) — OPM looks to how reasonable ratifiers would have understood the text; New Originalism: Solum, Barnett, Whittington — distinguish between constitutional interpretation (finding meaning) and constitutional construction (applying meaning to cases not determined by meaning alone); originalism's justifications: rule of law, democratic legitimacy, constrains judges; critique of originalism: history is contested, original meaning is uncertain, original public meaning may be racist/exclusionary, constitutional provisions don't have a single fixed meaning; living constitutionalism (David Strauss, Jack Balkin): the Constitution's meaning evolves with changing social conditions; common law constitutionalism (Strauss) — constitutional doctrine develops incrementally through precedent, like common law; living constitutionalism's justifications: allows Constitution to remain relevant, reflects moral progress; critique: courts become unelected policy-makers; textualism: interpretation that focuses tightly on the text as written, as understood in ordinary usage; distinguishes textual meaning from legislative intent or purpose; Scalia's textualism vs purposivism: purpose of statute is relevant vs. text is authoritative; purposivism (Hart & Sacks): interpretation should advance the statute's underlying purpose, determined from statutory text and legislative history; constitutional purposivism: Dworkin's law as integrity: law as integrity (Ronald Dworkin): law is not just rules but principles; judges must find the interpretation that both fits prior law and shows it in the best moral light; law is a moral practice — integrity requires coherence across all legal materials; Dworkin vs Hart (positivism): does law include principles beyond rules?; Dworkin vs originalism: interpreters should ask what the text was meant to achieve, not the specific applications framers had in mind; constitutional construction zone (Solum/Barnett): when text is ambiguous or vague, construction fills the gap; constitutional construction is not interpretation — it is a separate practice); structural and intratextualist arguments (structural interpretation (Charles Black): infer constitutional meaning from the structure of government the Constitution creates; McCulloch v Maryland — implied powers from constitutional structure; intratextualism (Akhil Amar): use the Constitution to interpret itself — constitutional provisions illuminate each other; Amar's amendments theory — the 14th Amendment transforms how we read the original Constitution; departmentalism vs judicial supremacy: Cooper v Aaron (1958) — SCOTUS asserted judicial supremacy in constitutional interpretation; departmentalism: each branch has independent authority to interpret the Constitution; Lincoln's rejection of Dred Scott; popular constitutionalism (Larry Kramer, Mark Tushnet): the people, not courts, are the primary constitutional interpreters; judicial supremacy is a historically contingent practice; tushnet's proposal: abolish judicial review; judicial minimalism (Cass Sunstein): courts should decide cases on narrow grounds — avoid broad constitutional rules; judicial maximalism: broad rules; underenforcement of constitutional norms (Lawrence Sager): some constitutional provisions are not fully judicially enforceable; political question doctrine as underenforcement)",
            successCriteria: "Constitutional theory study session completed (originalism's OPM vs OI distinguished, living constitutionalism's justifications described, Dworkin's law as integrity explained, departmentalism vs judicial supremacy distinguished, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "book.closed.fill",
            task: "Write my constitutional theory paper — this may include: an interpretive methodology comparison (select one debate: originalism vs living constitutionalism — choose a constitutional provision (the 8th Amendment's cruel and unusual punishments clause, the 14th Amendment's equal protection clause, the 2nd Amendment's right to keep and bear arms, or the 1st Amendment's free speech guarantee); apply originalist analysis: what was the original public meaning of the provision at the time of ratification? What sources (ratification debates, dictionaries, contemporaneous usage) are most authoritative?; apply living constitutionalist analysis (Strauss's common law method): how has constitutional doctrine evolved? Does precedent reflect moral progress or social change that justifies departing from original meaning?; identify where the methodologies converge and diverge on the specific provision; evaluate the epistemological critique of originalism: is original public meaning determinable from available historical evidence?; evaluate the democratic legitimacy critique of living constitutionalism: does evolutionary interpretation vest too much policy discretion in unelected judges?; defend one methodology as superior for this provision and for constitutional interpretation generally, textualism vs purposivism in constitutional interpretation — apply to the Commerce Clause, the Necessary and Proper Clause, or the 14th Amendment Section 5 enforcement power; what does the text say? What does a purposivist ask? Do they reach different results on a specific case (NFIB v Sebelius, City of Boerne v Flores, Gonzales v Raich)?; evaluate Dworkin's integrity critique of both — does it offer a third way?), a Dworkin vs positivism or originalism paper (identify Dworkin's core critique of Hart's positivism: legal positivism fails to account for principles; explain Dworkin's law as integrity: coherence, fit, and moral justification as dimensions of legal interpretation; apply Dworkin's framework to a constitutional hard case — Bush v Gore, Obergefell v Hodges, Dobbs v Jackson Women's Health, or a structural constitutional question; analyze: what interpretation best fits prior constitutional materials AND shows those materials in the best moral light?; evaluate the originalist critique of Dworkin: does asking what the Constitution was meant to achieve produce judicial activism? Is moral reading of the Constitution compatible with democratic self-governance?; evaluate the positivist critique: does Dworkin's framework provide sufficient constraint on judicial discretion?; defend or critique Dworkin's claim that there is always a right answer in hard constitutional cases), or a departmentalism or popular constitutionalism paper (select one: departmentalism — evaluate whether Congress, the President, or state governments have independent authority to interpret the Constitution; apply to a specific historical episode: Lincoln's response to Dred Scott, Jackson's veto of the National Bank bill, or congressional enforcement of the 14th Amendment under Section 5; evaluate Cooper v Aaron: was the SCOTUS's assertion of constitutional supremacy constitutionally justified? What is the strongest argument for departmentalism?; evaluate Tushnet's proposal to take the Constitution away from the courts — what would popular constitutionalism mean for constitutional practice?; constitutional construction — apply Solum and Barnett's distinction between interpretation and construction to a constitutional provision where text is underdeterminate; what fills the construction zone? Does originalism or living constitutionalism provide a superior construction methodology?; evaluate judicial minimalism (Sunstein) — does deciding cases on narrow grounds preserve democratic space, or does it merely defer hard questions?)",
            successCriteria: "Constitutional theory paper completed (originalism vs living constitutionalism, Dworkin's law as integrity, or departmentalism/popular constitutionalism argument developed for a specific provision or case; methodology applied; and document saved in required format)",
            preferredDuration: 90 * 60
        ),
        // bankinglaw
        SuggestedTemplate(
