            task: "Study banking law for my exam — review Dodd-Frank, Basel III, FDIC, the Federal Reserve Act, Glass-Steagall, and bank supervision: the regulatory architecture (the dual banking system: national banks (chartered by OCC, federal law) vs state banks (chartered by state, state law + federal oversight); federal regulators: OCC (national banks), Federal Reserve Board (bank holding companies, state-chartered Fed members), FDIC (state-chartered non-Fed members + deposit insurance), CFPB (consumer financial protection); Federal Reserve: lender of last resort, monetary policy, systemic risk oversight; the National Bank Act (1864): established OCC and national banking charters; the Federal Reserve Act (1913): created the Federal Reserve System — 12 regional Federal Reserve Banks + Board of Governors; the Glass-Steagall Act (1933): separated commercial and investment banking — banks could not underwrite or deal in securities; repealed by Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (1999) — allowed commercial banks, investment banks, and insurance companies to affiliate; the Bank Holding Company Act (1956): regulated companies owning banks; the Douglas Amendment prevented interstate banking; Riegle-Neal Act (1994): allowed interstate banking and branching; FDIC deposit insurance: insures deposits up to $250,000 per depositor per insured institution; funded by assessments on insured depository institutions; resolution authority: FDIC as receiver for failed banks; bridge banks, purchase and assumption transactions, deposit payoffs; too big to fail and systemic risk: moral hazard problem — large institutions may take excessive risk expecting bailout; implicit government guarantee; lender of last resort (Bagehot principle): lend freely at penalty rates against good collateral; emergency liquidity facilities — TARP (2008), PDCF, MMLF); Dodd-Frank and post-2008 reform (Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010): most significant bank regulatory overhaul since the New Deal; Title I: Financial Stability Oversight Council (FSOC) — systemic risk monitor; systemically important financial institutions (SIFIs) — enhanced prudential standards; Title II: Orderly Liquidation Authority (OLA) — FDIC-administered resolution for failing SIFIs, replacing disorderly bailout/bankruptcy; Title VI: Volcker Rule (Section 619) — prohibits banks from proprietary trading and from sponsoring hedge funds/private equity funds; Title X: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) — consolidated consumer protection authority; mortgage regulation, payday lending, debt collection; Title VII: derivatives regulation — most OTC derivatives cleared through central counterparties (CCPs), reported to swap data repositories; FSOC designation: non-bank SIFIs subject to Fed supervision; MetLife v FSOC (D.C. Cir. 2016) — court vacated SIFI designation; stress testing: DFAST (Dodd-Frank Act Stress Test) and CCAR (Comprehensive Capital Analysis and Review) — banks must show capital adequacy under severely adverse scenarios; the 2019 Economic Growth, Regulatory Relief, and Consumer Protection Act (EGRRCPA): raised SIFI threshold from $50B to $250B; relief for community banks); Basel III capital and liquidity (Basel Accords: international standards set by the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision (BCBS) at the BIS (Bank for International Settlements); Basel I (1988): 8% minimum capital requirement; risk-weighted assets; Basel II (2004): three pillars — minimum capital (IRB models allowed), supervisory review, market discipline; Basel III (2010–2019): enacted in response to 2008 crisis; increased minimum Tier 1 common equity to 4.5%; capital conservation buffer (2.5%); countercyclical capital buffer (0–2.5%); G-SIB surcharge (1–3.5%); total loss-absorbing capacity (TLAC) for G-SIBs; leverage ratio: minimum 3% Tier 1 capital to total exposure; liquidity: Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) — high-quality liquid assets (HQLA) sufficient to survive 30-day stress scenario; Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR) — stable funding for one year; Community Reinvestment Act (CRA, 1977): banks must serve the credit needs of the communities in which they operate, including low- and moderate-income (LMI) communities; CRA ratings affect mergers and acquisitions; 2023 CRA modernization rule)",
            successCriteria: "Banking law study session completed (Glass-Steagall and its repeal described, Dodd-Frank's Volcker Rule explained, Basel III Tier 1 capital requirement stated, FDIC deposit insurance limit identified, and exam notes saved)",
