            task: "Study energy economics for my exam — review electricity market design, carbon pricing, LCOE, energy transition economics, oil market dynamics, and capacity markets: electricity market structure (wholesale electricity markets: energy-only markets vs capacity markets; merit order dispatch: generators dispatched from lowest to highest marginal cost (variable cost); marginal cost pricing: clearing price set by the most expensive unit dispatched; why prices can be zero or negative (renewable with zero marginal cost); locational marginal pricing (LMP): nodal pricing reflects transmission congestion; energy-only markets: Texas (ERCOT); energy + capacity markets: PJM, ISO-NE; capacity markets: address missing money problem — energy prices alone may not recover fixed costs; capacity auctions: PJM's Reliability Pricing Model (RPM); demand response as a capacity resource; nodal vs zonal electricity pricing; ancillary services: frequency regulation, spinning reserves, operating reserves; unit commitment problem; peak load pricing); renewable energy economics (levelized cost of energy (LCOE): sum of lifetime costs / lifetime energy production; LCOE of solar PV and wind have fallen below thermal generation; learning curves (Wright's Law): cost declines as cumulative production doubles; capacity factor: actual output / maximum possible output; intermittency and system integration costs: backup capacity, transmission, storage; duck curve: midday solar overproduction, evening ramp-up challenge; merit order effect: renewable generation lowers wholesale prices, squeezes thermal margins); carbon pricing and energy transition (carbon tax: price per ton of CO2 equivalent; cap-and-trade: aggregate emission cap + tradeable permits; EU Emissions Trading System (EU ETS); California Cap-and-Trade Program; Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative (RGGI); carbon border adjustment mechanism (CBAM); social cost of carbon (SCC): EPA estimates ~$190/ton (Biden administration); energy transition economics: stranded asset risk for fossil fuel infrastructure; learning curves and cost competitiveness of clean energy; energy subsidy reform: fossil fuel subsidies (IMF estimates: $7T/year including implicit subsidies); natural gas markets (Henry Hub spot price; LNG market: liquefaction, shipping, regasification; take-or-pay contracts; pipeline capacity markets; gas-to-power economics; natural gas as bridge fuel debate); oil market structure (OPEC+ production quotas; oil price cycles; Hubbert's peak oil theory; shale revolution and U.S. tight oil; oil rent and resource curse; Hotelling's rule for non-renewable resources: efficient depletion path equates rent appreciation to interest rate))",
