        // laboreconomics
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "person.badge.clock.fill",
            task: "Study labor economics for my exam — review human capital theory, the Mincer earnings equation, search-and-matching models, wage determination, monopsony, minimum wage effects, labor market discrimination, and collective bargaining: human capital and earnings (human capital theory (Becker, 1964): workers invest in education and training that raises productivity and wages; general vs firm-specific human capital: general — portable, worker bears cost and captures return; specific — not portable, cost/return shared between worker and firm; Mincer earnings equation: ln(wage) = α + βS + γExp + δExp² + ε; where S = years of schooling, Exp = potential experience; β estimates the private return to a year of schooling (~8–10% in U.S.); returns to education: Mincerian OLS estimates suffer from ability bias and selection — Card (1995) uses geographic proximity to college as IV; Angrist and Krueger (1991) use compulsory schooling laws as IV; Duflo (2001) uses Indonesian school construction; signaling theory (Spence, 1973): education signals pre-existing productivity; policy implications differ from human capital theory; on-the-job training: Becker's general/specific human capital and training investment sharing); labor supply and demand (neoclassical labor supply: income-leisure tradeoff; backward-bending labor supply curve; participation decision; labor demand: derived demand — depends on product demand and production technology; elasticity of labor demand: Hicks-Marshall rules; monopsony (Manning, 2003): single buyer in labor market; monopsonist hires below competitive quantity, pays below competitive wage; dynamic monopsony: even competitive markets exhibit monopsony power if search frictions exist; minimum wage effects: competitive model — minimum wage above equilibrium reduces employment; monopsony model — minimum wage can increase employment and wages; Card and Krueger (1994): NJ-PA border study found minimum wage increase didn't reduce employment; Neumark and Wascher: critique and replications; Seattle Minimum Wage Study (Jardim et al.): high earner gains, low earner hours reduction; Cengiz et al. (2019): bunching estimator — little employment loss in U.S. up to 59% of median wage); search-and-matching and unemployment (Diamond-Mortensen-Pissarides (DMP) model: wage bargaining, matching function M(u,v), Beveridge curve — unemployment vs vacancy rate; job creation condition and wage determination through Nash bargaining; reservation wages; heterogeneous firms and workers (Burdett-Mortensen): wage dispersion in equilibrium even with homogeneous workers; structural unemployment vs frictional unemployment); discrimination (Becker (1957): employer taste for discrimination — discriminating employers hire less of the discriminated group, earn lower profits; statistical discrimination: employers use group averages as proxies for unobserved productivity; Blau-Kahn gender wage gap decomposition: Oaxaca-Blinder decomposition — explained vs unexplained gap; audit studies (Bertrand and Mullainathan, 2004): identical resumes with Black-sounding vs white-sounding names — callbacks differ); collective bargaining (union wage premium: ~10–15% in U.S.; union density decline in private sector; right-to-work laws; efficiency wage theory: wages above market clearing to increase productivity — Shapiro-Stiglitz shirking model)",
            successCriteria: "Labor economics study session completed (Mincer earnings equation components stated, Card-Krueger minimum wage finding described, monopsony definition given, DMP model's Beveridge curve explained, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
