has the gender wage gap narrowed but not closed? What role does nonlinearity in returns to hours worked play?; evaluate statistical discrimination models vs Becker's taste model: what empirical predictions differ? How would you distinguish between them?; apply to a specific context: racial wealth gap, disability employment gap, or gender gap in a specific occupation), or a human capital / returns to education analysis (select one: evaluate Card (1995)'s geographic proximity IV — what is the identification assumption? Is proximity to college a valid instrument? What LATE does it estimate? Compare to Mincerian OLS — is the IV estimate larger or smaller? Why?; evaluate Angrist and Krueger (1991)'s quarter-of-birth instrument — how does compulsory schooling law interact with birth quarter to create variation in education? What is the exclusion restriction? What criticisms (Bound, Jaeger, Baker) does this IV face?; evaluate the signaling vs human capital debate: what empirical tests discriminate between them? What does the return to education tell us under each model?; evaluate the college wage premium — how has the premium evolved since 1980? What does skill-biased technological change (SBTC) theory predict? What does Goldin and Katz (2008)'s 'Race between Education and Technology' framework argue?; apply to policy: should policymakers subsidize higher education on efficiency grounds if signaling is the primary mechanism?)",
