            task: "Study marine geology for my exam — review seafloor spreading, mid-ocean ridges, oceanic crust structure, turbidites, pelagic sediments, hydrothermal vents, and deep-sea drilling: plate tectonics and seafloor spreading (seafloor spreading (Hess, 1960): new oceanic crust generated at mid-ocean ridges by mantle upwelling; magnetic anomaly stripes: normal and reversed polarity recorded symmetrically on either side of ridge — confirms seafloor spreading; Vine-Matthews hypothesis (1963); spreading rate classification: slow (Mid-Atlantic Ridge: 2–5 cm/yr) vs fast (East Pacific Rise: 10–20 cm/yr); ridge morphology: axial rift valley (slow ridges) vs axial high (fast ridges); ridge segmentation: transform faults, overlapping spreading centers, non-transform offsets; transform faults and fracture zones: active transform (seismic) vs fossil fracture zone (aseismic); age-depth relationship of oceanic crust: thermal subsidence — older crust is deeper; GDH1 model: depth = 2500 + 350√(age in Ma)); oceanic crust structure (ophiolite sequence as analog for oceanic crust: Layer 1 — pelagic sediments; Layer 2A — pillow lavas (extrusive basalts); Layer 2B — sheeted dike complex; Layer 3 — gabbro (plutonic); upper mantle — peridotite (harzburgite, dunite); MORB (mid-ocean ridge basalt) composition: tholeiitic basalt, depleted in incompatible elements relative to primitive mantle; N-MORB vs E-MORB vs OIB geochemistry; partial melting at ridges: mantle adiabatic upwelling crosses solidus; melt fraction depends on spreading rate and mantle temperature; subduction of oceanic crust: oceanic crust is denser than continental — must subduct; age-density relationship; oldest oceanic crust: ~200 Ma (Mesozoic Pacific)); marine sedimentary systems (turbidites: density currents triggered by slope instability; Bouma sequence (Ta–Te): Ta = massive graded sand, Tb = planar laminated sand, Tc = rippled sand, Td = laminated silt/clay, Te = pelagic clay; turbidite systems: canyon-fan systems, channels, lobes; contourites: sediment deposited by deep thermohaline currents; pelagic sediments: calcareous ooze (foraminifera, coccoliths) — deposited above CCD; siliceous ooze (diatoms, radiolarians) — dominant in upwelling zones and high latitudes; red clay / brown clay — below CCD in deep basins; carbonate compensation depth (CCD): depth at which calcite dissolution rate equals supply (~4 km in Pacific, ~5 km in Atlantic); lysocline: depth of increased dissolution; pteropod ooze: aragonite — dissolves at shallower depth than calcite); hydrothermal vents and ocean drilling (hydrothermal vents: black smokers (350°C) and white smokers (100–300°C); mineralogy: sulfide chimney deposits (pyrite, chalcopyrite, anhydrite); vent fauna: chemolithotrophic bacteria, tube worms, vent crabs; mid-ocean ridge hydrothermal circulation: seawater penetrates fractures, is heated, reacts with basalt (Mg removed, Ca/Si/metals added), vented as hot fluid; ocean drilling: DSDP (1968–1983), ODP (1985–2003), IODP (2003–present); core recovery and drilling technology; use in paleoceanography — oxygen isotope stratigraphy, biostratigraphy, magnetostratigraphy for age models)",
