        // behavioralfinance
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "chart.line.uptrend.xyaxis",
            task: "Study behavioral finance for my exam — review prospect theory in financial markets, overconfidence bias, herding behavior, disposition effect, noise trader risk, market anomalies, and limits to arbitrage: prospect theory and loss aversion in markets (Kahneman and Tversky (1979): prospect theory — value function is concave for gains (risk-averse), convex for losses (risk-seeking), and steeper for losses than gains (loss aversion coefficient ~2.25); reference point dependence; probability weighting function — overweight small probabilities, underweight large probabilities; applications to investing: investors sell winners too early (fear of loss turning into gain) and hold losers too long; Shefrin and Statman (1985) disposition effect: propensity to sell winning stocks and hold losing stocks; aggregate market implications: Barberis and Xiong — individual disposition effect generates momentum in stocks; overconfidence bias (Daniel, Hirshleifer, Subrahmanyam, 1998): investors overconfident in private signals, underreact to public information; Barber and Odean (2000): individual investors who trade most earn least — overconfidence drives excessive trading; gender differences: men trade more than women, underperform more; overconfidence in analyst forecasts; market anomalies and calendar effects (size effect (Banz, 1981): small-cap stocks outperform large-cap — challenges CAPM; value effect (Fama and French, 1992): high book-to-market (value) stocks outperform low book-to-market (growth) stocks; momentum effect (Jegadeesh and Titman, 1993): stocks that performed well over 3–12 months continue to outperform; reversal effect: long-run reversal (De Bondt and Thaler, 1985) — past losers outperform past winners over 3–5 years; January effect: small-cap stocks outperform in January — tax-loss selling hypothesis; weekend effect: Monday returns are negative; earnings announcement drift: PEAD); noise traders and limits to arbitrage (Shleifer and Vishny (1997): limits to arbitrage — rational arbitrageurs cannot fully correct mispricing because: 1) arbitrage requires capital and leverage; 2) noise trader risk — prices can move further against arbitrageur before correcting; 3) principal-agent problem: investors withdraw capital when arbitrageur loses money; irrational exuberance (Shiller, 2000): stock prices exceed fundamental value; excess volatility puzzle (Shiller, 1981): stock prices too volatile relative to future dividends; rational bubbles vs behavioral bubbles; herding behavior (Sias, 2004): institutional investors follow each other's trades; information cascades; animal spirits (Keynes: beauty contest analogy); mental accounting (Thaler, 1985): investors treat money differently based on source and intended use; house money effect: gamblers (and investors) take more risk after gains; narrow framing: evaluate each investment in isolation rather than as a portfolio)",
            successCriteria: "Behavioral finance study session completed (prospect theory value function shape described, disposition effect definition given, momentum anomaly explained, limits to arbitrage concept stated, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
