n fee revenue sustain sufficient miner security? Apply: fee revenue needed = current annual PoW security budget; estimate required fee per transaction at different volume and fee levels; evaluate whether Bitcoin's fee market (bidding for block space) can sustain security without a block subsidy; compare to Ethereum post-Merge: how does EIP-1559 base fee burn + priority fees create a different security model?; evaluate MEV and its solutions — describe the three main MEV types: arbitrage (benign), sandwich attacks (extractive, zero-sum), and liquidations (protocol-necessary). Quantify MEV: apply Flashbots data — what fraction of blocks include MEV extraction? What is the estimated annual MEV? Evaluate proposer-builder separation (PBS) as a solution: does it democratize MEV extraction or entrench builder centralization? Evaluate MEV redistribution mechanisms (MEV smoothing, MEV blocker, SUAVE); evaluate PoS validator economics — apply Ethereum staking: what is the current staking yield (real vs nominal)? How does EIP-1559 burning interact with validator issuance? What is the net issuance rate? Evaluate liquid staking protocol concentration risk: Lido's share of staked ETH and what it means for Ethereum's decentralization and censorship resistance; evaluate the Nakamoto coefficient for different blockchain networks), or a crypto market structure analysis (select one: evaluate crypto market microstructure and manipulation — apply evidence of wash trading on centralized exchanges: what proportion of reported volume is estimated to be wash traded? What are the incentives? How does this affect price discovery? Evaluate regulatory approaches to market manipulation in crypto vs traditional securities; evaluate the relationship between Bitcoin halving cycles and price performance — apply the stock-to-flow model (PlanB): what is the S2F ratio? What does the model predict? Evaluate the criticism: is the S2F model a data-fitted relationship or a causal mechanism? What alternative