        // constitutionalcomparativelaw
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Study comparative constitutional law for my exam — review constitutional design, judicial review models (Kelsenian vs Marbury), rights entrenchment, constitutional courts, amendment formulas, parliamentary sovereignty, and presidential vs parliamentary systems: models of judicial review (Marbury v Madison (1803): U.S. concrete review — any court can review constitutionality of legislation in the context of a case; decentralized review; no erga omnes effect (ruling binds parties, not all); Kelsen's model: centralized abstract review — specialized constitutional court reviews legislation in the abstract, before or after enactment, without a concrete case; erga omnes effect — ruling binds everyone, legislation annulled; examples: Germany (BVerfG, 1949), Austria (VfGH, Kelsen's design), France (Conseil Constitutionnel — originally not a court, now closer to one post-2008 QPC reform), Italy (Corte Costituzionale), Spain (Tribunal Constitucional); hybrid models: a priori vs a posteriori review; abstract vs concrete review; incidental referral (France's QPC: any litigant can refer to Conseil Constitutionnel via cassation or conseil d'état); constitutional complaint (Germany's Verfassungsbeschwerde: individuals can petition BVerfG after exhausting other remedies); judicial supremacy vs departmentalism (judicial supremacy: courts have final word on constitutional meaning — U.S. post-Cooper v Aaron 1958; departmentalism: each branch interprets the constitution independently; popular constitutionalism: elected branches should dominate constitutional interpretation; commonwealth model of rights: UK HRA, Canada's Section 33 notwithstanding clause, New Zealand's NZBORA — dialogue between legislature and courts)); constitutional design and amendment (constitutional design variables: written vs unwritten (UK); federal vs unitary; presidential vs semi-presidential vs parliamentary; unicameral vs bicameral; entrenched vs flexible constitution; amendment formulas: supermajority (U.S. Article V: 2/3 Congress + 3/4 states); referendum (Australia, Ireland); parliamentary supermajority (Germany: 2/3 Bundestag + Bundesrat); eternity clauses (unamendable provisions): Germany's Basic Law Art. 79(3) protects human dignity (Art. 1) and federal structure (Art. 20) — Ewigkeitsklausel; India's basic structure doctrine (Kesavananda Bharati, 1973); constitutional identity: how courts use constitutional identity to limit EU primacy (Germany, Czech Republic, Poland, Hungary); rights entrenchment: negative vs positive rights; justiciability of socioeconomic rights; South Africa's Section 26 (housing) — transformative constitutionalism; parliamentary sovereignty: UK — Parliament can pass any law (Dicey); but: devolution, HRA, EU withdrawal — implied limits?; New Commonwealth Model: legislative override vs judicial invalidation; dialogue theory (Hogg and Bushell, 1997)); government structure comparisons (presidential systems: U.S. — fixed terms, separated survival, no vote of no confidence; Linz thesis (1990): presidentialism less stable than parliamentarism — winner-take-all elections, dual democratic legitimacy, zero-sum; Latin American presidential systems; parliamentary systems: Westminster (UK, Canada, Australia) — fusion of executive and legislature; constructive vote of no confidence (Germany); proportional representation and coalition governments; semi-presidential systems (Duverger, 1980): directly elected president + PM accountable to parliament; premier-presidential (France: PM can be dismissed by president only); president-parliamentary (Russia: PM accountable to both); cohabitation: France 1986–88, 1993–95, 1997–2002; federalism: dual vs cooperative; fiscal federalism; judicial federalism; asymmetric federalism (Spain comunidades autónomas, Canadian provinces))",
            successCriteria: "Comparative constitutional law study session completed (Kelsen model vs Marbury review described, eternity clause example given, notwithstanding clause mechanism stated, Linz thesis on presidentialism summarized, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Write my comparative constitutional law paper — this may include: a judicial review models analysis (select one: compare Kelsen's centralized abstract review model to the U.S. decentralized concrete review model — what are the institutional implications of each? How does erga omnes effect differ from inter partes effect? Evaluate the German BVerfG's success in establishing constitutional democracy post-1949: what institutional design features (independent appointment, abstract and concrete referral, Verfassungsbeschwerde) explain its authority? What lessons for constitutional design in new democracies?; evaluate the French Conseil Constitutionnel's evolution — from 1958 anti-assembly device to rights-protective court: how did the 1971 Liberté d'Association decision and 1974 opposition referral reform transform the Conseil? How did the 2008 QPC reform change France's model toward ex-post concrete review? What remains distinctive about the French model?; evaluate the Commonwealth model of rights — apply the Canadian Charter and Section 33 notwithstanding clause, UK Human Rights Act, and New Zealand NZBORA: what is the dialogue thesis (Hogg and Bushell)? Does the evidence support genuine judicial-legislative dialogue, or does legislative override rarely occur? Compare the three models: which best balances judicial protection with democratic legitimacy?; evaluate India's basic structure doctrine — apply Kesavananda Bharati (1973): what is the basic structure doctrine? What constitutional provisions are beyond amendment? How has the doctrine been used? Evaluate whether the basic structure doctrine is judicially illegitimate (anti-democratic) or necessary (protecting constitutional democracy against populist amendments)), a constitutional design or amendment analysis (select one: evaluate Germany's eternity clause (Ewigkeitsklausel, Article 79(3)) — what provisions are unamendable? Why were these chosen? How has the BVerfG used constitutional identity doctrine to limit EU law primacy (Solange I/II, Maastricht, Lisbon, Honeywell decisions)? Evaluate whether constitutional identity doctrine threatens EU legal order or reflects legitimate constitutional pluralism; evaluate the role of constitutional courts in protecting democracy — apply Hungary's Orbán amendments (2010–2014): how did constitutional amendments restructure the Constitutional Court? Can constitutional courts protect democracy when the constitutionmakers are the threat? Evaluate Ginsburg and Huq's 'How Democracies Die' — what democratic erosion patterns circumvent constitutional courts?; evaluate amendment difficulty and constitutional rigidity — apply Tsebelis and Nardi (2016): what is the relationship between amendment formula difficulty and constitutional longevity? Does extreme rigidity prevent democratic adaptation? Compare U.S. Article V (very rigid), German Basic Law (moderately rigid with eternity clause), and New Zealand (flexible — no written constitution)?; evaluate the South Africa Constitution's transformative constitutionalism — apply the Constitutional Court's socioeconomic rights decisions (Government of the Republic of South Africa v Grootboom (2000), Minister of Health v TAC (2002)): what does 'reasonableness' review of socioeconomic rights require? How does Section 26 (housing) + Section 27 (health) interact with resource constraints? Is transformative constitutionalism judicially manageable?), or a government structure comparison (select one: evaluate Linz's thesis that presidentialism is less stable than parliamentarism — apply to Latin American experience (Argentina, Brazil, Peru): what are the zero-sum election and dual legitimacy mechanisms Linz identifies? What empirical evidence supports or challenges the thesis? Apply Cheibub's critique: is presidential instability explained by coup-prone militaries rather than constitutional design?; evaluate semi-presidentialism and cohabitation — apply France under the Fifth Republic: what 