        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "cross.circle.fill",
            task: "Study international humanitarian law for my exam — review the Geneva Conventions, principles of IHL (distinction, proportionality, military necessity), ICRC mandate, combatant status, protected persons, and war crimes: sources and structure of IHL (Hague Law (means and methods of warfare): 1899 and 1907 Hague Conventions; 1907 Hague Regulations — rules on land warfare, occupation; Martens Clause: customary law fills gaps not addressed by treaty; Geneva Law (protection of victims): Geneva Conventions I (wounded/sick on land, 1864/1949), II (wounded/sick/shipwrecked at sea), III (prisoners of war), IV (civilian persons in occupied territory); Additional Protocol I (1977): international armed conflicts (IAC); Additional Protocol II (1977): non-international armed conflicts (NIAC); Additional Protocol III (2005): Red Crystal emblem; ICRC Customary IHL Study (2005): 161 rules applicable to both IAC and NIAC; distinction between IAC and NIAC: Article 2 GC applies to IAC (states at war); Common Article 3 applies to NIAC (armed conflict not of international character — minimum standards for all parties); Additional Protocol II raises threshold for NIAC (organized armed group, territorial control)); fundamental principles (principle of distinction: parties must at all times distinguish between civilians and combatants (AP I Art. 48); direct participation in hostilities (DPH): civilians lose protection when directly participating; ICRC Interpretive Guidance on DPH (2009); legitimate military targets: military objectives — contribution to military action + definite military advantage; principle of proportionality: attacks must not cause civilian casualties/damage excessive in relation to concrete and direct military advantage anticipated (AP I Art. 51(5)(b)); precautions in attack: do everything feasible to minimize civilian harm (AP I Art. 57); principle of military necessity: only measures necessary to achieve legitimate military aim are permitted; prohibition on unnecessary suffering: means and methods causing superfluous injury prohibited; Martens Clause and customary IHL); combatant status and protected persons (combatant status (IAC): members of armed forces of a party; lawful combatant = prisoner of war (GCIII) if captured; militias and volunteer corps meeting four conditions (Art. 4A GCIII): commanded by responsible person, fixed distinctive sign recognizable at distance, carry arms openly, conduct operations in accordance with IHL; unlawful combatant: loss of combatant privilege; unlawful combatants still protected by GC IV or AP I Art. 75 minimum guarantees; protected persons (GCIV, IAC): civilians in the hands of a party of which they are not nationals; rights: humane treatment, no coercion, family reunification; occupying power obligations (GC IV, Section III): cannot alter penal law, cannot deport, must provide food/medical care; POW treatment (GC III): humane treatment, no torture, must give name/rank/date of birth/number, cannot compel military service; detainee protections in NIAC: Common Article 3 minimum standards + APII for qualifying NIACs); war crimes and enforcement (Nuremberg and Tokyo tribunals: first international criminal accountability; Rome Statute (1998): ICC — jurisdiction over genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes, crime of aggression; war crimes (Art. 8 Rome Statute): grave breaches of Geneva Conventions (willful killing, torture, unlawful confinement, taking hostages) + serious violations of LOAC; universal jurisdiction: some states can prosecute war crimes committed anywhere; ICRC mandate: neutral and impartial humanitarian organization; confidential bilateral representations; public denunciations (exceptional); ICRC's role in monitoring detention conditions; individual criminal responsibility vs command responsibility (doctrine of superior responsibility))",
            successCriteria: "IHL study session completed (Geneva Conventions I-IV structure described, principle of distinction stated, proportionality test elements named, Common Article 3 scope explained, and exam notes saved)",
