 the ICRC position vs U.S. position on detainee treatment at Guantánamo; apply AP I Art. 75 minimum guarantees: what protections apply to all persons in the hands of a party even if they are not POWs or protected civilians?; evaluate occupation law under GC IV — apply the belligerent occupation rules: what obligations does an occupying power have under Article 64 (preserving local penal law), Article 49 (prohibition on deportation), and Articles 55–56 (food, medical care)? Apply to a specific occupation context: what IHL obligations apply to Israel in the West Bank, or Russia in occupied Ukrainian territory?; evaluate detention in NIAC — apply Common Article 3 and AP II: what minimum standards apply to detention in a NIAC? How do ICRC detention standards (Internment in Armed Conflict, 2014) go beyond treaty requirements? Evaluate the legality of administrative detention (internment) in NIAC; evaluate POW status determination under GC III — apply the GC III Article 4A requirements: which categories of persons qualify as POWs? What happens to the status of members of irregular forces (guerrillas, partisans, resistance movements)? How does the mixed-review status (Article 5 tribunal) process work?), or a war crimes and accountability analysis (select one: evaluate the command responsibility doctrine — apply the Rome Statute Art. 28: what is the difference between military commander liability (should have known) and superior liability for non-military commanders (consciously disregarded)? Apply to a specific case: evaluate ICTY jurisprudence on command responsibility (Delalić, Blaškić, Halilović); evaluate the ICC's war crimes jurisdiction — apply Art. 8 Rome Statute: what is the difference between grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions and serious violations of the laws and customs of war? Apply to a specific situation: evaluate the ICC's investigation into a specific armed conflict (Gaza, Ukraine, or Sudan); evaluate universal jurisdiction for war crimes — compare Belgium's universal jurisdiction experience (pre-2003 and post-2003 reform), Spain's Audiencia Nacional, and Germany's Code of Crimes Against International Law: what are the political and practical limits on universal jurisdiction?; evaluate humanitarian intervention and its IHL implications — apply the R2P doctrine and NATO's Kosovo intervention: is humanitarian intervention legal under international law without SC authorization? What IHL obligations apply to humanitarian interveners? Evaluate the consistency between protecting civilians (R2P) and IHL compliance (distinction, proportionality))",
