dominant in Southeast Asia, East Africa, parts of Middle East; Hanbali (Ahmad ibn Hanbal, d. 855): most textualist, weakest qiyas; Saudi Arabia; basis for Wahhabi/Salafi thought; Shia law: Ja'fari school — Usuli vs Akhbari debate; wilayat al-faqih (Khomeini); difference from Sunni: mut'a (temporary marriage), khums (fifth tax), marjaiyya); criminal law and penalties (hudud (fixed punishments prescribed by Quran or Sunnah): zina (adultery/fornication): flogging (unmarried) or stoning (married); strict evidentiary standards (4 male witnesses); qadhf (false accusation of zina): flogging; sariqah (theft): hand amputation; harabah (highway robbery/brigandage); ridda (apostasy): death (contested); shurb al-khamr (drinking wine): flogging; evidentiary requirements: very high burden of proof historically interpreted to make hudud difficult to impose; ta'zir (discretionary punishment): anything not covered by hudud; judge determines appropriate penalty; qisas (retaliation in kind) and diyya (blood money): homicide; heirs may pardon, accept diyya, or demand retaliation; Islamic contract law: prohibition of riba (usury/interest) — extends to all fixed predetermined return on loans; Islamic finance instruments: murabaha (cost-plus sale), musharaka (partnership), mudaraba (profit-sharing), ijara (lease), sukuk (Islamic bonds); prohibition of gharar (excessive uncertainty); prohibition of maysir (gambling))",
