        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.2.fill",
            task: "Write my operations management paper — this may include: an inventory and supply chain analysis (select one: evaluate the bullwhip effect and remedies in a specific supply chain — apply Lee-Padmanabhan-Whang (1997) and Forrester (1961): what are the four root causes (demand signal processing, rationing game, order batching, price fluctuation)? Evaluate a specific supply chain's bullwhip dynamics (CPG, automotive, semiconductor, healthcare); what interventions (vendor-managed inventory, CPFR, POS data sharing, order smoothing) reduce the bullwhip effect? Evaluate the evidence; evaluate inventory policy design for a specific product — apply the newsvendor model (single-period) or (Q,R) model (multi-period) to a real product category (fashion apparel, seasonal produce, pharmaceutical safety stock): derive optimal Q* or ROP; what are the key parameters (holding cost, stockout cost, lead time variability)? Evaluate the evidence on safety stock drivers and the cost of inventory leanness; analyze a make-to-stock vs make-to-order strategy tradeoff — apply the decoupling point concept: for a specific manufacturer (automotive, FMCG, industrial equipment), where is the optimal push-pull boundary? Evaluate the inventory-service tradeoff; how does demand variability and product mix complexity affect the optimal decoupling point?), an operations systems and ERP analysis (select one: evaluate the ERP implementation failure problem — apply an ERP case study (Nike's i2 SCM failure 2000, Hershey's SAP 1999, or FoxMeyer Drug's SAP bankruptcy): what organizational and technical factors explain ERP implementation failures? Evaluate the change management literature (Hammer BPR, Kotter 8-step); apply the critical success factors framework (CSF: top management support, business process redesign, project management, user training, data migration); what does the implementation literature find about success rates and cost overruns?; evaluate revenue management system design for a specific industry — apply airline yield management (EMSR model, seat inventory control, overbooking policy) or hotel revenue management (rate fences, duration control, displacement analysis): what customer segmentation criteria support revenue management? Evaluate the welfare effects (consumer vs producer surplus, fairness perceptions); what are the limits of dynamic pricing (price-sensitive markets, commodity products)?; analyze process improvement using the TOC and lean frameworks — apply Goldratt's Theory of Constraints (identify bottleneck → exploit → subordinate → elevate → repeat) to a specific production or service process; how does TOC differ from lean manufacturing (value stream mapping, muda/mura/muri, takt time)? Evaluate the empirical evidence on lean adoption outcomes (operational performance, worker well-being); or a capacity and service operations analysis (select one: evaluate capacity strategy in a cyclical industry — apply lead, lag, or match capacity timing strategies to a specific industry facing cyclical demand (semiconductor fabs, hotel construction, hospital bed planning): what factors determine the optimal timing? Evaluate real options theory (option to delay, option to expand) applied to capacity investment decisions; what does the capacity management literature find about the costs of under- vs over-capacity?; analyze service quality and the SERVQUAL model — apply Parasuraman-Zeithaml-Berry (1985, 1988) to a specific service context (banking, healthcare, hospitality): measure the five SERVQUAL dimensions (reliability, assurance, tangibles, empathy, responsiveness); what gaps (between customer expectations and perceptions) explain service quality failures? Evaluate alternative models (SERVPERF, kano model); what operational changes (staffing, training, process design) close the gaps?)",
