        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Study urban economics for my exam — review the monocentric city model, bid-rent theory, agglomeration economies, spatial equilibrium, housing supply, and place-based policies: monocentric city model and land use (Alonso (1964)/Muth (1969)/Mills (1967) AMM model: bid-rent function B(x) = Revenue - Transport_cost(x) - Production_cost; equilibrium: all land rents equal across uses; residential bid-rent: steeper for households with high transport cost; commercial bid-rent: steeper in central locations; negative rent gradient; implications: density gradient (higher density near CBD), city size determined by commuting cost; monocentric city extensions: polycentric cities (subcenters); edge cities; suburban employment; critique: urban sprawl; monocentric city empirical evidence (McDonald-Prather, Giuliano-Small)); agglomeration economies (Marshall's (1890) triad: labor market pooling (deep local labor pool reduces search frictions), input sharing (specialized suppliers cluster near firms), knowledge spillovers (face-to-face, tacit knowledge diffusion); localization economies: firms in same industry benefit from clustering (Silicon Valley, Hollywood); urbanization economies: firms in any industry benefit from urban size; agglomeration elasticity: 10% increase in employment density → ~3–8% productivity increase (Rosenthal-Strange); mechanisms evidence: Ellison-Glaeser (1997) concentration index, Moretti knowledge spillovers, Greenstone-Hornbeck-Moretti plant openings); spatial equilibrium (Rosen (1979)/Roback (1982) model: workers indifferent across cities → wage-amenity-rent equilibrium; spatial equilibrium condition: U(w,r,a) = Ū (utility same everywhere); firms: R&D/labor costs determine wages; implications: higher wages compensate for higher rents or worse amenities; Bartik/Card-DiNario local labor demand shocks; quantitative spatial models (Eaton-Kortum, Redding-Venables)); housing supply and demand (housing demand: income elasticity ~0.5-0.8 (Malpezzi-Mayo); price elasticity -0.5 to -1.0; filtering model (Rosenthal); housing supply: Saiz (2010) supply elasticity: land constraints (geography) + regulatory constraints (zoning, permitting); elastic supply cities (Phoenix, Las Vegas): prices rise and quantity responds; inelastic cities (NYC, SF, Boston): prices absorb demand shocks; Glaeser-Gyourko: zoning raises construction costs above $100K+ per unit in NYC; rent control: Diamond-McQuade-Qian (2019) SF study: reduced supply by 15%, increased rents by 5-7% in uncontrolled units; misallocation (Sims)); place-based policies and gentrification (place-based policies: Empowerment Zones (EZs): Busso-Gregory-Kline (2013) — earnings of residents up 13%, mixed zone effects; Opportunity Zones (OZ): Corinth et al.: real estate investment up, limited effects on low-income residents; HUD moving programs: MTO — MTO (Moving to Opportunity): Chetty-Hendren-Katz (2016): long-run earnings effects for children moved at young ages; gentrification: definition (Lees-Slater-Wyly; Hackworth); displacement evidence: Diamond-McQuade-Qian SF; Guerrieri et al. (2013): complementarity in neighborhood quality; amenity effects (Albouy-Ehrlich-Liu); crime effects (Lees))",
