licies: Empowerment Zones or Opportunity Zones — apply Busso-Gregory-Kline (2013) EZ evaluation or Corinth et al. (2022) OZ study: what are the mechanisms through which place-based subsidies affect local economic activity? Evaluate the targeting problem (Glaeser-Gottlieb); what does the MTO (Moving to Opportunity) literature (Chetty-Hendren-Katz 2016) imply about place vs people policies?; evaluate the spatial equilibrium implications of remote work — apply Autor-Reynolds and Ramani-Bloom: how has remote work changed the spatial equilibrium across cities? Evaluate the evidence on urban rental price divergence (SF/NYC vs mid-size cities), commuting patterns, and the monocentric city model prediction errors; what is the long-run spatial equilibrium adjustment?), or a spatial equilibrium analysis (select one: evaluate the Rosen-Roback model and its policy implications — apply Roback (1982) to a specific policy question (minimum wage, local tax changes, environmental amenities): what does the model predict about the incidence of local labor market changes (Bartik shocks)? Evaluate the empirical approach (Bartik instrument validity: Goldsmith-Pinkham et al.); how do local fiscal policies (tax and spend) affect spatial equilibrium in the Tiebout (1956) framework?; evaluate Krugman's new economic geography — apply the core-periphery model (Krugman 1991) to a specific regional agglomeration: what are the three forces (market access, labor market pooling, forward/backward linkages)? What is the equilibrium selection under increasing returns? Evaluate the empirical evidence on the home market effect (Davis-Weinstein; Head-Mayer) and cumulative causation (path dependence in regional specialization))",
