): horizon of expectations, horizon change (Horizontwandel), aesthetic distance; Eco: model reader, over-interpretation; cultural studies: Hall's encoding/decoding); postcolonial and world literature (Said's Orientalism (1978): discourse of the Orient; imaginative geography; Foucauldian genealogy applied to colonial knowledge; Spivak: Can the Subaltern Speak? (1988): subaltern silence, epistemic violence, strategic essentialism; Bhabha: hybridity, mimicry, third space, ambivalence; Glissant: creolization, Relation, Tout-Monde; world literature (Damrosch: what circulates, what gets translated, untranslatability); Casanova: world republic of letters, consecration, literary capitals); narratology (Genette's Narrative Discourse (1972): three aspects — histoire (story: events), récit (narrative: text), narration (act of narrating); time: order (analepsis/prolepsis), duration (summary/scene/pause/ellipsis), frequency (singulative/iterative/repetitive); mood: focalization (zero — omniscient; internal — character; external — behaviorist); voice: narrator levels (extradiegetic/intradiegetic/homodiegetic/heterodiegetic); Bakhtin: dialogism and heteroglossia; polyphonic novel (Dostoevsky); chronotope (time-space); carnivalesque; Kristeva: intertextuality (every text absorbs prior texts)); feminist and psychoanalytic criticism (Cixous: écriture féminine; Irigaray: l'écriture; Butler: performativity (Gender Trouble 1990); de Beauvoir: The Second Sex (woman as Other); feminist narratology (Lanser); psychoanalytic criticism: Freud (Oedipus, unconscious, uncanny/das Unheimliche in literature); Lacan; Bloom's anxiety of influence)",
