 deconstructive move or install a new essentialism?; analyze a world literature text through translation theory — apply Damrosch's world literature (what circulates) or Venuti's foreignization/domestication to a specific text in translation (choose: Kafka in English, García Márquez in English, Homer in multiple translations, or Du Boscq's Genji): what is lost or gained in translation? Evaluate Spivak's critique of English-language world literature programs: is the world literature frame itself a form of epistemic violence?; apply feminist or queer literary theory to a specific text — apply Butler's performativity or Cixous's écriture féminine to a specific text (choose: Woolf's Orlando, Carter's The Bloody Chamber, Anzaldúa's Borderlands, or Rich's Diving into the Wreck): how does the text perform, resist, or parody gender norms? Evaluate the relationship between form (style, genre) and feminist/queer content), or an intertextuality and genre analysis (select one: analyze intertextuality in a specific literary text — apply Kristeva's intertextuality, Bloom's anxiety of influence, or Genette's transtextuality (hypertext, hypotext, paratext, metatext) to a specific text (choose: Milton's Paradise Lost (Virgil/Homer hypotexts), Atwood's The Handmaid's Tale (Hawthorne hypertext), Walcott's Omeros (Homer palimpsest), or Borges's Ficciones): trace the intertextual web; evaluate what the transformation (hypertext relation) does to meaning; analyze genre and its political implications — apply Bakhtin's novel theory (dialogism, polyphony, heteroglossia, carnivalesque) or Frye's Anatomy of Criticism (archetypal myth criticism: comedy/tragedy/romance/satire, displacement) to a specific text or genre (choose: Cervantes's Don Quixote, Rabelais's Gargantua, the postcolonial novel, or the graphic novel): how does the text's generic positioning shape its political possibilities?)",
