stice; ideal vs non-ideal theory; Rawls vs utilitarianism: why Rawls rejects aggregation); Nozick and libertarianism (Anarchy State and Utopia (1974): entitlement theory: (1) justice in acquisition (Lockean proviso: enough and as good for others); (2) justice in transfer (voluntary transactions); (3) justice in rectification; critique of patterned principles: Wilt Chamberlain argument — any patterned distribution will be disrupted by voluntary transactions; liberty upsets patterns; minimal state (night-watchman): only legitimate state function is protecting rights; taxation as forced labor; critique of Rawls: difference principle violates self-ownership; libertarian vs classical liberal vs conservative distinctions); communitarianism (Sandel's Liberalism and the Limits of Justice (1982): critique of unencumbered self — Rawls's subjects have no prior commitments; situated self: identity constituted by community/tradition; Justice: What's the Right Thing to Do? (2009): civic engagement, common good; MacIntyre's After Virtue (1981): virtues require practice-internal goods; narrative unity of a life; politics of the common good; Taylor's Communitarian Critique: atomism; authenticity and the modern identity; politics of recognition (Honneth); Walzer's Spheres of Justice: complex equality, separate spheres; communitarianism vs liberalism: can liberal theory accommodate shared values?); civic republicanism and deliberative democracy (Pettit's republicanism: freedom as non-domination (not merely non-interference — Hobbes/Berlin); arbitrary interference vs non-arbitrary interference; republican liberty: resilience against interference; contestatory citizenry; Machiavelli and civic republicanism; Arendt: The Human Condition (1958): vita activa: labor/work/action; action = political activity in public realm; natality: beginning something new; plurality: political life requires many; power vs violence; Origins of Totalitarianism; Habermas: Theory of Communicative Action: communicative vs strategic action; discourse ethics: valid norms require consensus under ideal conditions; ideal speech situation: no coercion, all voices equal; deliberative democracy: Bohman, Cohen; Mouffe's agonistic democracy: antagonism → agonism; democratic paradox; critique of deliberative democracy; Schmitt's friend/enemy distinction)",
