        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "chart.line.uptrend.xyaxis",
            task: "Study microeconomics for my exam — review consumer theory, producer theory, elasticity, market equilibrium, welfare analysis, and imperfect competition: consumer theory (preferences and utility: completeness, transitivity, monotonicity, convexity; utility function U(x,y) represents preferences up to monotone transformation; indifference curves slope downward (MU positive), convex (diminishing MRS); marginal rate of substitution MRS = MU_x/MU_y = P_x/P_y at optimum; budget constraint: P_x x + P_y y = I; consumer optimum: tangency condition; corner solutions when MRS ≠ P_x/P_y at any interior point; Cobb-Douglas utility U=x^α y^β: optimal x* = αI/P_x, y* = βI/P_y; expenditure shares constant at α/(α+β) and β/(α+β); demand derivation via Lagrangian: max U(x,y) s.t. budget constraint → MU_x/P_x = MU_y/P_y = λ (marginal utility of income); income expansion path and Engel curves; income elasticity: normal (ε_I>0), inferior (ε_I<0), luxury (ε_I>1); Slutsky decomposition: price effect = substitution effect + income effect; substitution always negative (Slutsky matrix negative semi-definite); Giffen good: price increases → demand increases (inferior good with strong income effect; rare); compensated vs uncompensated demand curves; expenditure function e(P_x,P_y,U); Shephard's lemma: ∂e/∂P_x = x^h (Hicksian demand); Roy's identity: x^M = -(∂v/∂P_x)/(∂v/∂I) (Marshallian from indirect utility); elasticities: price elasticity ε = (∂Q/∂P)(P/Q); unit elastic at midpoint of linear demand; TR maximized at ε=-1; cross-price elasticity: substitutes positive, complements negative); producer theory and firm behavior (production function Q=f(K,L); marginal products MP_L, MP_K; MRTS = MP_L/MP_K = w/r at cost minimum; Cobb-Douglas Q=L^α K^β: returns to scale α+β; profit max: MR=MC; supply curve = MC above AVC; shutdown: P<AVC; long-run: P<AC exit, P>AC entry; cost functions: TC = FC+VC; AC = TC/Q; MC = dTC/dQ; relationship: MC crosses AC and AVC at their minima; duality: Shephard's lemma ∂c(w,r,q)/∂w = L* conditional factor demand; envelope theorem: ∂π/∂P = Q* (Hotelling's lemma)); market structures (perfect competition: P=MC=AC in LR equilibrium, zero profit, efficient; monopoly: MR=MC where MR=P(1+1/ε); monopoly markup: (P-MC)/P = -1/ε (Lerner Index); DWL triangle; natural monopoly: AC declining; monopolistic competition: zero profit LR, excess capacity, P>MC; oligopoly: Cournot quantity competition, Bertrand price competition; Nash equilibrium); welfare analysis (CS = area under demand above price; PS = area above supply below price; total surplus = CS + PS + tax revenue - DWL; First Welfare Theorem: competitive equilibrium Pareto efficient; Second Welfare Theorem: any Pareto allocation via redistribution + competition; externalities: negative externality → overproduction, corrected by Pigouvian tax; public goods: non-rival and non-excludable; free-rider problem; asymmetric information: adverse selection (hidden type — Akerlof lemons), moral hazard (hidden action))",
