luate the Bertrand paradox and its resolutions (product differentiation, capacity constraints, repeated games — Folk theorem); apply to a real oligopoly (airline pricing, smartphone OS, streaming platforms); evaluate price discrimination — apply first-degree (perfect), second-degree (versioning/menus), and third-degree (market segmentation) price discrimination to a specific market; calculate welfare effects for each type; which consumers gain and lose? Evaluate the role of arbitrage in limiting price discrimination; when does price discrimination increase total welfare?), or a welfare and externality analysis (select one: evaluate the Coase theorem and its limits — apply Coase (1960) to a specific externality (factory pollution, noise, cattle/crops): under what conditions does bargaining produce the efficient outcome regardless of the initial property rights allocation? Evaluate the objections: transaction costs, wealth effects, bilateral monopoly, public goods externalities; what are the policy implications for environmental regulation (Pigouvian taxes vs cap-and-trade vs Coasian bargaining)?; evaluate public goods provision — apply the Samuelson condition for optimal public good provision (ΣMB = MC); what does the free-rider problem predict about voluntary provision? Evaluate voluntary contribution mechanisms (Bergstrom-Blume-Varian), government provision, and Lindahl pricing; apply to a specific public good (national defense, basic research, open-source software, herd immunity); evaluate the minimum wage controversy using a competitive vs monopsony labor market model — in competitive labor market: minimum wage above equilibrium causes unemployment (Δemployment = -ε_labor × Δw); in monopsony: minimum wage can increase both wages and employment; apply the Card-Krueger (1994) study and subsequent meta-analyses (Doucouliagou-Stanley): what does the evidence suggest about employment effects? Evaluate the theoretical and empirical controversy)",
