            task: "Study econometrics for my exam — review OLS assumptions and properties, hypothesis testing, instrumental variables, panel data, difference-in-differences, and regression discontinuity: OLS regression (simple regression: Yi = β₀ + β₁Xi + ui; OLS estimator: β̂₁ = Σ(Xi-X̄)(Yi-Ȳ)/Σ(Xi-X̄)² = Cov(X,Y)/Var(X); R² = ESS/TSS = 1-RSS/TSS; adjusted R² penalizes additional regressors; multiple regression: Y = Xβ + u; β̂ = (X'X)⁻¹X'Y; variance: Var(β̂) = σ²(X'X)⁻¹; Gauss-Markov theorem: β̂ is BLUE (Best Linear Unbiased Estimator) if (1) linearity, (2) random sampling, (3) no perfect multicollinearity, (4) zero conditional mean E[u|X]=0, (5) homoskedasticity E[u²|X]=σ²; OLS is consistent under (1)-(4); efficiency requires (5); assumption violations: omitted variable bias (E[β̂₁]≠β₁ if omitted Z correlates with X and Y — bias = (Cov(X,Z)/Var(X)) × γ_Z); multicollinearity inflates SE but doesn't bias; measurement error in X attenuates β̂ toward zero (attenuation bias); heteroskedasticity: OLS unbiased but inefficient; White heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors; Breusch-Pagan test; GLS/WLS when form is known; functional form: log-log (elasticity = β), log-linear (semi-elasticity = β), linear-log, quadratic; interpreting coefficient magnitudes; binary and categorical regressors: dummy variables, omitted category interpretation); hypothesis testing and inference (t-statistic: t = (β̂ - β₀)/SE(β̂); reject H₀ at 5% if |t|>1.96 (large sample); type I (false positive) vs type II (false negative) errors; p-value: probability of observing |t̂| or larger if H₀ true; confidence interval: β̂ ± 1.96 SE(β̂); F-statistic: joint hypothesis test q restrictions; F = (RSS_R - RSS_U)/q ÷ RSS_U/(n-k-1); Chow test for structural break; standard error clustering: cluster at level of treatment assignment; HAC (Newey-West) for time-series autocorrelation; testing for heteroskedasticity: Breusch-Pagan (LM test), White test; testing for serial correlation: Durbin-Watson, Breusch-Godfrey); instrumental variables and endogeneity (endogeneity sources: simultaneity (price and quantity determined together), reverse causality, omitted variable; IV requirements: (1) relevance — Cov(Z,X)≠0 (testable — F>10 in first stage); (2) exclusion restriction — Cov(Z,u)=0 (not directly testable); IV estimator (TSLS): β̂_IV = (Z'X)⁻¹Z'Y; equivalently 2SLS: first stage X = π₀ + π₁Z + v (get X̂), second stage Y = β₀ + β₁X̂ + u; Wald estimator: β_IV = (Ȳ₁-Ȳ₀)/(X̄₁-X̄₀) for binary instrument; weak instruments: F<10 → biased IV, Anderson-Rubin confidence sets; overidentification: test exclusion with Sargan-Hansen J-statistic; local average treatment effect (LATE): IV estimates effect for compliers only; panel data and fixed effects (panel data: repeated observations for same units over time; within-unit variation vs between-unit variation; fixed effects (FE) estimator: demean by unit — removes time-invariant unobserved heterogeneity; β̂_FE = β̂_within; random effects (RE): efficient if E[αᵢ|Xᵢₜ]=0; Hausman test discriminates FE vs RE; first differences: Δyᵢₜ = βΔxᵢₜ + Δuᵢₜ eliminates αᵢ; two-way FE: unit + time effects; cluster standard errors at unit level for serial correlation in uᵢₜ); difference-in-differences and RD (DiD: β_DiD = (Ȳ_T,post - Ȳ_T,pre) - (Ȳ_C,post - Ȳ_C,pre); parallel trends assumption: treatment/control groups would trend the same absent treatment; event study: test pre-trends — should be zero; staggered DiD pitfalls (Callaway-Sant'Anna); RD: compare outcomes just above vs just below threshold; bandwidth selection: bias-variance tradeoff; McCrary density test: no bunching at cutoff; sharp vs fuzzy RD; local polynomial regression; RD as IV at the cutoff)",
