        // industrialorganization
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "chart.line.uptrend.xyaxis",
            task: "Study industrial organization for my exam — review market structure, Cournot and Bertrand competition, monopolistic competition, vertical relations, two-sided markets, and antitrust economics: market power and monopoly (monopoly pricing: MR=MC; MR = P(1 + 1/ε); Lerner Index: L = (P-MC)/P = -1/ε; DWL = ½(Q_c-Q_m)(P_m-MC); natural monopoly: declining AC curve; regulation: marginal cost pricing (P=MC → deficit), average cost pricing (P=AC → zero profit but DWL), two-part tariff (fixed fee + marginal cost → efficient but redistributive), Ramsey pricing (inverse elasticity rule: (P_i-MC_i)/P_i = λ/ε_i); price discrimination: first-degree (extract all CS), second-degree (nonlinear pricing, versioning, bundling), third-degree (segment markets; condition: can separate segments, different elasticities, no arbitrage; welfare: raises total output if yes, otherwise ambiguous)); Cournot and Bertrand oligopoly (Cournot: firms choose quantities simultaneously; firm i maximizes π_i = P(Q)q_i - c_i q_i; best response: q_i*(q_j) = (a - c - q_j)/(2b) for linear demand P=a-bQ; symmetric Nash equilibrium: Q_i* = (a-c)/((N+1)b); P* = (a+Nc)/(N+1); as N→∞: P→c (competitive limit); Cournot profit: π_i* = (a-c)²/(b(N+1)²); Bertrand with homogeneous goods and equal costs: P=MC (Bertrand paradox — two firms sufficient for competition); resolutions: capacity constraints (Edgeworth cycles), product differentiation (Hotelling), repeated games; Bertrand with differentiation: Nash equilibrium P_i > MC_i; comparative statics; Stackelberg leadership: leader commits first; leader q_L* = (a-c)/(2b), follower q_F* = (a-c)/(4b); leader profit > Cournot; first-mover advantage requires commitment; collusion and cartel: joint profit maximization Q_m = (a-c)/(2b); temptation to deviate; Folk theorem: collusion sustainable with sufficiently patient firms; anti-cartel enforcement, leniency programs); monopolistic competition and vertical relations (monopolistic competition: many firms, differentiated products, free entry; SR: price above MC; LR: zero profit, tangency of demand and AC; excess capacity, price > minimum AC; love of variety; Hotelling linear city: firms locate at extremes (maximum differentiation) with quadratic transport; Salop circle model with free entry; vertical restraints: exclusive dealing, territorial restrictions, tying, RPM (resale price maintenance); double marginalization problem: two successive monopolists over-price — vertical integration solves this; franchise contracts solve double marginalization without integration; exclusive dealing as exclusionary — raises rivals' costs; Whinston foreclosure; two-sided markets and platforms (two-sided platforms: connect buyer and seller groups with cross-side network effects; pricing: p_B + p_S = (c_B + c_S) + markup; subsidy one side to attract the other; examples: credit cards (Rochet-Tirole 2003), operating systems, media, dating apps; multi-homing vs single-homing; network effects: direct (same side) vs indirect (cross-side); antitrust (Amex case: must account for effects on both sides)); antitrust economics (market definition: SSNIP (small but significant non-transitory increase in price) test — smallest market in which a monopolist would impose 5-10% price increase; HHI: Σs_i² (market share %; DoJ guidelines: <1500 unconcentrated, 1500-2500 moderate, >2500 high; merger: ΔHHI>200 in market >2500 scrutinized); unilateral effects (price increases post-merger) vs coordinated effects (facilitates tacit collusion); Williamson tradeoff: efficiency gain (rectangle) vs DWL (triangle); predatory pricing: below-cost pricing followed by recoupment; Brooke Group test; raising-rivals'-cost theory)",
