 evaluate the Cournot vs Bertrand model fit for a specific industry — apply the Bresnahan (1989) conduct parameter approach to electricity markets, airlines, or banking: how do empirical estimates of the conjectural variations parameter identify market conduct? Evaluate the empirical literature on market power in US banking (Berger-Hannan), hospitals (Gaynor-Town), or airlines (Berry); evaluate platform competition and antitrust in digital markets — apply the Rochet-Tirole (2003) two-sided market model to a specific platform (credit cards, app stores, search advertising, rideshare); what are the pricing implications of cross-side network effects? Evaluate the European Commission's Digital Markets Act and US Big Tech antitrust investigations through the lens of IO theory: does the consumer welfare standard capture all harms from platform market power?; or a vertical relations and entry analysis (select one: evaluate exclusive dealing as exclusionary foreclosure — apply Whinston (1990) and Rasmusen-Ramseyer-Wiley (1991): when is exclusive dealing anticompetitive vs efficient? Apply to a specific case (Microsoft browser exclusivity, Apple App Store exclusivity, pharma pay-for-delay); evaluate the Chicago School efficiency defense; evaluate the Aspen Skiing test for duty-to-deal; evaluate entry deterrence strategies — apply Dixit (1980) capacity commitment model and Milgrom-Roberts (1982) limit pricing: when is deterrence rational? Evaluate predatory pricing cases (Spirit Airlines, Standard Oil) against the recoupment standard; apply the Fudenberg-Tirole (1984) taxonomy (fat-cat, lean-and-hungry, top-dog, puppy-dog); evaluate switching costs and lock-in — apply Klemperer (1987, 1995): what determines switching cost magnitude? How do switching costs affect market dynamics (less entry ex ante, higher prices ex post)? Evaluate platform lock-in (smartphone ecosystems, ERP systems, cloud platforms) and policy responses (data portability, interoperability mandates))",
