            task: "Study organizational behavior for my exam — review motivation theories, leadership models, group dynamics, organizational structure, and organizational culture: individual motivation theories (content theories — what motivates: Maslow's hierarchy of needs (1943): physiological → safety → social/belongingness → esteem → self-actualization; deficit principle: unsatisfied need motivates; prepotency: must satisfy lower before higher; Alderfer's ERG: collapse to existence/relatedness/growth; regression principle if higher frustrated; Herzberg's two-factor theory (1959): hygiene factors (salary, supervision, working conditions, company policy, job security) — dissatisfiers; absence of hygiene causes dissatisfaction, presence neutral; motivators (achievement, recognition, work itself, responsibility, advancement, growth) — satisfiers; absence neutral, presence motivates; implication: enrich jobs with motivators; McClelland's acquired needs: nAch (achievement), nPow (power), nAff (affiliation); nAch characteristics: moderate risk, feedback, personal responsibility; high nAch ≠ high management potential; managers need nPow > nAff; process theories — how motivation works: Vroom's expectancy theory (1964): Motivation = Valence × Instrumentality × Expectancy; expectancy: effort → performance; instrumentality: performance → outcome; valence: value of outcome; multiplied — any zero kills motivation; implications: tie rewards to performance, ensure effort matters; Adams's equity theory (1963): compare input/outcome ratio to referent other; underpayment inequity → tension → reduce inputs, increase other's outcomes, leave, change comparison other; overpayment: guilt, less common behavioral response; equity restoration mechanisms; Locke's goal-setting theory (1968): specific, challenging goals + feedback → performance (vs vague or easy goals); moderators: goal commitment, self-efficacy, task complexity; MBO (management by objective) applications; reinforcement theory (Skinner): OB Mod — positive reinforcement (desired behavior → reward), negative reinforcement (remove aversive stimulus), punishment (undesired → aversive), extinction (remove reward); reinforcement schedules: continuous, fixed-ratio, variable-ratio, fixed-interval, variable-interval; variable-ratio most powerful for persistence); leadership theories (trait approach: Big Five correlates of leadership (extraversion most consistent); Ohio State two-factor model: initiating structure (task-oriented) vs consideration (relationship-oriented) — independent dimensions; Michigan leadership studies: production-centered vs employee-centered; Fiedler's contingency model (1964): LPC score — high LPC = relationship-oriented, low LPC = task-oriented; situational favorability = f(leader-member relations, task structure, position power); high LPC effective in moderate favorability; low LPC effective in high or low; can't change style — must engineer the situation; House's path-goal theory (1971): leader's role is to clarify path, remove obstacles, provide rewards; four styles: directive, supportive, participative, achievement-oriented; contingent on subordinate characteristics and task; SLT (Hersey-Blanchard): style contingent on follower maturity/readiness; transformational vs transactional leadership (Bass 1985, Burns 1978): transactional — contingent reward, management-by-exception; transformational — idealized influence (II), inspirational motivation (IM), intellectual stimulation (IS), individualized consideration (IC); transformational predicts performance beyond transactional; servant leadership (Greenleaf); leader-member exchange (LMX) theory: differentiated quality relationships with each subordinate; in-group vs out-group effects); group dynamics and organizational design (group development: Tuckman's stages — forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning; group vs team: teams have collective goals, mutual accountability; social loafing: effort reduction when individual contribution not identifiable — counteracted by accountability; groupthink (Janis 1972): antecedents (cohesion, insulation, directive leadership, high stress); symptoms (illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalization, stereotyping outgroups, self-censorship, mindguards); organizational structure: mechanistic (formalization, specialization, centralization) vs organic (flexibility, cross-functional teams); span of control; matrix structure; flat vs tall hierarchy; organizational culture (Schein 1985): artifacts, espoused values, basic underlying assumptions; culture strength; culture-performance link; socialization; power and politics: French-Raven power bases (reward, coercive, legitimate, referent, expert); coalition building; conflict management: Thomas-Kilmann dual-concern model (competing/collaborating/compromising/avoiding/accommodating)",
            successCriteria: "Organizational behavior study session completed (Vroom's VIE formula explained, Herzberg's two-factor distinction memorized, Fiedler's contingency model situational favorability described, Tuckman's stages named, groupthink symptoms listed, and exam notes saved)",
