        // moralphilosophy
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "text.book.closed.fill",
            task: "Study moral philosophy for my exam — review metaethics, consequentialism, Kantian deontology, virtue ethics, contractualism, and applied ethics: metaethics (metaethics asks: what is the nature of moral claims? Moral realism: moral facts are mind-independent objective facts; cognitivism: moral statements are truth-apt; natural vs non-natural realism; Cornell realism (Sturgeon, Boyd, Brink): moral properties are natural but not reducible; error theory (Mackie 1977): moral claims are truth-apt but uniformly false — no objective prescriptivity; expressivism: moral statements express attitudes, not beliefs; non-cognitivism: Ayer emotivism (moral claims = expressions of emotion); Stevenson subjectivism; Blackburn quasi-realism; Gibbard norm expressivism; constructivism: Rawlsian — moral facts constructed by rational agreement under idealized conditions; Korsgaard Kantian constructivism: reflective endorsement; relativism: moral facts vary across cultures (descriptive vs metaethical); supervenience: moral properties supervene on natural properties — identical natural facts entail identical moral facts; the open question argument (Moore 1903): can always ask 'is the good thing good?' without contradiction — proves good is non-natural; naturalistic fallacy); consequentialism (act utilitarianism (Bentham 1789; Mill 1861): right action maximizes total happiness/utility; felicific calculus: intensity, duration, certainty, propinquity, fecundity, purity, extent; higher pleasures (Mill): intellectual > bodily; utility = subjective wellbeing; objections: violates rights (innocent scapegoat), demandingness (always maximize), integrity (sacrificing personal commitments), distribution (could justify inequality if total utility higher); preference utilitarianism (Singer, Hare): satisfy preferences, not pleasures; effective altruism application; rule utilitarianism: adopt rules that if generally followed maximize utility — closer to intuition; indirect utilitarianism (Brandt); two-level utilitarianism (Hare): intuitive level (rules/heuristics) + critical level (pure impartialism); Peter Singer's drowning child argument: geographical distance is morally irrelevant; obligation to donate to effective charities; impartiality and demandingness objection; satisficing utilitarianism (Slote)); Kantian deontology (Kant Groundwork (1785): three formulations of categorical imperative: (CI-1) Formula of Universal Law: act only on maxims you could universalize without contradiction (perfect duties: never lie, never murder; imperfect: beneficence; universalizability test: performative vs causal contradiction); (CI-2) Formula of Humanity: treat rational beings always as ends in themselves, never merely as means (means vs mere means distinction); (CI-3) Formula of Kingdom of Ends: act as legislating member of kingdom of ends; perfect vs imperfect duties; categorical vs hypothetical imperatives; autonomy and heteronomy; moral worth requires acting from duty not merely in accord with duty; W.D. Ross's prima facie duties (1930): fidelity, reparation, gratitude, justice, beneficence, non-maleficence, self-improvement; always-prima-facie vs actually binding; cannot systematize — judgment required; Frances Kamm, doctrine of double effect (DDE): foreseeing vs intending harm; trolley vs footbridge problem (Thomson 1985, 1986)); virtue ethics (Aristotle NE: eudaimonia as highest good (function argument: unique human function = rational activity in accordance with virtue); virtue = stable disposition to feel and act appropriately (habituation); Golden Mean: courage between cowardice and rashness; phronesis (practical wisdom) master virtue; unity of virtues; MacIntyre After Virtue (1981): virtues intelligible within practices; practice-internal vs external goods; narrative unity of a life; tradition; critique of Enlightenment moral philosophy; neo-Aristotelian virtue ethics: Foot, Hursthouse, Annas; care ethics (Gilligan 1982): different moral voice — relationships, responsibilities; Nel Noddings; critique of Kohlberg justice ethics; Feminist ethics: intersection of feminist theory and ethics; contractualism (Scanlon 1998): an act is wrong if it would be disallowed by principles no one could reasonably reject; contractualism vs contractarianism; reasons-based morality; relativism about wrongness; comparison with utilitarianism and Kantianism; applied ethics (Trolley problem and variants: Thomson, Judith Jarvis Thomson violinist; abortion, famine and affluence (Singer), animal rights (Peter Singer), environmental ethics, business ethics, bioethics)",
            successCriteria: "Moral philosophy study session completed (metaethics positions listed, Moore's open question argument stated, three formulations of categorical imperative named, Ross's prima facie duties listed, eudaimonia-phronesis connection explained, Scanlon's contractualism summarized, and exam notes saved)",
