        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Study institutional economics for my exam — review Coase theorem, transaction cost economics, Douglass North's NIE, Ostrom's commons governance, and principal-agent theory: Coase theorem and property rights (Coase (1960) 'The Problem of Social Cost': in the absence of transaction costs, parties will negotiate to the efficient outcome regardless of initial property rights assignment; example: factory smoke and laundry — if factory has right to pollute, laundry pays factory to reduce; if laundry has right to clean air, factory pays laundry for right to pollute; either way, efficient level of abatement if TC=0; Coase theorem significance: positive transaction costs are the reason inefficiencies exist; the theorem motivates the analysis of TC rather than prescribing non-intervention; Demsetz (1967) on property rights emergence: property rights arise when the gains from internalizing externalities exceed the costs of establishing and enforcing them; common property vs open access vs private property vs state property; Alchian-Demsetz (1972): team production with shirking problem motivates the firm as a monitoring solution; property rights fully specified, freely transferable, and enforced → efficient resource use); transaction cost economics (Williamson's TCE: transactions have three key dimensions: (1) asset specificity (site-, physical-, human-, dedicated-, brand-capital specificity) — most important; higher specificity → holdup risk → need for governance; (2) uncertainty (behavioral uncertainty from opportunism + environmental uncertainty); (3) frequency; governance structures: market (price mechanism), hybrid (long-term contracts, franchises, joint ventures), hierarchy (firm, vertical integration); fundamental transformation: ex ante competitive bidding → ex post bilateral monopoly when assets are specific; efficient boundaries of the firm determined by minimizing sum of production costs + transaction costs; Williamson's four-level schema: (L1) embeddedness — norms, customs (centuries); (L2) institutional environment — property rights, constitutions, laws (decades); (L3) governance — contracts, firms (years); (L4) resource allocation — prices, quantities (continuous); alignment hypothesis: match governance structure to transaction characteristics; empirical tests: Joskow (1985) coal contracts, Masten (1984) aerospace, Monteverde-Teece (1982) auto components); North's new institutional economics (North (1990) 'Institutions, Institutional Change and Economic Performance': institutions are the 'rules of the game' — humanly devised constraints that structure incentives; formal institutions: constitutions, laws, property rights, regulations; informal institutions: codes of conduct, norms, conventions, self-imposed codes of behavior; organizations are 'players': firms, political parties, agencies; institutional change is typically incremental and path-dependent — informal constraints persist even when formal rules change; 'lock-in': initial institutions constrain future options (QWERTY, track gauge, common law vs civil law); why do inefficient institutions persist? Distributional conflict: rulers benefit from institutional status quo; ideological beliefs; transition costs; North-Thomas (1973): efficient property rights are the key to Western economic growth; North-Weingast (1989): Glorious Revolution 1688 and credible commitment to property rights explained British capital market development; AJR (2001): settler mortality → settlement type → institutions → income today); Ostrom commons governance (Governing the Commons (1990): challenges Hardin's 'tragedy of the commons' (1968) — many CPRs are governed sustainably by user communities without privatization or state control; CPRs: subtractable but non-excludable resources (fisheries, groundwater, pastures, forests); design principles for sustainable CPR governance: (DP1) clearly defined boundaries: who can use the resource; (DP2) congruence: rules fit local conditions; (DP3) collective choice: users participate in rule modification; (DP4) monitoring: accountable to users; (DP5) graduated sanctions: low to high violations; (DP6) conflict resolution: low-cost arenas; (DP7) recognition: external authority respects local rights; (DP8) nested enterprises: multiple overlapping governance levels; polycentric governance: multiple overlapping institutions; IAD framework (Institutional Analysis and Development): action arena + actors + rules-in-use → outcomes; SES framework: social-ecological systems analysis; Nobel Prize 2009 (with Williamson)); principal-agent theory (hidden action (moral hazard): agent's effort e unobservable; principal maximizes expected profits net of wage w; optimal contract trades off incentive provision (fixed share of output: w=α+βy where β=risk-incentive tradeoff) against risk imposition on risk-averse agent; optimal β*=1/(1+rσ²/effort-sensitivity) where r=risk aversion, σ²=output variance; full insurance: β=0 but no incentive; first-best: β=1 if agent risk-neutral; hidden information (adverse selection): agent knows type θ (high/low cost); screening: separate contracts that make each type self-select; revelatory principle; Spence signaling: education as signal of ability (separating equilibrium requires signal cost decreasing in ability); hold-up problem: relation-specific investment → ex post renegotiation extracts quasi-rents → underinvestment; solution: vertical integration (Grossman-Hart 1986 property rights theory: ownership of physical assets determines residual control rights; integrate when integration better protects the party making the more important investment); relational contracts: self-enforcing agreements sustained by repeated interaction (Folk theorem application))",
            successCriteria: "Institutional economics study session completed (Coase theorem zero-TC logic stated, Williamson's three transaction dimensions named, North's formal vs informal institutions distinguished, Ostrom's eight design principles listed, principal-agent hidden action vs hidden information distinction explained, and exam notes saved)",
