            task: "Write my institutional economics paper — this may include: a transaction cost and firm boundary analysis (select one: evaluate the make-or-buy decision using Williamson's TCE framework — apply the alignment hypothesis to a specific industry (automobile vertical integration, pharmaceutical R&D alliances, airline maintenance outsourcing, or software development): identify the asset specificity type and level, behavioral and environmental uncertainty, and transaction frequency; predict the efficient governance structure (market, hybrid, hierarchy); evaluate the empirical evidence (Monteverde-Teece 1982 auto components, Masten 1984 aerospace); what are the efficiency vs market power explanations for vertical integration?; evaluate the Grossman-Hart-Moore property rights theory — apply to a specific integration decision: who should own the asset when investments by both parties are important? Derive the underinvestment prediction when the non-owner cannot capture all returns from investment; evaluate the theory's predictions against Williamson's TCE: when do they give different answers? Evaluate the empirical challenges (Acemoglu et al. 2010 on integration and technology choice); evaluate relational contracts and self-enforcement — apply Levin (2003) relational contracting theory: what conditions make a relational contract self-enforcing (discount factor δ ≥ cost of deviation / surplus)? Apply to employment relationships, supply chain partnerships, or franchise contracts; evaluate Baker-Gibbons-Murphy (2002) on formal vs relational contracts: when does a formal contract crowd out a relational contract?), an institutional change and development analysis (select one: evaluate North's theory of institutional change — apply to a specific historical case (the Glorious Revolution and British property rights (North-Weingast 1989), US land policy and western expansion, colonial institutional legacies in Africa or Latin America, or post-Soviet institutional transition): what were the initial institutions, who benefited from the status quo, what triggered the change, and why was change incremental rather than radical? Evaluate the role of informal constraints in resisting or facilitating formal institutional change; evaluate the AJR (Acemoglu-Johnson-Robinson 2001) colonial origins hypothesis — apply the settler mortality instrument: what is the first-stage relationship between mortality and settler colonialism type? What does the exclusion restriction require? Evaluate the robustness challenges (Albouy 2012 criticism); compare AJR's 'reversal of fortune' with North's incremental change thesis: are inclusive institutions sufficient for growth, or are complementary factors needed?; evaluate Ostrom's IAD framework applied to a specific CPR — apply the Institutional Analysis and Development framework to a fishery, irrigation system, or forest commons: identify the action arena, actors, rules-in-use, and biophysical conditions; evaluate which of Ostrom's design principles are present and absent; predict sustainability outcome; what changes in external conditions (climate, market integration, population growth) would destabilize the governance arrangement?), or a principal-agent and contract design analysis (select one: evaluate adverse selection and signaling in a specific market — apply Akerlof's (1970) lemons model to a market (used cars, health insurance, credit markets, labor markets): derive the unraveling equilibrium; identify the information asymmetry and adverse selection mechanism; evaluate the signaling (Spence 1973) and screening (Rothschild-Stiglitz 1976) responses; what regulation or institutional mechanism resolves the market failure? Evaluate the empirical evidence; evaluate incentive contract design under moral hazard — apply Holmström-Milgrom (1987) linear contract model to a specific employment or procurement setting: what are the optimal power-of-incentives predictions? How does multi-tasking (Holmström-Milgrom 1991) change the prediction: when does tying pay to measurable output reduce unmeasured effort? Apply to teacher pay-for-performance, executive compensation, or sales force incentives; evaluate the hold-up problem and relationship-specific investment — apply Klein-Crawford-Alchian (1978) and Hart-Moore (1988) to a specific vertical relationship (supplier-assembler, hospital-physician, platform-app developer): identify the specific asset, predict the underinvestment, and evaluate the solution (vertical integration, long-term contract, hostage, asset ownership allocation); what empirical evidence supports or challenges the hold-up explanation for observed integration?)",
