fects (unlike in Solow); Lucas (1988): human capital accumulation; externalities from average human capital; debate: Arrow (1962) learning by doing; Aghion-Howitt (1992) Schumpeterian creative destruction; scale effects controversy: Jones (1995) critique — semi-endogenous growth without scale effects; Acemoglu-Restrepo (2018) tasks and automation); institutions and long-run growth (AJR (2001) 'The Colonial Origins of Comparative Development': European settler mortality predicted settlement type (extractive vs settler colony) → institutional quality today (property rights, rule of law, constraints on executive) → per capita income today; IV: mortality rates of soldiers, bishops, sailors in early colonial period; first stage F>10; exclusion: mortality affected income only through institutions; 'reversal of fortune': relatively prosperous pre-colonial regions now poorer — institutions, not geography or culture, drove reversal; North-Weingast (1989): Glorious Revolution 1688 → secure property rights and credible commitment → financial development → Industrial Revolution; Knack-Keefer (1995), Hall-Jones (1999) on social infrastructure; Banerjee-Iyer (2005) Indian colonial land tenure; 'Why Nations Fail' (Acemoglu-Robinson 2012): inclusive (secure property rights, open access, pluralism) vs extractive (concentrated power, appropriation) institutions; critical junctures and path dependence; virtuous vs vicious cycles; Botswana as positive exception); poverty traps and program evaluation (poverty trap: multiple equilibria arising from S-shaped savings function, nutritional efficiency wages, or credit market failures; Sachs 'big push' (2005): coordinated investment across sectors to escape trap; Easterly (2006) critique: top-down aid fails, local knowledge and spontaneous order needed; RCT methodology (Banerjee-Duflo, Nobel 2019): randomly assign treatment (deworming, CCT, microcredit, agricultural extension); internal validity vs external validity; LATE (local average treatment effect); CCT programs: Progresa/Oportunidades (Mexico, 1997): randomized evaluation (Schultz 2004) — enrollment +3.4-9.3pp, health visits up 35%, consumption up 15%; Bolsa Família (Brazil): 14 million families, ~0.5% GDP; evidence on inequality reduction; Uganda SAGE pilot; Malawi SCTP; microfinance: Grameen Bank group lending; ATE from RCTs (Banerjee et al. 2015 six-country study): moderate consumption gains, no transformative poverty reduction; structural transformation (Lewis 1954 dual-sector model: unlimited labor supply in subsistence agriculture at constant wage; industrial sector absorbs labor; wage rises only when surplus labor exhausted (Lewis turning point); Kuznets (1955) inverted-U hypothesis: inequality rises then falls with income; Chenery structural change patterns; East Asian tigers: export-led manufacturing; Flying Geese model (Akamatsu): technology catch-up sequencing; structural change and agricultural productivity: Murphy-Shleifer-Vishny (1989) big push with demand complementarities; human development index (HDI): health (life expectancy) + education (mean and expected years) + income (GNI per capita) geometric mean; UNDP Human Development Report; HDI vs GDP per capita debate; capability approach (Sen); multidimensional poverty index (MPI))",
