he exclusion restriction? Evaluate Albouy's (2012) data critique: does correcting mortality data undermine the first stage or the exclusion restriction? Evaluate Nunn's (2008) slave trade IV as an alternative instrument for institutional quality in Africa; compare AJR's institutions-first view with Sachs's geography hypothesis (malaria ecology, tropical soils) and Glaeser et al.'s (2004) human capital alternative; evaluate institutional complementarities — apply North's concept of path dependence to explain the persistence of bad institutions in a specific regional context (Latin American extractive institutions, post-Soviet nomenklatura privatization, or sub-Saharan Africa's colonial land tenure systems): what informal constraints reinforce formal institutional failures? What triggered or failed to trigger institutional reform?; evaluate 'Why Nations Fail' vs 'The Narrow Corridor' (Acemoglu-Robinson 2019) as explanatory frameworks — what is the Shackled Leviathan concept? Apply to a specific contemporary case (Venezuela, South Korea vs North Korea, or Botswana's institutional success)), or a poverty, programs, and development finance analysis (select one: evaluate the RCT methodology in development economics — apply to a specific program evaluation (Kremer-Miguel deworming (Kenya 1998-2001), Duflo-Saez retirement savings (US), Karlan-Zinman microcredit (Philippines), or Dupas-Robinson savings (Kenya)): describe the randomization design (unit of randomization, stratification, compliance issues, LATE vs ITT); evaluate the external validity concern (Deaton-Cartwright critique vs Banerjee-Duflo defense); what causal mechanism does the RCT identify, and does it illuminate the policy-relevant question?; evaluate conditional cash transfer programs — apply Progresa's evaluation (Schultz 2004, Behrman et al. 2005): compare the randomized control group with treatment group on enrollment, health visits, and consumption outcomes; evaluate the co-responsibility conditions: are they binding constraints or deadweight administrative burden? Apply the CCT model to a low-income country context: what complementary supply-side investments (schools, clinics) are needed for CCTs to work?; evaluate microfinance's impact on poverty — apply the six-country RCT evidence (Banerjee et al. 2015): what are the average effects on consumption, business investment, and female empowerment? Why do the results differ from the Grameen Bank's claims? Evaluate the Roodman-Morduch (2014) reanalysis of Grameen data; apply the Cull-Demirgüç-Kunt-Morduch (2009) framework on the trade-off between financial sustainability and outreach to the poorest)",
