nd Medicaid (Affordable Care Act (2010): Title I — insurance market reforms: guaranteed issue (no denial for pre-existing conditions), community rating (same premium regardless of health status, ±3:1 age band), essential health benefits (ten categories: ambulatory, emergency, hospitalization, maternity, mental health/SUD, prescription drugs, rehabilitative, lab, preventive, pediatric), no lifetime/annual limits, coverage of dependents to age 26; Title II — Medicaid expansion to 138% FPL (NFIB v. Sebelius 2012 made optional; 41 states + DC as of 2024); Title III — improving quality and efficiency (ACOs, bundled payments, CMMI); Title IV — prevention; individual mandate (§1501): eliminated tax penalty via TCJA 2017; risk corridors, reinsurance, risk adjustment to stabilize markets; ACA coverage impact: uninsured rate 16.3% (2010) → 7.9% (2023); net impact ~20 million gained coverage; Medicare (1965): Part A (hospital insurance, funded by payroll tax), Part B (medical insurance, premium + general revenue), Part C (Medicare Advantage — private plans receive capitated payment), Part D (prescription drugs, 2003 MMA); DRG (diagnosis-related group) payment for inpatient since 1983 (prospective payment system); TEFRA, BBA, ACA reforms; Medicare spending ~3.7% GDP; MedPAC advisory; Medicare solvency HI Trust Fund; Medicaid (1965): federal-state partnership; federal matching (FMAP): 50-83% federal share based on state per capita income; eligibility historically categorical (AFDC-linked); ACA expansion changed to income-based (138% FPL); managed care: ~70% Medicaid enrollees in MCOs; CHIP (1997) for children; Medicaid covered 90 million in 2024; DSH (Disproportionate Share Hospital) payments); QALY, ICER, and priority setting (QALY (Quality-Adjusted Life Year): combines life quantity and quality; utility weight elicited by time trade-off (TTO), standard gamble (SG), or EQ-5D instrument; EQ-5D dimensions: mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain/discomfort, anxiety/depression