ent? Evaluate the accessibility design argument: do difficulty options (Celeste assist mode) make games more inclusive without undermining authorial intent?; evaluate reinforcement schedules and ethical game design — apply Skinner's variable ratio schedule to a specific F2P mobile game or live-service game with loot boxes (FIFA Ultimate Team, Genshin Impact): how do variable reward mechanics create compulsion loops? Evaluate regulatory responses (Belgium/Netherlands loot box prohibition, UK GRC position); argue for a design approach that maintains engagement while avoiding exploitative mechanics; evaluate narrative design and player agency — apply Aarseth's ergodic literature theory and Chris Crawford's interactivity spectrum to a specific narrative game (Disco Elysium, Undertale, 80 Days, or Deus Ex): how does player choice affect narrative coherence? Evaluate the writing challenge of branching vs authored linearity; what does ludonarrative dissonance (Hocking) reveal about the limits of interactivity in narrative games?), or a game monetization and industry analysis (select one: evaluate free-to-play monetization and game design — apply the whaling model (1% of players → 50% of revenue) and ARPU/ARPPU analysis to a specific F2P game: how does the monetization model shape the game's core loop, social mechanics, and difficulty design? Evaluate ethical design principles: what distinguishes cosmetic monetization (Fortnite) from pay-to-win (mobile strategy games)? What are the game design arguments for and against subscription models (PS+/Game Pass) replacing F2P?; evaluate game accessibility as a design value — apply the Game Accessibility Guidelines (GAG) and the Ablegamers accessibility principles to a specific game or genre: what accessibility features (colorblind modes, remappable controls, difficulty options, subtitles, motor accessibility) are missing or exemplary? Evaluate the argument that accessibility features improve games for all players, not just disabled