tique; delay discounting and addiction; temporal self-regulation theory); risk and ambiguity in the brain (risk: known probabilities; ambiguity: unknown probabilities (Ellsberg paradox 1961); Becker-DeGroot-Marschak lottery; neural correlates: insula → loss aversion/risk signals; amygdala → ambiguity aversion; OFC → risk encoding; ventral striatum → expected value under risk; Platt-Glimcher (1999): LIP neurons encode expected reward and probability; framing effects neural basis: Kahneman-Tversky prospect theory with neural correlates (loss > gain asymmetry in insula/amygdala)); social preferences and strategic behavior (ultimatum game: rejection of unfair offers activates right DLPFC (Sanfey et al. 2003) and insula; DLPFC TMS → more acceptance of unfair offers (Knoch 2006); trust game: oxytocin increases trust (Kosfeld 2005); inequity aversion (Fehr-Schmidt model) neural correlates: ACC for unfairness; TPJ (temporoparietal junction): mentalizing and theory of mind in strategic games; TPJ-TMS → reduced cheating detection; strategic interaction: BOLD signal in dlPFC tracks opponent strategy estimation); neuromarketing applications (EEG alpha power suppression: cognitive load; gamma: attention; P300 event-related potential: attention capture; ERP N400: semantic incongruity in advertising; eye-tracking: saccade patterns, fixation duration, AOI analysis; pupillometry: pupil dilation → cognitive load/arousal; skin conductance response (SCR): emotional arousal; IAT (implicit association test): implicit brand attitudes; fMRI product preference: Plassmann-Deppe Pepsi paradox; neuromarketing ethics)",
