ssions (1/25th–1/5th second); Paul Ekman Police Video 'wizards' with >80% accuracy (0.4% of sample); deception cues: gaze aversion (unreliable), response latency, speech hesitations, verbal-nonverbal inconsistency; Reid Technique (Inbau-Reid-Buckley-Jayne): Behavior Analysis Interview (BAI) + nine steps of interrogation: maximization (scare) + minimization (minimize moral seriousness); Reid Technique critique: Kassin-Gudjonsson 2004 — false confessions, coercive interrogation; Gudjonsson suggestibility scales; PEACE model (UK Home Office 1992): Planning-Engage/Explain-Account-Closure-Evaluate — non-accusatorial, cognitive interview, open questions; Vrij CBCA (criteria-based content analysis) and RM (reality monitoring)); criminal career typologies (Lombroso (1876) born criminal: atavistic features; Sheldon somatotype theory; differential association (Sutherland 1947): learning crime through intimate groups; social bond theory (Hirschi 1969): attachment, commitment, involvement, belief; strain theory (Merton 1938, Agnew 1992 general strain); routine activity; Gottfredson-Hirschi (1990) general theory: low self-control = stable propensity; Moffitt (1993) dual taxonomy: life-course persistent (LCP, early-onset, persistent, neurological) vs adolescence-limited (AL, peer influence, temporary, desistance); Farrington (1986, 2003) Cambridge longitudinal study: onset, duration, frequency, seriousness, desistance; Laub-Sampson desistance: marriage, employment as turning points)",
