            task: "Study public international law for my exam — review Article 38 ICJ sources, customary international law, jus cogens, the VCLT, state responsibility, diplomatic immunity, and jurisdictional principles: sources of international law (Article 38(1) ICJ Statute: (a) international conventions (treaties): bilateral/multilateral; treaty definition (VCLT Article 2: written agreement between states governed by international law); (b) international custom: two elements — state practice (widespread, consistent, general) + opinio juris (sense of legal obligation; Nicaragua v US ICJ 1986: opinio juris established separately from practice; North Sea Continental Shelf 1969: short duration + extensive participation for rapid custom; persistent objector: state that consistently objects during custom formation is not bound (Fisheries Case, UK v Norway 1951)); (c) general principles of law recognized by civilized nations: procedural principles (res judicata, good faith), substantive principles (no one profits from own wrong, unjust enrichment); (d) judicial decisions and teachings: subsidiary means — ICJ decisions not binding precedent (Article 59 ICJ Statute), but de facto authority; jus cogens peremptory norms (Article 53 VCLT): non-derogable — genocide, slavery, torture, aggression, self-determination; void treaties; erga omnes obligations (Barcelona Traction Case, ICJ 1970): owed to international community as a whole — genocide, slavery, fundamental human rights; any state may invoke (not only injured state)); treaty law and VCLT (Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties 1969: Article 26 pacta sunt servanda — every treaty in force binding; Article 27: no domestic law justification for non-performance; treaty interpretation (Articles 31-32): Article 31 general rule — ordinary meaning + context (treaty text, preamble, annexes, subsequent agreements) + object and purpose (teleological); Article 32 supplementary means — travaux préparatoires (preparatory works) when ambiguous or manifestly absurd; dynamic (evolutive) interpretation vs original intent; reservations (Articles 19-23): must not be incompatible with object and purpose (Genocide Convention Advisory Opinion 1951); Article 60: material breach (violation of essential provision) → suspension/termination; Article 62: fundamental change of circumstances (rebus sic stantibus) — strict conditions; Article 64: jus cogens supervenes → treaty void); state responsibility (ILC Articles on State Responsibility 2001 (ARSIWA): internationally wrongful act = attribution + breach of international obligation; attribution rules: Article 4 (state organs including de jure + de facto); Article 7 (ultra vires acts of organs still attributable — excess of authority); Article 8 (instruction, direction, or control: effective control test Nicaragua; overall control test Tadic/ICTY); Article 11 (state adoption of private conduct); circumstances precluding wrongfulness (CPW) Articles 20-27: consent (Article 20), self-defense (Article 21 + Charter 51), countermeasures (Articles 22 + 49-54): must be directed at responsible state, temporary, proportionate; necessity (Article 25): sole means, essential interest, not contributed to; force majeure (Article 23): physically impossible; distress (Article 24): saving life; consequences: cessation and non-repetition (Article 30), full reparation (Article 31): restitution → compensation → satisfaction); diplomatic and consular immunity (Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations (VCDR) 1961: diplomatic premises inviolable (Article 22); agent's person inviolable (Article 29); diplomatic bag inviolable (Article 27); diplomatic immunity ratione personae: absolute during posting for diplomatic agents; ratione materiae: functional acts survive posting end; family members immunity; severance of relations/recall; VCCR 1963: consular immunity narrower — consular officers: immunity for official acts only; LaGrand Case (ICJ 2001) and Avena Case (ICJ 2004): Article 36 consular notification obligations); jurisdictional principles (territoriality principle: subjective (domestic offense commences inside) vs objective (effect felt inside — Lotus PCIJ 1927); nationality principle: active (offender's nationality) vs passive (victim's nationality — US rejects); protective principle: affects state's vital interests (currency counterfeiting); universality principle: piracy (historic), war crimes, genocide, crimes against humanity (jus cogens basis); Lotus principle: no prohibition in international law = permissible (consent-based order critique); UN Charter Article 2(4) use of force prohibition + Article 51 self-defense exception; collective security (Chapter VII); R2P — responsibility to protect)",
            successCriteria: "Public international law study session completed (Article 38 four sources named, customary IL two elements stated, jus cogens definition given, VCLT Article 31 interpretation rule explained, ILC ARSIWA attribution rules described, VCDR diplomatic immunity scope stated, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
