            task: "Write my public international law paper — this may include: a sources and custom analysis (select one: evaluate the formation and identification of customary international law — apply the Nicaragua v United States (ICJ 1986) framework (separate proof of state practice and opinio juris) and North Sea Continental Shelf (ICJ 1969) (extensive participation + short time period) to assess whether a specific norm has crystallized into CIL: evaluate the persistent objector doctrine (Fisheries Case 1951): can a state opt out of forming custom? Evaluate the Lotus principle critique (consent-based vs interest-based international order); what does the ILC (2018 Draft Conclusions) add to the identification methodology?; evaluate jus cogens and erga omnes in international law — apply the VCLT Article 53 jus cogens framework and the Barcelona Traction Case (ICJ 1970) erga omnes concept to a specific norm candidate (prohibition on torture, right to self-determination, prohibition on apartheid, climate obligations): what criteria must a norm satisfy to be jus cogens? Evaluate the ILC Draft Conclusions on Peremptory Norms (2022); what are the procedural and substantive consequences of jus cogens status? What standing does erga omnes create for third states?; evaluate treaty interpretation under the VCLT — apply Articles 31-32 to a disputed treaty clause (WTO DSB interpretation of GATT Article XX exceptions; ICJ interpretation of UN Charter Article 51; ECtHR evolutive interpretation of the ECHR): what is the hierarchy of interpretive means? When do travaux préparatoires legitimately supplement? Evaluate the dynamic vs original intent debate; does the VCLT permit teleological reasoning that departs from ordinary meaning?), a state responsibility and diplomatic law analysis (select one: evaluate the ILC ARSIWA attribution rules — apply Articles 4-11 to a specific fact pattern (state-sponsored cyber attack; use of private military contractors; rebel group conduct adopted by government): which attribution test applies (Nicaragua effective control; Tadic overall control)? Evaluate the ICJ-ICTY split (Bosnia Genocide Case vs Tadic) on attribution standard for state responsibility vs individual criminal responsibility; what are the policy consequences of a stricter vs looser attribution standard?; evaluate the circumstances precluding wrongfulness — apply the ARSIWA countermeasures regime (Articles 49-54) to a specific interstate dispute: what conditions must countermeasures meet (proportionality, temporarily, obligation cessation, injured state only)? Evaluate the Gabčíkovo-Nagymaros (ICJ 1997) necessity defense: what does 'sole means' and 'essential interest' require? What is the relationship between necessity (Article 25) and self-defense (Article 21)?; evaluate diplomatic immunity and consular notification — apply the VCDR and the LaGrand (ICJ 2001) and Avena (ICJ 2004) cases to assess the scope of Article 36 consular notification obligations: when does diplomatic immunity attach? Does it extend to family members? When may a receiving state expel (persona non grata) a diplomat? Evaluate the tension between diplomatic immunity and accountability for serious crimes; evaluate the UK's position on diplomatic premises inviolability in Wikileaks/Assange (Ecuador embassy) and what Art. 22 VCDR permits a sending state to do), or a jurisdictional and use of force analysis (select one: evaluate the Lotus principle and contemporary jurisdictional limits — apply the Lotus PCIJ 1927 decision to assess the default rule of international law (permissible unless prohibited): how has the Lotus principle been modified by subsequent treaty practice (UNCLOS, ICAO Chicago Convention) and jus cogens? Evaluate the passive personality principle (US practice) and universal jurisdiction (Belgium/Spain practice for war crimes): what are the conditions for universal jurisdiction to comply with sovereign equality?; evaluate the UN Charter Article 2(4) use of force prohibition and exceptions — apply Article 51 self-defense (necessity + proportionality: Caroline test) and Chapter VII collective security authorization to a specific contemporary conflict: what does 'armed attack' require? Evaluate anticipatory self-defense (Webster doctrine) and pre-emptive self-defense: what is the legal status of unilateral pre-emption? Evaluate humanitarian intervention/R2P and its consistency with Article 2(4); evaluate state responsibility for internationally wrongful acts including reparation — apply the Chorzow Factory (PCIJ 1928) principle (full reparation) and the three forms of reparation (restitution, compensation, satisfaction) to a specific interstate dispute: what is the standard for compensation? Evaluate moral damages (satisfaction); what procedural requirements apply before invoking countermeasures vs invocatio ICJ jurisdiction?)",
