        ),
        // islamicfinance
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Study Islamic finance for my exam — review the prohibition of riba, core Islamic finance instruments, takaful insurance, AAOIFI/IFSB regulatory frameworks, and Islamic banking: prohibition of riba and core principles (riba prohibition: riba al-fadl (excess in same-commodity spot exchange — 6 ribawi items: gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, salt — must be exchanged equally and hand-to-hand); riba al-nasi'ah (interest on deferred payment — the riba of excess and delay); Quran 2:275-279 and 3:130 foundational texts; ribawi items and their modern extension to currencies; gharar prohibition: excessive uncertainty or deception in contracts — gharar fahish (major) voids contract; examples: futures with excessive uncertainty, insurance (without takaful structure), short selling debates; maysir prohibition: gambling and speculation; classical school rulings vs contemporary debates (derivatives, FX forwards for hedging); qard hasan: benevolent (interest-free) loan — pure charitable lending; bay' al-dayn: sale of debt — prohibited by most schools); core Islamic finance instruments (murabaha (cost-plus sale): bank purchases asset → resells to client at disclosed cost + profit margin; deferred payment (murabaha to purchase orderer / commodity murabaha); tawarruq (monetization): client buys commodity → sells to third party for cash — controversial; AAOIFI Standard 8; musharaka (equity partnership): profit sharing per agreement; loss sharing per capital contribution; diminishing musharaka (musharaka mutanaqisa): home finance structure — client buys out bank's share gradually; mudaraba (profit-sharing trust): rab al-mal (silent partner — capital, bears loss) + mudarib (working partner — effort, loses only labor); two-tier mudaraba in Islamic banking: depositors-bank mudaraba + bank-entrepreneur mudaraba; unrestricted vs restricted mudaraba; ijara (leasing): operational lease (ijara) vs finance lease (ijara muntahia bittamleek — ownership transfer option at end); ijara wa iqtina (lease to own); ijara mawsoofa fi al-dhimma (forward ijara); istisna' (construction contract): manufacturer builds to specification, deferred delivery; salam (forward sale): advance payment, deferred delivery — allowed exception to gharar prohibition for agricultural goods; sukuk (Islamic bonds/certificates): sukuk al-ijara (most common — lease-backed), sukuk al-musharaka, sukuk al-murabaha; asset-backed (transfer of true ownership) vs asset-based (no true sale, contractual rights only) — controversy; sukuk default experience (Investment Dar, Dana Gas); takaful (Islamic mutual insurance): conventional insurance has gharar + maysir; takaful structure: tabarru' (donation) concept — participants donate to pool; wakala model (agent manages pool for fee); mudaraba model (operator shares in surplus); re-takaful; family takaful (life equivalent) vs general takaful (P&C equivalent); AAOIFI Standard 26); regulatory frameworks (AAOIFI (Accounting and Auditing Organization for Islamic Financial Institutions): Bahrain; 57 Shariah Standards; FAS (financial accounting standards); governance standards; IFSB (Islamic Financial Services Board): Kuala Lumpur; prudential standards — IFSB-2 (capital adequacy for institutions offering IIFS), IFSB-12 (liquidity risk), IFSB-15 (revised capital adequacy); OIC Fiqh Academy (Jeddah): fatwa-issuing body; resolutions on tawarruq, sukuk standards; sharia supervisory board (SSB): 3+ qualified scholars; ex-ante approval + ex-post audit; potential conflicts of interest (scholar sits on multiple SSBs); Central Shariah Committee (Bank Negara Malaysia): apex body; dual banking system (Malaysia): conventional + Islamic banks side-by-side; GCC Islamic banking: Gulf international banks; London as Western sukuk hub; Islamic Development Bank (IsDB); ISDA/IIFM Islamic hedging docs; London Metal Exchange (LME) tawarruq transactions)",
            successCriteria: "Islamic finance study session completed (riba al-fadl vs riba al-nasi'ah distinction made, murabaha mechanics described, sukuk asset-backed vs asset-based distinction explained, takaful tabarru' structure stated, AAOIFI vs IFSB roles distinguished, and exam notes saved)",
            preferredDuration: 60 * 60
        ),
        SuggestedTemplate(
            icon: "building.columns.fill",
            task: "Write my Islamic finance paper — this may include: an Islamic finance instruments analysis (select one: evaluate murabaha as an interest substitute in Islamic banking — apply AAOIFI Standard 8 and the tawarruq controversy to assess whether murabaha cost-plus sale and commodity murabaha (tawarruq) genuinely avoid riba or merely replicate its economics in a different form; evaluate the El-Gamal critique of 'Islamic finance as legal arbitrage'; evaluate the AAOIFI and OIC Fiqh Academy fatwa on organized tawarruq: under what conditions is tawarruq mafrud (prohibited)? What alternative structures (diminishing musharaka, equity partnerships) are proposed as more authentically Islamic?; evaluate sukuk structures and the authenticity debate — apply the AAOIFI Shariah Standard 17 (2008) and the Sheikh Taqi Usmani critique that most sukuk are not sharia-compliant (asset-based rather than asset-backed): what is the economic difference between asset-backed sukuk (true sale of underlying asset) and asset-based sukuk (contractual obligations only)? Evaluate the Dana Gas sukuk dispute (2017): how did the UAE court ruling on sukuk mudaraba expose the legal risk in sukuk structures? Evaluate the role of IFSB-7 capital treatment for sukuk in bank regulatory capital; evaluate the takaful model debate — apply the wakala vs mudaraba models for takaful fund management: what are the differences in operator incentives and participant protections? Evaluate the AAOIFI Standard 26 takaful requirements; how does re-takaful function? What are the surplus distribution debates (participants vs operator)?), a regulatory and governance analysis (select one: evaluate sharia supervisory board governance problems — apply the principal-agent framework (Eisenhardt 1989) to the relationship between Islamic bank management and SSB scholars: what conflicts of interest arise when scholars sit on multiple SSBs? Evaluate the SSB compensation problem: do fee arrangements create incentives for sharia arbitrage (fatwa shopping)? Evaluate the IFSB-10 governance guidelines and the OIC Fiqh Academy composition requirements; compare the Malaysian centralized SSB model (BNM Central Shariah Committee) with the decentralized Gulf model; evaluate the AAOIFI and IFSB regulatory coordination — apply the Basel III framework (pillar 1 IFSB-2 capital adequacy, pillar 2 IFSB-7 supervisory review, pillar 3 market discipline) to Islamic banks: how does risk sharing (mudaraba/musharaka) change capital treatment compared to conventional lending? Evaluate the PSIA (profit-sharing investment accounts) as displaced commercial risk: why do IFSB standards treat PSIA differently from conventional deposits? Evaluate the Islamic LOLR (lender of last resort) problem: can a central bank provide emergency liquidity to Islamic banks without interest?; evaluate Islamic finance in Muslim-minority jurisdictions — apply the UK 'level playing field' approach (Finance Acts 2003, 2005, 2007, 2009) to assess how stamp duty land tax relief, alternative finance arrangement rules, and sukuk capital gains exemptions have made Islamic mortgages and sukuk commercially viable in the UK: evaluate the Malaysia MIFC (Malaysia International Islamic Financial Centre) strategy; what institutional infrastructure (UK tax neutrality, arbitration under English law, Sharia court for personal law) is needed to support Islamic finance in a secular jurisdiction?), or an Islamic finance applications analysis (select one: evaluate Islamic microfinance as a development finance tool — apply Grameen Bank and BRAC models to assess whether Islamic microfinance (qard hasan, murabaha, musharaka for SMEs) can be scaled: what are the product design challenges (group liability contracts, collateral-free lending, musharaka monitoring)? Evaluate the IsDB and Islamic Relief programs; what evidence exists on repayment rates and poverty impact for Islamic microfinance vs conventional microfinance?; evaluate the green sukuk and ESG Islamic finance alignment — apply the Climate Bonds Initiative (CBI) green bond framework and AAOIFI standards to assess whether green sukuk structures align Islamic finance principles with climate finance goals: what is the underlying asset requirement for a green sukuk? Evaluate Malaysia's Sustainable and Responsible Investment (SRI) sukuk framework; what are the sharia concerns with carbon trading and ESG screening (exclusion of tobacco, alcohol, weapons vs sharia prohibition list overlap)?; evaluate Islamic insurance (takaful) against conventional insurance — apply the takaful structure (tabarru' pool, wakala/mudaraba operator) to the risk pooling economics: does takaful provide genuine risk sharing or does it replicate insurance in Islamic dress? Evaluate the actuarial challenges (adverse selection, moral hazard in takaful pools without experience rating); compare the GCC takaful market penetration with Malaysia; what regulatory frameworks (IFSB-8, AAOIFI-26) govern takaful solvency and capital adequacy?)",
            successCriteria: "Islamic finance paper completed (murabaha authenticity, sukuk structure debate, takaful model, SSB governance, AAOIFI/IFSB regulation, or Islamic finance in secular jurisdictions argument developed; evidence applied; and document saved in required format)",
